WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.890
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:05.670
for everyday life.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.900
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.060
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.740
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.020
Today, my guest is
Dr. Layne Norton.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.120
Dr. Norton is one of
the foremost experts

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.210
in protein metabolism,
fat loss, and nutrition.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.850
He did his degrees in
biochemistry and nutritional

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.820
sciences and is considered
one of the world experts

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.970
in understanding how we
extract energy from our food

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.380
and how exercise and what we
eat combine to impact things

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.210
like body composition
and overall health.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.420
Today, we discuss an
enormous number of topics

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.730
under the umbrella of
nutrition and fitness,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.850
including, for instance,
what is energy balance?

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.430
That is, how do we actually
extract energy from our food?

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.820
We also discuss the
somewhat controversial topic

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.620
of artificial sweeteners,
whether or not

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.245
they are safe or not
and whether or not

00:00:55.245 --> 00:00:57.190
they are an effective
tool for weight loss,

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.720
in particular, for
people suffering

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:01.540
from obesity and different
types of diabetes.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.840
We also talk about gut health--
that is the gut microbiome--

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.820
and how it's impacted
by food and how

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.490
it can actually impact the
metabolism of the foods

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:10.400
that we eat.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.480
We also discuss fasting, or
so-called intermittent fasting

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.790
or time-restricted
feeding, what it does

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.210
and what it does not do in
terms of how effective it

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.540
is for weight loss and, perhaps,
even for health and longevity.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.330
We also talk about protein
and define very clearly

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.490
how much protein each
and all of us need,

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.430
depending on our daily
activities and life demands.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.145
We discuss the
various types of diets

00:01:32.145 --> 00:01:33.520
that you've probably
heard about,

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.660
including ketogenic diets,
vegan diets, vegetarian diets,

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.390
and pure carnivore diets, as
well as more typical omnivore

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:42.190
diets, and how to
make sure that you

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:44.410
get all of the
essential amino acids

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.230
that are critical for healthy
weight maintenance, weight

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.540
loss, or directed muscle gain.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.450
We also talk about supplements,
in particular, the supplements

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.330
for which there is an immense
amount of science pointing

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.600
to their safety and
efficacy for fitness

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.515
and for overall
body composition.

00:02:00.515 --> 00:02:02.140
What I'm sure will
become clear to you,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:03.515
as you hear Layne
talk about each

00:02:03.515 --> 00:02:05.320
and every one of
these topics, is

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.110
that he has an incredible
ability to both understand

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.810
the mechanistic science
but also the real world

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.870
applications of the various
discoveries that are made

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.630
in particular papers
and, in particular,

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.370
in the randomized
controlled trials.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:23.110
That is when a given scientific
hypothesis has been raised.

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.200
He's extremely good at
understanding why it was raised

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.540
but also at evaluating
whether or not

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.160
it works in the
real world, which

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.475
is what I believe most everybody
out there is concerned with.

00:02:33.475 --> 00:02:35.350
I think this is one of
the things that really

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.360
distinguishes him
from the other voices

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.100
in the nutritional landscape.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.500
I assure you that by the
end of today's discussion,

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.350
you will have a much clearer
understanding about what

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:48.010
the science says about
nutrition, about fitness,

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:51.820
and about how different diets
and fitness programs combine

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.800
to achieve the
results that you want.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.602
Before we begin, I'd
like to emphasize

00:02:55.602 --> 00:02:58.060
