00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:33.260
I think most people
have heard of a calorie.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.840
I am assuming that most
people don't actually

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:39.480
know what that is in
terms of how it works,

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:40.679
what it represents.

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:42.890
And so maybe you
could just explain

00:08:42.890 --> 00:08:47.390
for people what happens when
we eat food, of any kind,

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.100
and how is that
actually converted

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:53.180
into energy, as a way of framing
up the discussion around weight

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:56.955
loss, weight maintenance, weight
gain, and body composition.

00:08:56.955 --> 00:08:58.580
LAYNE NORTON: So it's
a great question.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:01.100
And like you said, this is one
of those things where people

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:02.750
use the term calories
in, calories out,

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:05.560
and they say, well,
that's way too simplistic.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:08.900
I'm like, if you look at what
actually makes up calories in,

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.990
calories out, it's
actually very complicated.

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:16.550
So let's deal with what
you mentioned first.

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:17.600
What is the calorie?

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.050
Because I think a lot of people
don't quite understand this.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:25.460
So a calorie just refers to
a unit of energy, of heat

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:26.820
specifically.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.670
And so what does that
have to do with food?

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:33.350
What does that have to do
with what we digest and eat?

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:34.880
Really, what you're
talking about

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:38.060
is the potential
chemical energy that

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:41.390
is in the bonds of the
macronutrients of food.

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:46.100
And by digesting,
assimilating, and metabolizing

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:48.830
those nutrients, we
are able to create

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:53.330
energy and the in-product
of that, mostly,

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:58.850
is ATP, adenosine triphosphate,
which is your body's energy

00:09:58.850 --> 00:09:59.570
currency.

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:04.280
So to understand ATP,
just try to think about--

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:07.010
if you're trying to power
these various reactions

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:08.262
in your body--

00:10:08.262 --> 00:10:09.970
and we're talking
about tens of thousands

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:11.680
of enzymes that require ATP--

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:16.210
it doesn't make
sense that you would

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:19.300
have to create a bunch
of micro explosions.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:23.980
You want something that can
transfer high-energy phosphates

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.470
to power these
reactions, to give up

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.720
essentially its energy
to power something

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.740
that might otherwise
be unfavorable.

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:36.620
So a lot of metabolism
is simply creating

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:40.880
ATP, which the end
of the line of that--

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:43.190
I'm going to work
backwards-- is what's

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.750
called oxidative respiration.

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.540
So that happens in
the mitochondria.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:50.160
Everybody's heard mitochondria,
a powerhouse of the cell.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.830
And that is done through
essentially creating a hydrogen

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:57.620
ion gradient across
the mitochondria, which

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:00.770
powers the production
of ATP by converting

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:04.580
free phosphate plus ATP to ADP.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:07.730
Now the way that hydrogen
ion gradient is created

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:13.010
is through creating hydrogen
ions that can be donated

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:15.180
through the Krebs cycle.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.760
Now the Krebs cycle is
linked to glycolysis.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.030
So if we talk about
carbohydrate metabolism,

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:24.140
carbohydrates basically,
other than fructose,

00:11:24.140 --> 00:11:28.220
get converted into glucose,
which can go into glycolysis,

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:30.860
and you can produce some
ATPs through glycolysis.

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:33.500
And then it boils
down to pyruvate,

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:36.080
then acetyl-CoA, which
goes to the Krebs cycle,

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:39.350
produces a lot more
ATPs from that.

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:41.285
If you talk about
protein, protein

00:11:41.285 --> 00:11:44.450
is a little bit different
because protein gets converted

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:46.130
to amino acids,
which can be used

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:48.860
for muscle protein
synthesis or protein

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:50.840
synthesis in other tissues.

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:53.360
But it also can be converted
through gluconeogenesis

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:54.710
to glucose.

00:11:54.710 --> 00:11:58.860
And there also are some
ketogenic amino acids as well.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.610
And so you can have
a few different ways

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.170
to get to the Krebs cycle.

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:07.220
Either being through
acetyl-CoA or through

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:11.150
glucose going through the
glycolysis to pyruvate.

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:17.510
Then you have fatty acids,
which are able to create energy

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.910
through what's called beta
oxidation where, essentially,

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:22.202
you're taking these fatty
acids and you're lopping them

00:12:22.202 --> 00:12:25.040
off two carbons at a time
to produce acetyl-CoA which,

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:27.112
again, can go into
the Krebs cycle,

00:12:27.112 --> 00:12:28.820
produce those hydrogen
ions that can then

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:30.960
power the production of ATP.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.050
So that's kind of like
at the cellular level

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:34.770
of how this stuff works.

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.970
But stepping back and
taking it back out,

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:41.780
what does that have to do with
weight loss or weight gain?

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:46.110
Well, when you think about
the balance of energy

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:47.310
in versus energy out.

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:48.510
Sounds very simple.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:50.400
But let's look at
what actually makes up

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:52.560
energy in versus energy out.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.810
First of all, you've
got to realize

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:57.360
that the energy
inside of the equation

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.840
is more difficult to
track than people think.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:04.260
So one, food labels,
which we like

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:08.790
to think as being
from upon high,

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:10.740
can have up to a
20% error in them.

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:11.025
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Really?

00:13:11.025 --> 00:13:11.850
LAYNE NORTON: Oh, yeah.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:13.365
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So a 100 calorie--

00:13:13.365 --> 00:13:15.240
something listed as 100
calories per serving,

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.677
it could-- what's actually
in there could be 80 or 120.

00:13:17.677 --> 00:13:18.510
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:19.410
Exactly.

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:22.630
So that's one aspect of it.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.480
The second aspect is there's
what's called your energy,

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:27.420
but then there's also
metabolizable energy.

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:30.660
So if you have food stuff with
say, a lot of insoluble fiber,

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:34.050
typically, insoluble fiber
is not really digestible,

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:38.040
and so you could have "quite
a bit of carbohydrate," know

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:41.880
but if you can't extract
the energy from it--

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.950
and typically, this is
because insoluble fiber

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:48.270
from plant material, the
carbohydrate and even

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:53.220
some of the protein is bound up
in the plant structure, which

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:55.830
makes it inaccessible
to digestive enzymes.

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:59.050
And so this is what adds bulk
to your stool and whatnot.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:02.190
But again, reduces the
metabolizable energy in there.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:04.200
And there's some
evidence that based

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:08.400
on people's individual gut
microbiome, that some people

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:13.200
may actually be better at
extracting energy out of fiber

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:14.820
compared to other people.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:17.730
So just starting
off right there,

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:22.810
OK, there's quite a bit of play
in the energy inside of things.

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:24.477
Now, one of the
things people will say

00:14:24.477 --> 00:14:26.310
is, well, see that's
why you shouldn't worry

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:29.520
about tracking calories, because
the food levels can be 20% off.

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:33.337
And what I'll say is, OK.

00:14:33.337 --> 00:14:34.920
I understand where
you're coming from.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:37.960
But typically, if it's off, it's
going to be consistently off.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:40.140
And if you're consistent
with how you track it,

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:44.310
eventually, you'll be able to
know what you're taking in.

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:48.645
And that's like saying, well,
don't worry about tracking

00:14:48.645 --> 00:14:49.290
if you're--

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:53.470
I like to use
financial examples.

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:56.260
We know that to
save money, you have

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.360
to earn more money
than you spend.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:00.400
Well, you can't exactly
know how much money

00:15:00.400 --> 00:15:02.980
you're earning at a time
because there's inflation

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:04.030
and then there is--

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:08.020
if you have investments, those
can be different interest rates

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:09.310
and whatnot.

00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:11.620
It's like, OK, if
you have a budget,

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:14.260
you have a reasonable idea
of what it's going to be.

00:15:14.260 --> 00:15:15.730
And you make
certain assumptions,

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.560
but you can relatively guess.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:17.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:18.627
That's a good example.

00:15:18.627 --> 00:15:19.683
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

00:15:19.683 --> 00:15:21.350
So now, let's look
at the energy outside

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:23.960
of the equation, which is
actually way more complicated.

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:29.070
And so your energy out is
a few different buckets.

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:30.500
The first one and
the biggest one

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:32.750
is your resting metabolic rate.

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:34.560
So your RMR.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.920
And that, for most people,
is anywhere from 50% to 70%

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:40.480
of your total daily
energy expenditure.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:43.440
Now, people use the term
metabolic rate and energy

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.080
expenditure interchangeably,
but they're not the same thing.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:48.960
So your total daily
energy expenditure

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:53.497
is the summation of all the
energy you expend in a day.

00:15:53.497 --> 00:15:55.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Walking upstairs,

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:56.850
exercise if you do it--

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:57.270
LAYNE NORTON: Fidgeting.

00:15:57.270 --> 00:15:58.187
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:15:58.187 --> 00:15:59.760
Plus your resting
metabolic rate.

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:00.030
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:02.170
So resting metabolic rate
is a big part of that,

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.990
but it's not the only thing.

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:06.360
So that's usually
about 50% to 70%.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:09.640
And sedentary people will be
on the higher end of that.

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:11.580
So it'll be a bigger
proportion whereas people

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.847
who are more active, it'll
be a little bit lower, not

00:16:13.847 --> 00:16:15.430
because their metabolic
rate is lower,

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:17.055
but because they're
expending a greater

00:16:17.055 --> 00:16:20.098
percentage of their calories
from physical activity.

00:16:20.098 --> 00:16:22.140
Then you have something
called the thermic effect

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:24.960
of food, which is a
relatively small percentage

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:26.730
of your total daily
energy expenditure.

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:28.980
It's about 5% to 10%.

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.260
And very difficult to
measure and usually

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:34.230
what researchers do when
they're looking at this stuff is

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:35.820
they just make an
assumption about it.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.460
They use a constant.

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:39.960
But that's about 5% to 10% of
your daily energy expenditure.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:42.360
And that refers to
the amount of energy

00:16:42.360 --> 00:16:45.630
it takes to extract
the energy out of food.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:49.260
So think about your
body like a car.

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:50.760
You don't just have
gas in your tank

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:52.140
and it spontaneously starts up.

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:54.390
You have to have a battery
so you put in energy so you

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:56.610
can get the energy
out of the petrol

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.370
that you have in your car.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:02.390
Similar with food, you
can't just eat food

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:04.700
and then it just
appears in your cells

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:06.260
and you start doing stuff.

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:08.270
It has to be
systematically broken

00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:12.500
down and put into forms that
can actually produce energy.

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:16.220
And so you have to put some
energy in to achieve that.

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:18.920
And a lot of times,
people will say something

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:22.920
like, well, not all
calories are created equal.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:25.910
That's not true, because calorie
is just a unit of measurement.

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:28.190
That would be like saying
not all seconds on a clock

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:29.210
are created equal.

00:17:29.210 --> 00:17:30.530
Yes, they are.

00:17:30.530 --> 00:17:35.030
All sources of calories may have
differential effects on energy

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:36.080
expenditure and appetite.

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:40.410
So if we look at something
like fat, for example,

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:42.830
the TEF of fat is
about 0% to 3%.

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:45.050
Meaning, if you eat
100 calories from fat,

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:46.800
your net will be
about 97 to 100.

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:48.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So the
process of breaking down

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:52.940
that fat, essentially,
subtracts some of the calories

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:55.233
away because you used
it in creating energy--

00:17:55.233 --> 00:17:56.150
LAYNE NORTON: Correct.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:57.440
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --by
breaking those chemical bonds

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:58.055
to create ATP.

00:17:58.055 --> 00:17:58.972
LAYNE NORTON: Correct.

00:17:58.972 --> 00:17:59.610
Correct.

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:02.240
So you have like, for example,
some enzymes that require

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:04.020
ATP to run these processes.

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:07.285
Now, fat is actually the easiest
thing to convert into energy.

00:18:07.285 --> 00:18:08.660
Then you have
carbohydrate, which

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:10.950
has a TEF of like 5% to 10%.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:12.950
So you eat 100 calories
from carbohydrate.

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:16.580
And obviously, the fiber content
makes a big difference on this.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:19.850
But if you eat 100
calories, you'll net 92 95.

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:23.540
Protein is about
a 20% to 30% TEF.

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:25.040
So if you eat
calories from protein,

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:27.050
you're only netting 70 to 80.

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:28.700
Now, you're still net--

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:31.280
people say, well, you
can't eat too much protein.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:36.060
Well, people will ask, well,
can protein be stored as fat?

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:37.740
The carbon's from
protein it's unlikely,

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:39.160
it's going to wind
up in adipose tissue.

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:40.743
But if you're eating
a lot of protein,

00:18:40.743 --> 00:18:43.668
overall as part of a lot of
calories, it has to be oxidized

00:18:43.668 --> 00:18:45.960
and it can't provide a calorie
cushion for other things

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:47.430
to be stored in fat.

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:52.530
But protein itself does provide
a net positive for calories,

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.600
but less so than
carbohydrate or fat.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:56.310
And tends to be more satiating.

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:59.850
So again, when people talk about
are all calories created equal,

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:01.620
yes, but all sources
of calories may

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.630
have differential effects
on energy expenditure

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:05.010
and appetite.

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:07.980
So that's the TEF bucket
and the BMR bucket.

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:09.630
Then we go to physical activity.

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:11.920
And physical activity is,
essentially, two parts.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:16.050
There's exercise, which is kind
of your purposeful movements

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:19.480
like you go out for a walk,
you do a training session.

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:20.370
I mean, whatever.

00:19:20.370 --> 00:19:23.020
Any purposeful activity.

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:24.670
And then you have
what's called NEAT

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:27.760
which is non-exercise activity
thermogenesis, which I think

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:29.170
is actually really cool.

00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:29.745
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It's fascinating.

00:19:29.745 --> 00:19:30.310
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:30.810
It is.

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:33.610
So I was actually hanging
out with somebody last night

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:36.520
and I was noticing them,
they were fidgeting

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:38.830
their feet and their fingers.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:42.923
And I said, have you
always been pretty lean?

00:19:42.923 --> 00:19:44.590
And they were like,
yeah, I never really

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:47.260
had a problem
maintaining leanness.

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:50.620
And when you look at the
obese resistant phenotype,

00:19:50.620 --> 00:19:53.950
people think they
have high BMR or they

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:57.250
exercise a lot and really
what it seems to be as neat.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:00.850
They tend to-- if they
overeat, they just

00:20:00.850 --> 00:20:03.170
spontaneously increase
their physical activity.

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:05.590
Now, people get NEAT confused.

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:07.590
I've heard people say,
well, I'm going to go out

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:08.950
for a walk to get my NEAT up.

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:10.340
That's not NEAT.

00:20:10.340 --> 00:20:13.000
NEAT is not something you
can consciously modify.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:15.980
What you're doing there, if
it's purposeful, it's exercise.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.770
So for example,
when I'm talking,

00:20:17.770 --> 00:20:20.260
if I'm waving around my
hands, if I'm tapping my feet,

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:21.100
if I'm--

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:22.330
whatever.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:24.010
That's NEAT.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:26.920
But trying to get yourself--

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:28.840
I'm just going to
tap my foot more,

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:30.610
well, now if I'm
consciously having

00:20:30.610 --> 00:20:32.020
to do this, then my focus--

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:34.030
I mean, you know
how the brain works.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:35.200
Very hard to do--

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:36.910
you don't really do
two things at once.

00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:37.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

00:20:37.090 --> 00:20:39.050
LAYNE NORTON: You switch
quickly between tasks, right?

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:39.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Absolutely.

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:41.950
Can I quickly ask, was the
person that you were referring

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:43.735
to our friend Ben Bruno?

00:20:43.735 --> 00:20:44.950
LAYNE NORTON: No, no.

00:20:44.950 --> 00:20:46.015
But he is fidgety too.

00:20:46.015 --> 00:20:46.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:20:46.150 --> 00:20:48.070
Amazing online fitness channel.

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:50.038
He's a freakishly
strong individual.

00:20:50.038 --> 00:20:50.830
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:51.747
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:20:51.747 --> 00:20:54.310
And I can't remember
whether or not,

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:55.660
Ben, you're a fidgetter or not.

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:57.430
But anyway, I'll
have to go check

00:20:57.430 --> 00:20:59.260
and we'll measure
your fidgeting.

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:03.250
About non-exercise induced
thermogenesis NEAT,

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:06.970
my understanding of the old
papers on this, old being,

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:08.920
I guess, back to
the mid '90s, is

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:12.303
that the calorie burn from NEAT
is actually pretty significant.

00:21:12.303 --> 00:21:14.470
We're not talking about 100
calories or 200 calories

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:15.010
per day.

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:17.210
We're talking about,
in some cases,

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:20.860
hundreds of thousand-- excuse
me, hundreds to maybe even

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:22.423
close to 1,000 calories per day.

00:21:22.423 --> 00:21:23.590
Could you elaborate on that?

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:24.382
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:21:24.382 --> 00:21:27.120
So there was actually a really
classic study, I think, from--

00:21:27.120 --> 00:21:30.370
I want to say it's
from Levine in 1995.

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:32.080
It was metabolic ward study.

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:34.480
And hopefully, I don't
butcher the study

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:37.547
because I'm trying to
pull it out of my brain.

00:21:37.547 --> 00:21:40.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I don't expect
you to have that in your head.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:45.530
Although, I must say, you have
a quite extensive PubMed ID,

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:46.680
grab bag in there.

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:47.180
So--

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:48.350
LAYNE NORTON: I try
to bring the receipts.

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:49.880
I try to bring the receipts.

00:21:49.880 --> 00:21:51.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We will put a
link to this study in the show

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:52.250
note captions.

00:21:52.250 --> 00:21:55.160
So people can peruse it
if they like it, yeah.

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:57.440
LAYNE NORTON: So I believe
they had people overeat.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:59.910
And I think it was by
like 1,000 calories a day

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:01.365
and I think for six weeks.

00:22:01.365 --> 00:22:02.990
And I mean, this is
the metabolic ward.

00:22:02.990 --> 00:22:05.490
So this is very
tightly controlled.

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:07.010
It's as tight as you get.

00:22:07.010 --> 00:22:09.290
And what was interesting
is, of course, on average,

00:22:09.290 --> 00:22:12.050
people gained weight
and gained fat mass.

00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:16.100
But some people gained
more than expected,

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:18.680
and there was one person, in
particular, who only gained

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:21.620
like just over 1/2 a kilo.

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:23.510
They should have gained like--

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:25.340
I think it was something
like 3 to 4 kilos.

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:26.660
It was predicted.

00:22:26.660 --> 00:22:29.120
And what they found
is this individual

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:33.440
just spontaneously increased
their physical activity.

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:36.200
He didn't purposefully
do it, it just happened.

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:39.830
And I mean,
anecdotally, I've seen

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:44.510
people who are, again,
very lean even eat a meal,

00:22:44.510 --> 00:22:46.870
sit down, and start sweating.

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:48.222
And be very fidgety.

00:22:48.222 --> 00:22:50.180
There was a natural
bodybuilder back in the day

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:51.440
named Jim Cordova.

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:55.160
And this guy was just
very lean all the time,

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:57.980
and he was exactly
that phenotype.

00:22:57.980 --> 00:22:59.503
He would walk up
a flight of stairs

00:22:59.503 --> 00:23:00.920
and all of a sudden
he's sweating.

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:02.855
Sit down eat a
meal, he's sweating.

00:23:02.855 --> 00:23:03.470
He's just--

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:04.803
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He's a furnace.

00:23:04.803 --> 00:23:07.760
LAYNE NORTON: Just
expending energy.

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:09.890
And what's very
interesting about NEAT

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:13.040
is that seems to be
the most modifiable--

00:23:13.040 --> 00:23:14.840
I mean, exercise
is very modifiable

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:16.860
because you can be
intentional with that.

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:21.140
But of BMR, TEF,
and NEAT, NEAT seems

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:22.610
to be far more modifiable.

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:28.170
So even a bodyweight
reduction of 10%,

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:31.050
they've observed
a decrease in NEAT

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:36.820
of almost 500 calories a day for
a 10% reduction in body weight.

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:39.300
Now, you also do
get a decline in BMR

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:41.880
when you lose weight,
one, because you're just

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:44.460
in a smaller body now and
so it takes less energy

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:46.710
to lock them out.

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:49.680
But also there's what's called
metabolic adaptation, which

00:23:49.680 --> 00:23:54.180
is a further reduction
in your BMR than expected

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:55.590
from the loss of body mass.

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:59.160
And that's on average
usually around like 15%.

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:01.620
But it does seem to be--

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:04.290
there's new evidence coming
out on the metabolic adaptation

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:05.520
from BMR.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:08.190
And it seems to
be a little bit--

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:10.380
kind of in the
transition phases.

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:14.280
So if you start a diet
within the first few weeks,

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:20.340
you will have a reduction
in BMR that then just--

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:22.740
thereafter, any
further reduction

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:25.740
is mostly from the amount
of body mass you lose.

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:28.770
And then if you, like for
example, finish a diet

00:24:28.770 --> 00:24:31.860
and move your calories
to maintenance,

00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:35.580
within a few weeks, BMR
starts to come back up.

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:37.890
There is still a
small reduction,

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:39.960
but I used to be
somebody who thought

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:42.570
that BMR, metabolic
adaptation was

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:46.110
a big reason why people stopped
losing weight or plateaued.

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:48.292
And now, I think it's
much more to do with NEAT.

00:24:48.292 --> 00:24:49.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:52.890
And you said that it
can't be conscious

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:55.183
because that will distract
us from other activities.

00:24:55.183 --> 00:24:57.600
I don't know if you've had a
chance to look at this study.

00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:58.320
And I'll send it to you.

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:00.630
Maybe I'd be fun to do a kind
of an online journal club

00:25:00.630 --> 00:25:01.750
about this at some point soon.

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:04.167
But there's a study that came
out of University of Houston

00:25:04.167 --> 00:25:07.800
recently having people do, now,
this is a long period of time.

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:10.785
Four hours a day of, basically,
a soleus pushup, which

00:25:10.785 --> 00:25:11.970
is basically a heel raise.

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:14.273
A seated catchphrase with
one foot not weighted.

00:25:14.273 --> 00:25:15.690
And then they
looked at it a bunch

00:25:15.690 --> 00:25:19.080
of things about glucose
metabolism and glucose

00:25:19.080 --> 00:25:20.460
clearance and insulin levels.

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:22.770
And they didn't
conclude that people

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:24.240
burned a ton of
calories, but what

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:27.990
they concluded was that blood
sugar regulation improved

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:28.560
greatly.

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:31.290
And I think, there was
a lot of excitement

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:34.980
about this at some
level, but based

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:37.380
on everything you're telling
me, this fits perfectly

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:38.680
with what's known about neat.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:41.070
So this fell somewhere
in between with--

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:44.250
in between, excuse me,
sort of deliberate exercise

00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:45.378
and spontaneous movement.

00:25:45.378 --> 00:25:47.670
I guess they've tried to make
that spontaneous movement

00:25:47.670 --> 00:25:48.750
a little bit more conscious.

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:50.040
LAYNE NORTON: Well,
what I'll tell

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:51.750
people is if you're
worried about NEAT,

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:54.807
one thing you can do,
like these watches,

00:25:54.807 --> 00:25:56.640
for example where people
are like, oh, well,

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:58.740
told me I burned
these many calories.

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:01.500
They are not accurate
for energy expenditure.

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:06.360
I mean, it is like, there
was a meta analysis in 2018,

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:12.690
I want to say,
between a 28% and 93%

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:15.090
overestimation of energy
expenditure by these watches.

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:15.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Fitness track.

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:16.860
So for those of you
listening, we're

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:18.027
not going to name the brand.

00:26:18.027 --> 00:26:20.800
But fitness trackers-- so
wrist-worn fitness trackers.

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:22.592
LAYNE NORTON: And this
is across the board.

00:26:22.592 --> 00:26:23.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:26:23.230 --> 00:26:24.630
LAYNE NORTON: So like
depending on the brand,

00:26:24.630 --> 00:26:26.550
it could be more or
less but, they all

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:28.440
overestimated the amount of--

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:30.250
the calories you
burn from exercise.

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:32.880
So this is actually a great
example where people go, well,

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:34.797
calories in, calories
out doesn't work for me,

00:26:34.797 --> 00:26:37.260
because I eat in a calorie
deficit, I didn't lose weight.

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:39.900
When I talk to
them, usually, they

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:41.790
went to an online calculator.

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:42.540
It's a few things.

00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:44.550
They went to an
online calculator, put

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:47.522
in their information, it spat
out some calories to eat,

00:26:47.522 --> 00:26:49.230
and they ate that and
didn't lose weight.

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:51.480
And it's like, well, what
do you think is more likely?

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:54.900
That you're defying the laws
of conservation of energy

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:57.780
or that you might
have not gotten

00:26:57.780 --> 00:26:58.923
the right number for you?

00:26:58.923 --> 00:27:00.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The
measurement tool was off.

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:01.632
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:27:01.632 --> 00:27:05.010
The next thing is a lot of
people weigh very sporadically.

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:06.510
And I'll tell people
like, if you're

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:09.750
going to make an intentional
weight loss a goal,

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:12.000
and again, this can be
different for different people,

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.130
but typically, I
tell people, weigh

00:27:14.130 --> 00:27:16.050
in, first thing in the
morning or after you

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:17.640
go to the bathroom,
do it every day,

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:19.590
and take the average
of that for the week.

00:27:19.590 --> 00:27:21.900
And then compare that to
the next week's average.

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:23.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Can I ask
one-- sorry to interrupt,

00:27:23.490 --> 00:27:25.448
but one quick question
about that when you say,

00:27:25.448 --> 00:27:29.580
go to the bathroom not
to get too detailed here

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.800
unnecessarily, but are you
talking about urination

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:33.340
and emptying your bowels?

00:27:33.340 --> 00:27:35.715
Ideally, because you did eat
a big meal the night before.

00:27:35.715 --> 00:27:36.240
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:36.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:36.930
Got it.

00:27:36.930 --> 00:27:37.722
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:27:37.722 --> 00:27:42.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So wake up,
use the bathroom in all forms

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:45.570
that you're ready, and
then get on the scale,

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:49.260
take that measurement, average
that across the week, and then

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:50.907
maybe every Monday,
you take that value

00:27:50.907 --> 00:27:51.990
and see how it progresses.

00:27:51.990 --> 00:27:52.990
LAYNE NORTON: All right.

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:56.280
And the reason I recommend
doing that is, if you're just

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:59.850
sporadically weighing in, as
somebody who weighs themselves

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:01.860
pretty regularly,
I mean, my weight

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:07.050
will fluctuate 5, 6 pounds, and
not seemingly changing much.

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:10.350
And that's just-- those
short term changes are fluid.

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:13.890
So I've had it before,
where week to week,

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:15.060
my average didn't change.

00:28:15.060 --> 00:28:18.150
But between the lowest weigh
in from a previous week

00:28:18.150 --> 00:28:22.680
and the highest weigh in, might
have been like 8 pounds, right?

00:28:22.680 --> 00:28:26.580
So if you're somebody who
just randomly is weighing in

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:29.100
and you're eating in
a calorie deficit,

00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:31.830
and you just weigh in
one day where you've just

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:34.620
whatever reason holding some
more fluid, then you're oh,

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:37.200
see, this isn't working when
in reality, your average

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:38.380
might be dropping.

00:28:38.380 --> 00:28:40.830
So that's one of the
reasons, and actually,

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:42.960
believe it or not,
weight fluctuations

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:45.630
are actually identified as a
major reason why people get

00:28:45.630 --> 00:28:48.370
discouraged from weight loss.

00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:51.430
It stops the buy-in, when
they have a fluctuation up.

00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:52.680
So that's one of the reasons--

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:53.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Great point.

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:54.210
LAYNE NORTON: --one
of the reasons early

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:55.950
on that low carb diets
tend to work really

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:57.992
well is because people
lose a lot of water weight

00:28:57.992 --> 00:28:59.820
really quickly, and
they get that buy-in.

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:01.410
So they're, oh, this is working.

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:01.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:03.162
We can return to
that in a little bit

00:29:03.162 --> 00:29:06.305
because I have theories
as to how that--

00:29:06.305 --> 00:29:07.740
when people eat
less carbohydrate,

00:29:07.740 --> 00:29:10.110
they excrete more
water, and they'll see--

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:12.750
for the first time, they'll see
some definition in their abs,

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:13.875
oh, my God, this diet's amazing.

00:29:13.875 --> 00:29:14.667
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:29:14.667 --> 00:29:17.440
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And the fluid
loss does hold that promise.

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:19.273
I think fluid loss can
do some other things.

00:29:19.273 --> 00:29:21.807
It might make people
literally feel lighter,

00:29:21.807 --> 00:29:23.890
although it can be-- it
has some negative effects.

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:25.895
I do have one quick
question, and I

00:29:25.895 --> 00:29:27.770
do want-- we will return
to NEAT in a moment.

00:29:27.770 --> 00:29:29.950
But when you say,
the caloric burn

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:32.140
as a consequence
of exercise, I want

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:35.360
to ask about the caloric
burn during that exercise?

00:29:35.360 --> 00:29:37.480
So for instance, if
somebody is on the treadmill

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:39.465
and they'll see, OK,
they burn 400 calories.

00:29:39.465 --> 00:29:40.840
Actually, I think
this is a month

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:42.490
where a number of
prominent podcasters

00:29:42.490 --> 00:29:44.920
like Bert Kreischer, Tom
Segura, Joe Rogan, and others,

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:46.462
I think they call
it "Sober October,"

00:29:46.462 --> 00:29:48.070
but in addition to
avoiding alcohol,

00:29:48.070 --> 00:29:50.590
they're burning 500 calories
per day during the exercise.

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:50.830
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:52.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
They're measuring it.

00:29:52.150 --> 00:29:53.240
A lot of people do this.

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:55.750
They think-- they take
track of weather--

00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:59.050
excuse me, take stock of how
many calories they burned.

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:02.650
My understanding is that if
that particular form of exercise

00:30:02.650 --> 00:30:05.320
is a muscle building
form of exercise,

00:30:05.320 --> 00:30:09.790
that at some point later, there
might be an increase in muscle

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:12.040
if you did everything
right, do everything right,

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:13.690
and then you will
burn more energy

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:15.490
as a consequence of
adding that tissue.

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:18.880
That's a long process
as and we will discuss.

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:23.260
But I have heard about this
post-exercise induced increase

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:25.000
in oxidative metabolism.

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:27.500
I'm probably not using the
right language in here.

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:30.550
So if I were to go out, for
instance, and do some sprints.

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:33.190
Run hard for a minute, jog for
a minute, run hard for a minute,

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:36.390
and do that 10 times over.

00:30:36.390 --> 00:30:41.730
Let's assume I burn 400 calories
during that exercise bout.

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:44.790
But my understanding is that
in the hours that follow,

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:47.160
my basal metabolic rate
will have increased.

00:30:47.160 --> 00:30:49.410
Is that true, and is
it significant enough

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:50.085
to care about?

00:30:50.085 --> 00:30:53.490
LAYNE NORTON: So answer
both of those questions.

00:30:53.490 --> 00:30:56.580
Yes, there does seem
to be a small increase

00:30:56.580 --> 00:30:59.130
in metabolic rate,
and no, it does not

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:01.420
appear to be enough to
actually make a difference.

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:02.700
So when they look at--

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:06.630
and again, this is
where I tell people--

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:08.760
I think I have a good
perspective on this,

00:31:08.760 --> 00:31:11.560
because my undergraduate degree
was a biochemistry degree.

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:12.925
So I was very into mechanisms.

00:31:12.925 --> 00:31:13.800
You know what I mean?

00:31:13.800 --> 00:31:17.430
It was like, oh, if we just do
this and this, we'll get this.

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:19.920
And then I did nutrition
as a graduate degree,

00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:23.165
and then my advisor was so great
because you could do something

00:31:23.165 --> 00:31:25.290
over here and he could tell
you how it would affect

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:26.700
vitamin D metabolism over here.

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:27.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
This is Don Lemon?

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:28.700
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah, Don Lemon.

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:32.190
So he would always
kind of say, yeah,

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:34.170
but what's the outcome
going to be, right?

00:31:34.170 --> 00:31:37.385
So this is actually one of the
things I changed my mind on.

00:31:37.385 --> 00:31:38.760
Was I used to be
very much, well,

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:41.880
I think high intensity interval
training is probably better

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:44.160
because you get this
post-exercise energy burn

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:46.900
which they do see in
some of these studies.

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:52.200
But in the meta analyzes and
more tightly controlled studies

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:55.170
where they equate work between
high intensity intervals

00:31:55.170 --> 00:32:00.000
and moderate or low intensity
cardio, so equating work,

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:02.400
they don't see differences
in the loss of body fat.

00:32:02.400 --> 00:32:05.280
And so to me, if I'm
looking like, that's

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.280
the example of a
mechanism, which is OK,

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:11.830
we're seeing this small increase
in basal metabolic rate,

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:15.310
that should lead to
increased loss of body fat.

00:32:15.310 --> 00:32:19.680
But again, remember, you're
capturing a snapshot in time.

00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:22.210
But we don't see a difference
in the loss of body fat.

00:32:22.210 --> 00:32:24.960
So what may be happening-- and
again, I'm just speculating.

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:28.500
But a way to explain it could
be, you might have an increase

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:31.800
and then you might actually
have a decrease that tends

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:32.940
to just wash it out, right?

00:32:32.940 --> 00:32:33.898
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I see.

00:32:33.898 --> 00:32:36.600
And I have to imagine
some forms of exercise.

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:40.290
This would be highly individual,
but will spike appetite more

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:40.960
than others.

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:44.490
So for instance, if I go out
for a 45-minute jog which I do.

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:47.213
A 45 to 60-minute hike or
jog once a week, I just

00:32:47.213 --> 00:32:49.630
make it a point to do that or
ruck or something like that.

00:32:49.630 --> 00:32:51.210
Throw on a weight vest and hike.

00:32:51.210 --> 00:32:54.900
After that, I find I'm very
thirsty, I want to hydrate.

00:32:54.900 --> 00:32:56.670
But I'm not that hungry.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:33:00.730
And that's true of all
cardiovascular exercise for me.

00:33:00.730 --> 00:33:06.080
But after I weight train,
about 60 to 90 minutes later,

00:33:06.080 --> 00:33:08.090
I want to eat the refrigerator.

00:33:08.090 --> 00:33:11.150
And so obviously,
calories in, calories out

00:33:11.150 --> 00:33:14.060
dictates that that will
play an important role as to

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:16.100
whether or not I gain or
lose weight, et cetera.

00:33:16.100 --> 00:33:19.220
So is it safe to say that
the specific form of exercise

00:33:19.220 --> 00:33:24.687
that people choose needs to
be taken in consideration?

00:33:24.687 --> 00:33:26.270
Calories in, calories
out, so how much

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:27.740
is burned during the exercise?

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:30.640
Also how much that exercise
tends to stimulate appetite.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:32.390
I don't know whether
or not people explore

00:33:32.390 --> 00:33:34.010
this in their rigorous studies.

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:36.590
And whether or not
that form of exercise

00:33:36.590 --> 00:33:41.420
actually increases lean
muscle mass or not.

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:43.220
Now, we've taken
exercise and split it

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:44.803
into a number of
different dimensions,

00:33:44.803 --> 00:33:47.750
but this is what you are
so masterful at is really

00:33:47.750 --> 00:33:50.240
parsing how the different
components work individually

00:33:50.240 --> 00:33:51.090
and together.

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:53.780
So if you would
just expand on that,

00:33:53.780 --> 00:33:55.490
I'd love to know
what you're thinking.

00:33:55.490 --> 00:33:56.282
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:33:56.282 --> 00:33:58.580
So this is actually a
really fascinating thing.

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:00.620
So first thing, I
want to just go back

00:34:00.620 --> 00:34:04.220
to talking about like, for
example, Bert and Tom and Joe,

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:07.070
we're going to do 500
calories a day on whatever.

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:09.530
So those apparatuses
don't measure those things

00:34:09.530 --> 00:34:10.639
effectively either.

00:34:10.639 --> 00:34:12.060
Just like these watches.

00:34:12.060 --> 00:34:15.980
But the one thing I will
say is, if you are--

00:34:15.980 --> 00:34:18.980
like for example, if I do two
hours of resistance training,

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:21.440
typically, this will say I
burned about 1,000 calories.

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:23.239
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's a
lot of resistance training.

00:34:23.239 --> 00:34:23.690
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

00:34:23.690 --> 00:34:25.148
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
My weight workouts

00:34:25.148 --> 00:34:27.992
are warm up for 10 minutes and
then one hour of work done.

00:34:27.992 --> 00:34:28.909
LAYNE NORTON: I just--

00:34:28.909 --> 00:34:29.576
I love to train.

00:34:29.576 --> 00:34:30.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:31.639
And you can recover from.

00:34:31.639 --> 00:34:33.719
My recovery quotient
is pretty low.

00:34:33.719 --> 00:34:36.530
So I've been training
for 30 plus years,

00:34:36.530 --> 00:34:39.920
and I found that if I
do more than an hour

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:44.449
of hard work in the gym,
meaning resistance training,

00:34:44.449 --> 00:34:48.395
75 minutes maybe, I'm OK, but
past that, I have to take two,

00:34:48.395 --> 00:34:50.270
maybe even three days
off before I can train.

00:34:50.270 --> 00:34:52.550
My nervous system just
doesn't tolerate it well.

00:34:52.550 --> 00:34:55.135
So I limit it to
an hour, you know.

00:34:55.135 --> 00:34:57.260
LAYNE NORTON: And part of
that to remember is like,

00:34:57.260 --> 00:34:59.580
I've built up to that over
a long period of time.

00:34:59.580 --> 00:35:01.580
So you couldn't just throw
somebody in and start

00:35:01.580 --> 00:35:02.480
having them do two hours a day.

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:03.890
It's not going to
go well for them.

