00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:46.160
And then the other thing I
was kind of circling around on

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:48.770
was, if you're worried about
NEAT, tracking your steps

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:51.560
can be helpful because people
step counts can spontaneously

00:36:51.560 --> 00:36:53.268
decrease when they're
on a fat loss diet,

00:36:53.268 --> 00:36:55.250
they don't even realize it.

00:36:55.250 --> 00:36:57.290
And that, again, not
a complete measure

00:36:57.290 --> 00:37:01.130
of NEAT, but what we've had
some clients do with our team

00:37:01.130 --> 00:37:04.940
building coaches is, they'll
say OK, you're, at 8,000 steps

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:06.020
right now.

00:37:06.020 --> 00:37:08.450
We're not going to add
any purposeful cardio.

00:37:08.450 --> 00:37:13.220
But whatever you need to do
to maintain that 8,000 steps,

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:13.880
do that.

00:37:13.880 --> 00:37:18.122
And sometimes, they have to add
15, 20, 30 minutes of cardio,

00:37:18.122 --> 00:37:20.330
because they're spontaneous
activity that they're not

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:21.470
even aware of goes down.

00:37:21.470 --> 00:37:24.200
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's
a really excellent point.

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:27.770
I've heard the 10,000
steps per day number was,

00:37:27.770 --> 00:37:28.790
we all heard that.

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:31.640
And then I learned that, 10,000
were just kind of thrown out

00:37:31.640 --> 00:37:32.907
as an arbitrary number.

00:37:32.907 --> 00:37:35.240
So we're like get eight-hour
intermittent fasting thing,

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:37.483
there's a story behind
that because actually I

00:37:37.483 --> 00:37:38.900
spoke to Satchin,
and it turns out

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:40.760
that the graduate
student in his lab

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:43.830
did that initial study, which
was on mice, by the way,

00:37:43.830 --> 00:37:46.910
was limited to being in
lab for about eight hours

00:37:46.910 --> 00:37:48.080
by their significant other.

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:50.690
So the eight-hour feeding window
is actually the consequence

00:37:50.690 --> 00:37:53.100
of this person's relationship.

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:53.600
So--

00:37:53.600 --> 00:37:54.920
LAYNE NORTON: That is
a really great point

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:57.260
that people don't realize
when they-- a lot of people

00:37:57.260 --> 00:37:59.840
will try to copy like
scientific studies.

00:37:59.840 --> 00:38:03.950
And I'll tell people like,
listen, scientific studies are

00:38:03.950 --> 00:38:06.770
so confined.

00:38:06.770 --> 00:38:09.020
You need to be very careful
with how broadly you

00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:10.310
apply what's in there.

00:38:10.310 --> 00:38:14.330
Like they're a very big hammer
is the way I look at it.

00:38:14.330 --> 00:38:16.280
They're not a scalpel,
they're a big hammer.

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:19.490
And I think, a lot of
times in terms of coaching,

00:38:19.490 --> 00:38:21.920
scientific studies will
tell you what not to do

00:38:21.920 --> 00:38:24.295
rather than what to do, right?

00:38:24.295 --> 00:38:25.670
But getting back
to your question

00:38:25.670 --> 00:38:28.790
about exercise,
appetite, so first off,

00:38:28.790 --> 00:38:32.060
I'm not really aware if
there's evidence showing

00:38:32.060 --> 00:38:35.330
like differential effects of
different forms of exercise

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:39.950
on appetite, it's possible,
but again, it also

00:38:39.950 --> 00:38:41.870
could be like a
placebo effect, right?

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:45.260
Because we-- like for example,
you and I, grew up in an era

00:38:45.260 --> 00:38:46.927
where the muscle
magazines, it was like,

00:38:46.927 --> 00:38:48.635
well, as soon as you
finish your workout,

00:38:48.635 --> 00:38:50.240
you have your biggest
meal of the day.

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:54.710
And when I say placebo
effect, I think

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:57.350
people have the wrong idea of
what the placebo effect is.

00:38:57.350 --> 00:38:59.600
They think that's
just a feeling.

00:38:59.600 --> 00:39:02.630
Placebo effect can actually
change your physiology.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:04.770
People don't realize this.

00:39:04.770 --> 00:39:07.430
There's research showing
that a placebo or the power

00:39:07.430 --> 00:39:10.190
of suggestion is
basically as powerful

00:39:10.190 --> 00:39:11.750
as some pharmaceuticals.

00:39:11.750 --> 00:39:14.907
And one of the great examples
I like to use is-- actually,

00:39:14.907 --> 00:39:17.240
there was a study we just
covered in our research review

00:39:17.240 --> 00:39:22.027
on creatine where
they did four groups--

00:39:22.027 --> 00:39:24.610
not supplemented with creatine,
told they weren't supplemented

00:39:24.610 --> 00:39:26.980
with creatine, not
supplemented, told

00:39:26.980 --> 00:39:28.660
they were supplemented,
supplemented,

00:39:28.660 --> 00:39:32.260
told they weren't,
supplemented, until they were.

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:34.390
Basically, it just matters
what they told them.

00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:34.735
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Really.

00:39:34.735 --> 00:39:35.440
LAYNE NORTON: Oh, yeah.

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:37.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
This is incredible.

00:39:37.190 --> 00:39:38.860
I have to get this
study well so we

00:39:38.860 --> 00:39:41.417
can link to a colleague
of mine at Stanford.

00:39:41.417 --> 00:39:42.500
She's been on the podcast.

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:44.083
I'd love to introduce
you to because I

00:39:44.083 --> 00:39:45.360
think you guys really riff.

00:39:45.360 --> 00:39:47.980
First of all, she was
a former D1 athlete

00:39:47.980 --> 00:39:50.800
and then as runs a lab at
Stanford in psychology.

00:39:50.800 --> 00:39:52.480
This is Alia Crum.

00:39:52.480 --> 00:39:57.400
And she's-- and grew up in
this very athletic, obviously,

00:39:57.400 --> 00:39:58.730
and very, very smart.

00:39:58.730 --> 00:40:01.990
And her laboratory focuses on
these belief/placebo effects

00:40:01.990 --> 00:40:04.510
where if you tell people all
the horrible things that stress

00:40:04.510 --> 00:40:06.550
do to you in terms of
your memory and cognitive

00:40:06.550 --> 00:40:08.665
functioning and then you
give them a memory test,

00:40:08.665 --> 00:40:10.680
they perform well
below baseline.

00:40:10.680 --> 00:40:12.430
If you tell them that
stress sharpens them

00:40:12.430 --> 00:40:14.140
in the short term,
and that adrenaline

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:16.840
is this powerful
molecule that can really

00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:19.630
tune up a number of memory
systems, memory improves.

00:40:19.630 --> 00:40:22.030
And it's remarkable.

00:40:22.030 --> 00:40:24.035
And it's consistent.

00:40:24.035 --> 00:40:26.410
And they've done this for any
number of different things,

00:40:26.410 --> 00:40:28.920
including food
allergies, for instance.

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:30.250
Incredible results.

00:40:30.250 --> 00:40:34.420
In any case, I'm so glad
you're bringing this up.

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:37.420
I take creatine monohydrate,
and I have for years.

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:38.270
5 grams a day.

00:40:38.270 --> 00:40:38.770
I don't--

00:40:38.770 --> 00:40:39.860
LAYNE NORTON: And it's great.

00:40:39.860 --> 00:40:41.193
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And it's great.

00:40:41.193 --> 00:40:42.610
And I believe it's great.

00:40:42.610 --> 00:40:45.370
So is there a compound effect
of believing it's great

00:40:45.370 --> 00:40:47.247
and it actually being great?

00:40:47.247 --> 00:40:48.580
LAYNE NORTON: Not in this study.

00:40:48.580 --> 00:40:50.320
So I think the
thing to point out,

00:40:50.320 --> 00:40:53.570
people will misinterpret that
as creatine doesn't work.

00:40:53.570 --> 00:40:54.820
And that's not what that says.

00:40:54.820 --> 00:40:58.120
What it says is, your
beliefs about what it does

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:01.690
are probably just as
powerful as what it does.

00:41:01.690 --> 00:41:04.120
So they actually did a study--

00:41:04.120 --> 00:41:06.400
and I don't have the
citation, but it was--

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:07.960
I think, within
the last 10 years

00:41:07.960 --> 00:41:09.835
where they told people
they were putting them

00:41:09.835 --> 00:41:10.720
on anabolic steroids.

00:41:10.720 --> 00:41:12.958
And wouldn't you know it,
they had better gains--

00:41:12.958 --> 00:41:15.250
even though they weren't
actually on anabolic steroids,

00:41:15.250 --> 00:41:17.260
they had better gains than
people that they didn't tell

00:41:17.260 --> 00:41:18.025
were anabolic steroids.

00:41:18.025 --> 00:41:18.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Amazing.

00:41:18.310 --> 00:41:20.143
LAYNE NORTON: And that's
like hard outcomes.

00:41:20.143 --> 00:41:22.850
Strength, lean body mass,
those sorts of things.

00:41:22.850 --> 00:41:24.940
So when people say,
well, I wouldn't

00:41:24.940 --> 00:41:27.700
fall for the placebo
effect, it's like,

00:41:27.700 --> 00:41:29.800
you don't have to fall for it.

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:33.460
If you believe it to be
true, the power of belief

00:41:33.460 --> 00:41:36.110
is very, very powerful.

00:41:36.110 --> 00:41:38.943
And as a scientist, I wish,
sometimes, I was ignorant

00:41:38.943 --> 00:41:41.110
so that I could subject
myself to the placebo effect

00:41:41.110 --> 00:41:41.760
more often.

00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:43.177
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:41:43.177 --> 00:41:45.310
LAYNE NORTON: So
getting back to,

00:41:45.310 --> 00:41:48.370
that's just a possible
explanation of, maybe, why.

00:41:48.370 --> 00:41:50.980
And I'm the same way, like
I get done with a workout,

00:41:50.980 --> 00:41:52.855
like a resistance training
session, I'm like,

00:41:52.855 --> 00:41:54.340
I'm ready to eat.

00:41:54.340 --> 00:41:56.770
Now, if you look
at the literature

00:41:56.770 --> 00:41:58.690
overall on exercise
and appetite,

00:41:58.690 --> 00:42:02.500
it's not always
what you'd expect.

00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:05.980
Consistently, it seems to show
that exercise actually has

00:42:05.980 --> 00:42:07.700
an appetite suppressant effect.

00:42:07.700 --> 00:42:11.200
So people don't
tend to compensate

00:42:11.200 --> 00:42:14.920
at least fully for the
amount of movement they do.

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:18.290
And there is some
evidence that--

00:42:18.290 --> 00:42:20.290
you've probably heard
people say, well, exercise

00:42:20.290 --> 00:42:22.570
a really poor weight loss tool.

00:42:22.570 --> 00:42:24.850
If you figure out
how many calories

00:42:24.850 --> 00:42:27.170
you should be burning
from it and you do that,

00:42:27.170 --> 00:42:30.018
you end up getting less weight
loss that you would predict.

00:42:30.018 --> 00:42:31.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I have
a family member who

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:35.800
is perfectly happy to eat less,
but doesn't loathe exercise,

00:42:35.800 --> 00:42:38.290
but dislikes exercise.

00:42:38.290 --> 00:42:40.420
And they're of healthy weight.

00:42:40.420 --> 00:42:43.040
But I'm always encouraging
them to exercise more.

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:46.032
And so this is an ongoing battle
in our sibling relationship.

00:42:46.032 --> 00:42:47.740
LAYNE NORTON: Well,
one thing I would say

00:42:47.740 --> 00:42:50.373
is that, exercise
independent of anything

00:42:50.373 --> 00:42:52.790
that happens with your body
weight, you will be healthier.

00:42:52.790 --> 00:42:55.600
So exercise is one of the
only things that will actually

00:42:55.600 --> 00:42:57.610
improve your
biomarkers of health

00:42:57.610 --> 00:42:59.110
without even losing weight.

00:42:59.110 --> 00:43:01.870
So those-- it will improve
your insulin sensitivity,

00:43:01.870 --> 00:43:03.650
inflammation, all that stuff.

00:43:03.650 --> 00:43:07.360
So everybody out there looking
for a hack to be healthier

00:43:07.360 --> 00:43:08.710
exercises the hack, right?

00:43:08.710 --> 00:43:08.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:43:08.940 --> 00:43:09.523
Crucial point.

00:43:09.523 --> 00:43:14.230
And our mutual friend
Dr. Peter Attia, I think,

00:43:14.230 --> 00:43:16.360
has gone on record
several times now saying

00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:19.120
that of all the things
that one could take--

00:43:19.120 --> 00:43:21.840
NMN, et cetera, metformin.

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:24.340
Regardless of whether or not
one takes those or doesn't take

00:43:24.340 --> 00:43:27.640
those, that the positive
effects on longevity

00:43:27.640 --> 00:43:32.590
by way of biomarkers from
regular exercise is--

00:43:32.590 --> 00:43:35.380
far outweighs all of
those things combined.

00:43:35.380 --> 00:43:37.398
Not that those things
don't necessarily work,

00:43:37.398 --> 00:43:39.190
we're not going through
them in detail now,

00:43:39.190 --> 00:43:42.130
but that exercise
is by far the best

00:43:42.130 --> 00:43:44.295
thing we can do for our
health span and lifespan.

00:43:44.295 --> 00:43:44.710
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:43:44.710 --> 00:43:45.340
Absolutely.

00:43:45.340 --> 00:43:47.270
I 100% agree.

00:43:47.270 --> 00:43:49.720
And when you're talking
about weight loss,

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:52.215
people miss the point
of exercise, I think.

00:43:52.215 --> 00:43:54.340
There's some work that came
out from Herman Pontzer

00:43:54.340 --> 00:43:56.780
as well that basically
showed like, well,

00:43:56.780 --> 00:43:58.960
if you do 100 calories
from exercise,

00:43:58.960 --> 00:44:03.850
you have a 28-calorie reduction
in your basal metabolic rate

00:44:03.850 --> 00:44:04.760
in response to that.

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:07.690
So it's kind of like this
constrained energy expenditure

00:44:07.690 --> 00:44:08.710
model.

00:44:08.710 --> 00:44:11.410
But what I would say is, OK,
well there's still a net of 72.

00:44:11.410 --> 00:44:14.980
So it's still OK,
and the other thing

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:18.130
is, I think the effects
of exercise on weight loss

00:44:18.130 --> 00:44:21.460
are actually more due to
what it does to appetite.

00:44:21.460 --> 00:44:25.030
So if you look at people who
lose weight and keep it off

00:44:25.030 --> 00:44:27.340
for a number of years,
kind of outliers

00:44:27.340 --> 00:44:31.670
because most people don't keep
it off for years, over 70%

00:44:31.670 --> 00:44:33.590
of them engage in
regular exercise.

00:44:33.590 --> 00:44:35.420
Of people who do not
keep weight loss,

00:44:35.420 --> 00:44:39.560
like maintain weight loss, less
than 30% exercise regularly.

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:41.210
So now that's just
a correlation.

00:44:41.210 --> 00:44:43.160
That doesn't necessarily
prove causation.

00:44:43.160 --> 00:44:46.790
But there are some
pretty compelling studies

00:44:46.790 --> 00:44:49.550
showing that exercise
increases your sensitivity

00:44:49.550 --> 00:44:51.110
to satiety signals.

00:44:51.110 --> 00:44:53.510
So basically, you can have
the same satiety signals,

00:44:53.510 --> 00:44:56.570
but you're more sensitive
to them when you exercise.

00:44:56.570 --> 00:44:58.910
And there's actually
a really classic study

00:44:58.910 --> 00:45:01.790
from the 1950s in
Bengali workers

00:45:01.790 --> 00:45:06.850
where they looked at, basically,
four different quadrants

00:45:06.850 --> 00:45:07.760
of activity.

00:45:07.760 --> 00:45:11.680
So you had sedentary, lightly
active, moderately active,

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:13.240
heavily active.

00:45:13.240 --> 00:45:15.950
Basically, based on
their job choice.

00:45:15.950 --> 00:45:18.350
And they didn't have
an intervention.

00:45:18.350 --> 00:45:21.590
They just wanted to track
them and see how many calories

00:45:21.590 --> 00:45:22.910
did they actually eat.

00:45:22.910 --> 00:45:25.170
So it was like a J-shaped curve.

00:45:25.170 --> 00:45:28.490
So the sedentary
actually ate more food

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:31.620
than the lightly active
or moderately active.

00:45:31.620 --> 00:45:34.590
But from lightly active
to heavily active,

00:45:34.590 --> 00:45:37.710
they almost perfectly
compensated how many calories

00:45:37.710 --> 00:45:39.210
they should be eating.

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:42.520
So to me, that suggests,
when you become active,

00:45:42.520 --> 00:45:45.667
you can actually regulate your
appetite appropriately or much

00:45:45.667 --> 00:45:47.500
more appropriately than
if you're sedentary.

00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:48.875
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And do you think

00:45:48.875 --> 00:45:50.700
this has to do with
changes in the brain--

00:45:50.700 --> 00:45:52.830
brain centers that
respond to satiety signals

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:57.030
from the periphery,
and/or do you

00:45:57.030 --> 00:46:00.533
think it has to do with changes
in blood sugar regulation?

00:46:00.533 --> 00:46:02.700
What I was taught, and I
don't know if this is still

00:46:02.700 --> 00:46:05.010
considered true, is that
spikes in blood sugar

00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:07.140
will trigger a desire to
eat more even though it's

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:09.390
kind of exactly the opposite
of what you need when you

00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:10.710
have a spike in blood sugar.

00:46:10.710 --> 00:46:11.760
And there's this
kind of-- and we'll

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:14.052
get into this when we talk
about artificial sweeteners.

00:46:14.052 --> 00:46:15.160
This is the idea in mind.

00:46:15.160 --> 00:46:18.870
I think I adopted, perhaps,
falsely that you eat something

00:46:18.870 --> 00:46:20.550
that's sweeter, that
tastes really good,

00:46:20.550 --> 00:46:23.970
and you are suddenly on the
train of wanting to eat more.

00:46:23.970 --> 00:46:26.490
And I could imagine
how exercise,

00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:29.850
if it is increasing
the satiety signals,

00:46:29.850 --> 00:46:32.088
could be working a
number of different ways.

00:46:32.088 --> 00:46:32.880
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:34.170
I think it's--

00:46:34.170 --> 00:46:38.250
I think the effect is probably
mostly at the brain level.

00:46:38.250 --> 00:46:39.990
The effects on blood sugar--

00:46:43.560 --> 00:46:47.300
the research out there is not
very compelling for blood sugar

00:46:47.300 --> 00:46:49.070
driving appetite.

00:46:49.070 --> 00:46:52.880
Now, if you become hypoglycemic,
yes, you'll get hungry,

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:56.000
but it's a different kind of
hunger than your normal like,

00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:58.490
I feel kind of empty and
my stomach's growling.

00:46:58.490 --> 00:47:00.620
Like those are--
they can go together,

00:47:00.620 --> 00:47:04.110
but usually like the
hypoglycemia is like, I am hot,

00:47:04.110 --> 00:47:05.810
I feel like I'm
going to pass out.

00:47:05.810 --> 00:47:08.750
You want to eat something not
because your stomach's growling

00:47:08.750 --> 00:47:10.493
but because that you
just need some fuel.

00:47:10.493 --> 00:47:12.785
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's like
you're getting pulled under.

00:47:12.785 --> 00:47:13.747
LAYNE NORTON: Oh, yeah.

00:47:13.747 --> 00:47:14.215
Absolutely.

00:47:14.215 --> 00:47:15.757
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I've been there when

00:47:15.757 --> 00:47:17.330
I've done the longer
fasts, something

00:47:17.330 --> 00:47:20.093
I don't do anymore and
drink a lot of black coffee.

00:47:20.093 --> 00:47:22.010
There was probably an
electrolyte effect there

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:25.460
because coffee as you excrete
sodium and other electrolytes.

00:47:25.460 --> 00:47:27.807
And then just feeling
like, I needed something.

00:47:27.807 --> 00:47:29.390
This whole thing
like I need something

00:47:29.390 --> 00:47:30.995
that's kind of desperation.

00:47:30.995 --> 00:47:33.452
I never want to be
back here again.

00:47:33.452 --> 00:47:34.910
Hypoglycemia is
very uncomfortable.

00:47:34.910 --> 00:47:36.035
LAYNE NORTON: It's not fun.

00:47:36.035 --> 00:47:43.430
So again, then when they look
at actual randomized controlled

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:47.540
trials of implementing
some exercise where

00:47:47.540 --> 00:47:51.060
they're pretty
controlled environment,

00:47:51.060 --> 00:47:53.085
they typically see people--

00:47:53.085 --> 00:47:56.460
if anything, they eat less
as opposed to eating more.

00:47:56.460 --> 00:47:59.880
Now, some people, again,
studies report averages.

00:47:59.880 --> 00:48:01.450
And there's individual
data points.

00:48:01.450 --> 00:48:03.960
So there are some people who
at least anecdotally report

00:48:03.960 --> 00:48:06.030
that exercise makes
them more hungry.

00:48:06.030 --> 00:48:07.980
That's completely valid.

00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:09.842
It could be their
beliefs around it,

00:48:09.842 --> 00:48:11.550
it could be a number
of different things,

00:48:11.550 --> 00:48:13.342
but it's important to
understand that there

00:48:13.342 --> 00:48:14.500
is individual variability.

00:48:14.500 --> 00:48:17.040
And I think one of
the things that I've

00:48:17.040 --> 00:48:20.410
learned to appreciate
more is not

00:48:20.410 --> 00:48:23.887
trying to separate
psychology and physiology.

00:48:23.887 --> 00:48:26.470
We do this a lot and say, well,
I want to know the physiology,

00:48:26.470 --> 00:48:28.137
I don't care about
the psychology of it.

00:48:28.137 --> 00:48:32.830
And now I'm kind of appreciating
more, psychology is physiology.

00:48:32.830 --> 00:48:34.510
Like with most things
now, we have kind

00:48:34.510 --> 00:48:37.078
of the biopsychosocial model.

00:48:37.078 --> 00:48:38.620
And I'll give you
an example of this.

00:48:38.620 --> 00:48:42.580
A lot of people get really
caught up with appetite.

00:48:42.580 --> 00:48:46.770
And if we could just
suppress people's appetite,

00:48:46.770 --> 00:48:48.690
that's part of it.

00:48:48.690 --> 00:48:51.780
But people don't just eat
because they're hungry,

00:48:51.780 --> 00:48:55.000
they eat for a lot of different
reasons, social reasons,

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:55.727
especially.

00:48:55.727 --> 00:48:57.810
So can you remember the
last social event you ever

00:48:57.810 --> 00:48:58.755
went to that didn't have food?

00:48:58.755 --> 00:48:59.588
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

00:48:59.588 --> 00:49:00.720
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

00:49:00.720 --> 00:49:03.000
If you look at dinner
plates from the 1800s,

00:49:03.000 --> 00:49:04.255
they're about this big.

00:49:04.255 --> 00:49:05.505
Now how big are dinner plates?

00:49:05.505 --> 00:49:06.937
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
The whole buffet.

00:49:06.937 --> 00:49:07.770
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

00:49:07.770 --> 00:49:10.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:49:10.110 --> 00:49:12.750
LAYNE NORTON: There's
situational cues.

00:49:12.750 --> 00:49:14.160
You're sitting down to watch TV.

00:49:14.160 --> 00:49:16.255
Oh, grab some popcorn,
grab some snack, whatever.

00:49:16.255 --> 00:49:17.880
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
even see this with--

00:49:17.880 --> 00:49:19.680
you know, how one person
will pick up the phone

00:49:19.680 --> 00:49:20.980
and then everyone
picks up their phone.

00:49:20.980 --> 00:49:22.680
I think there's a
similar effect with food.

00:49:22.680 --> 00:49:23.550
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:24.570
And same thing, right?

00:49:24.570 --> 00:49:29.530
Like how many times have
we either done it ourselves

00:49:29.530 --> 00:49:32.470
or have been experienced
people saying, oh, you

00:49:32.470 --> 00:49:35.170
should have something--
you should have alcohol,

00:49:35.170 --> 00:49:35.920
especially, right?

00:49:35.920 --> 00:49:39.640
People-- I was hanging out
with somebody last night

00:49:39.640 --> 00:49:43.480
and I had a beer and
they just had a water.

00:49:43.480 --> 00:49:47.530
And I'm like, I feel no need
to try and convince them

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:48.550
to do that with me.

00:49:48.550 --> 00:49:49.480
You know what I mean?

00:49:49.480 --> 00:49:54.610
But as humans,
we're hurt animals.

00:49:54.610 --> 00:49:56.380
We don't want to be
doing something out

00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:57.530
in isolation on our own.

00:49:57.530 --> 00:50:02.030
Now, this is a very tenuous,
I guess, belief of mine.

00:50:02.030 --> 00:50:07.340
But doing things
alone in isolation

00:50:07.340 --> 00:50:12.570
during ancestral
times, that's going

00:50:12.570 --> 00:50:14.095
to set off your alarm system.

00:50:14.095 --> 00:50:15.720
Because if you don't
have other people,

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:17.670
you can't protect yourself.

00:50:17.670 --> 00:50:21.000
So typically, things were
done together in groups.

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:23.010
And I think that's a
lot of the reason why

00:50:23.010 --> 00:50:27.780
we tend to be just tribal in
nature about a lot of things.

00:50:27.780 --> 00:50:32.040
So the whole point to that
is, on the list of reasons

00:50:32.040 --> 00:50:33.782
why people eat, I
mean, I've gotten

00:50:33.782 --> 00:50:36.240
to the point where I think that
hunger is actually not even

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:37.710
the main reason people eat.

00:50:37.710 --> 00:50:40.268
Stress, lack of sleep--

00:50:40.268 --> 00:50:41.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Boredom.

00:50:41.310 --> 00:50:41.940
LAYNE NORTON: --boredom.

00:50:41.940 --> 00:50:42.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:50:42.570 --> 00:50:43.720
LAYNE NORTON: Absolutely.

00:50:43.720 --> 00:50:46.500
So unless we can
do something that

00:50:46.500 --> 00:50:50.220
addresses all those
things, there's

00:50:50.220 --> 00:50:51.990
a line from a review paper--

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:53.505
this review paper
came out in 2011

00:50:53.505 --> 00:50:55.170
is by a researcher
named MacLean.

00:50:55.170 --> 00:50:58.230
And it's the best review
paper I've ever read.

00:50:58.230 --> 00:51:00.970
It was called, biology's
response to dieting--

00:51:00.970 --> 00:51:02.370
the impetus for weight regain.

00:51:02.370 --> 00:51:04.470
And basically went
through all the mechanisms

00:51:04.470 --> 00:51:07.710
of these adaptations that
happen during fat loss diets

00:51:07.710 --> 00:51:11.580
and how biology's response
is to try to drive you back

00:51:11.580 --> 00:51:13.590
to your previous.

00:51:13.590 --> 00:51:15.060
And I'm going to
butcher the quote,

00:51:15.060 --> 00:51:18.390
but at the end of the
study he said, basically,

00:51:18.390 --> 00:51:23.910
the body's systems are
comprehensive, redundant,

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:27.120
and well-focused on restoring
depleted energy reserves.

00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:34.190
And any attempt or any
strategy for weight loss

00:51:34.190 --> 00:51:38.840
that doesn't attempt to
address a broad spectrum

00:51:38.840 --> 00:51:41.310
of these things
is going to fail.

00:51:41.310 --> 00:51:44.840
And so that's why when people
say, well, just do low carb,

00:51:44.840 --> 00:51:46.300
you won't be hungry.

00:51:46.300 --> 00:51:49.220
Look, people don't just
eat because they're hungry.

00:51:49.220 --> 00:51:53.300
So I think really like
trying to get outside the box

00:51:53.300 --> 00:51:54.650
and think about these things.

00:51:54.650 --> 00:51:57.080
And especially, when you
read some of the literature,

00:51:57.080 --> 00:52:00.650
I recently read a systematic
review of successful weight

00:52:00.650 --> 00:52:03.988
loss maintainers, which I
thought was really interesting.

00:52:03.988 --> 00:52:06.530
So they took people who had lost
a significant amount of body

00:52:06.530 --> 00:52:09.222
weight and kept it off for,
I think, it was three years.

00:52:09.222 --> 00:52:11.180
And it basically asked
them questions and tried

00:52:11.180 --> 00:52:12.295
to identify commonalities.

00:52:12.295 --> 00:52:13.670
And there were
some things that I

00:52:13.670 --> 00:52:18.440
expected like cognitive
restraint, self monitoring,

00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:20.780
exercise.

00:52:20.780 --> 00:52:22.280
And then one of the
things they said

00:52:22.280 --> 00:52:25.640
that I found really fascinating
was pretty ubiquitous

00:52:25.640 --> 00:52:26.300
between people.

00:52:26.300 --> 00:52:30.520
They said, I had to
develop a new identity.

00:52:30.520 --> 00:52:32.647
So are you familiar
with Ethan Suplee?

00:52:32.647 --> 00:52:33.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:35.710
LAYNE NORTON: So
Ethan is an actor.

00:52:35.710 --> 00:52:38.933
He's been in like remember the
Titans and American History X.

00:52:38.933 --> 00:52:41.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I certainly
saw American History X.

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:41.892
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

00:52:41.892 --> 00:52:43.560
So he was very large.

00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:45.150
He was like 550 pounds.

00:52:45.150 --> 00:52:47.547
And now he's like
230 and jacked.

00:52:47.547 --> 00:52:49.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
5-- he was how many--

00:52:49.380 --> 00:52:50.500
LAYNE NORTON: 550 pounds.

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:52.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Wow.

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:55.350
LAYNE NORTON: And he
has-- whenever he puts up

00:52:55.350 --> 00:52:58.950
post on his Instagram of
him training, it'll say,

00:52:58.950 --> 00:53:00.900
I killed my clone today.

00:53:00.900 --> 00:53:05.595
And I asked him, is this
what you're talking about?

00:53:05.595 --> 00:53:06.803
Creating a new identity.

00:53:06.803 --> 00:53:08.970
And he said, this is exactly
what I'm talking about.

00:53:08.970 --> 00:53:10.920
Because I had to kill who I was.

00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:12.660
Because there was
no way I was going

00:53:12.660 --> 00:53:15.750
to be able to make long
term changes if I just

00:53:15.750 --> 00:53:17.520
didn't become a new person.

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:20.220
Because I mean, and
addicts talk about this.

00:53:20.220 --> 00:53:22.290
Like people who are alcoholics.

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:23.672
They had to get new friends.

00:53:23.672 --> 00:53:25.380
They had to hang out
at different places,

00:53:25.380 --> 00:53:27.900
because their entire
life had been set up

00:53:27.900 --> 00:53:30.120
around this lifestyle
for alcohol.

00:53:30.120 --> 00:53:34.530
And I would actually argue
that eating disorders

00:53:34.530 --> 00:53:39.540
or disordered eating patterns
is much harder to break

00:53:39.540 --> 00:53:41.130
than other forms of addiction.

00:53:41.130 --> 00:53:43.330
And you think about
food addiction.

00:53:43.330 --> 00:53:51.030
Well, in some ways, bulimia and
anorexia are still addictions.

00:53:51.030 --> 00:53:52.758
You can't stop eating.

00:53:52.758 --> 00:53:55.050
Like if you're alcoholic,
you can abstain from alcohol.

00:53:55.050 --> 00:53:56.880
If you become addicted
to say, cocaine,

00:53:56.880 --> 00:53:59.480
you can abstain from that.

00:53:59.480 --> 00:54:01.290
You can never abstain from food.

00:54:01.290 --> 00:54:04.400
And so now imagine
telling a gambling addict,

00:54:04.400 --> 00:54:08.330
well, you've got to play this
slot a couple of times a day

00:54:08.330 --> 00:54:09.500
but no more.

00:54:09.500 --> 00:54:12.570
Like that's really challenging.

00:54:12.570 --> 00:54:14.330
So yeah.

00:54:14.330 --> 00:54:16.940
I just-- like all
this stuff, it's

00:54:16.940 --> 00:54:19.612
so important to be comprehensive
with how we treat these things.

00:54:19.612 --> 00:54:20.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

00:54:20.570 --> 00:54:22.693
These are incredibly
important points.

00:54:22.693 --> 00:54:24.110
And to my knowledge,
I don't think

00:54:24.110 --> 00:54:27.110
anyone has really described
it in a cohesive way the way

00:54:27.110 --> 00:54:28.440
that you're doing here.

00:54:28.440 --> 00:54:30.320
So important for people
to understand this

00:54:30.320 --> 00:54:32.120
because obviously,
as a neuroscientist,

00:54:32.120 --> 00:54:35.330
I think the nervous system
is creating our thoughts,

00:54:35.330 --> 00:54:37.820
our thoughts and feelings
are related to psychology,

00:54:37.820 --> 00:54:40.370
and therefore, of course, our
physiology and our psychology

00:54:40.370 --> 00:54:41.468
are one and the same.

00:54:41.468 --> 00:54:42.260
It's bidirectional.

00:54:42.260 --> 00:54:45.660
Now, nowadays, there's a lot
of interest in brain, body,

00:54:45.660 --> 00:54:47.060
and particular gut--

00:54:47.060 --> 00:54:49.775
brain axis and we
can talk about that.

00:54:49.775 --> 00:54:51.932
But I really
appreciate that you're

00:54:51.932 --> 00:54:54.140
spelling out how there are
these different variables.

00:54:54.140 --> 00:54:56.390
Each one can account for a
number of different things.

00:54:56.390 --> 00:54:59.778
Exercise clearly has a
remarkably potent effect--

00:54:59.778 --> 00:55:01.820
both during the exercise
in terms of caloric burn

00:55:01.820 --> 00:55:03.260
and overall health
and biomarkers.

00:55:03.260 --> 00:55:05.990
And then this is
wonderful to learn

00:55:05.990 --> 00:55:09.950
that it can increase the
sensitivity to satiety signals.

00:55:09.950 --> 00:55:12.230
I think that makes--
at least in my mind,

00:55:12.230 --> 00:55:14.750
places very high on the
list of things that people

00:55:14.750 --> 00:55:16.020
should absolutely do.

00:55:16.020 --> 00:55:18.920
But there are other factors too.

00:55:18.920 --> 00:55:22.647
And the identity
piece is fascinating.

00:55:22.647 --> 00:55:24.980
It reminds me also-- your
story reminds me also of David

00:55:24.980 --> 00:55:26.740
Goggins who is--

00:55:26.740 --> 00:55:29.780
he talks about his former
very overweight self

00:55:29.780 --> 00:55:32.630
almost as if it was
a different person.

00:55:32.630 --> 00:55:35.540
And he uses language that
I'm not going to use here.

00:55:35.540 --> 00:55:36.500
But you know what?

00:55:36.500 --> 00:55:39.110
I've met David, know David
a bit, and his every bit

00:55:39.110 --> 00:55:41.750
is intense and driven as
and a remarkable human

00:55:41.750 --> 00:55:44.060
being as he appears
to be online.

00:55:44.060 --> 00:55:46.070
He is that guy.

00:55:46.070 --> 00:55:49.100
But it does seem like he
had to more or less kill off

00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:52.590
a former version of himself and
continues to do that every day.

00:55:52.590 --> 00:55:56.756
And I think what your point
about this other fellow who

00:55:56.756 --> 00:56:00.080
does it through a similar
process, the word "today"

00:56:00.080 --> 00:56:01.250
seems to really matter.

00:56:01.250 --> 00:56:04.250
It's not like you defeat this
former version of yourself

00:56:04.250 --> 00:56:06.740
and then that person
is buried and gone,

00:56:06.740 --> 00:56:08.990
you said, you know, I
killed my clone today,

00:56:08.990 --> 00:56:11.390
and that's the way that
David talks about it also.

00:56:11.390 --> 00:56:13.260
So this is a daily process.

00:56:13.260 --> 00:56:17.402
And I think this is not just a
small detail in time together

00:56:17.402 --> 00:56:18.110
all these things.

00:56:18.110 --> 00:56:20.060
I think that what
you are describing

00:56:20.060 --> 00:56:21.945
is fundamental,
because we can pull

00:56:21.945 --> 00:56:23.570
on each one of these
variables and talk

00:56:23.570 --> 00:56:24.720
about each one of them.

00:56:24.720 --> 00:56:27.020
But at the end of the day,
we are a cohesive whole

00:56:27.020 --> 00:56:29.970
as an individual.

00:56:29.970 --> 00:56:30.470
Sorry.

00:56:30.470 --> 00:56:31.387
You were about to say.

00:56:31.387 --> 00:56:34.200
LAYNE NORTON: That gets actually
into one of my favorite topics,

00:56:34.200 --> 00:56:38.840
which is, why do we have such
a hard time with losing weight

00:56:38.840 --> 00:56:39.980
but more so keeping it off?

00:56:39.980 --> 00:56:44.210
Because of obese people, six
out of every seven obese people

00:56:44.210 --> 00:56:47.040
will lose a significant amount
of body weight in their life.

00:56:47.040 --> 00:56:49.340
So why do we still have
an obesity problem?

00:56:49.340 --> 00:56:50.870
They don't keep it off.

00:56:50.870 --> 00:56:52.550
Why don't they keep it off?

00:56:52.550 --> 00:56:55.310
When you look at the
research, basically,

00:56:55.310 --> 00:56:59.070
what it suggests is
because people think about,

00:56:59.070 --> 00:57:01.560
I am going to do a diet, and
I'm going to lose this weight,

00:57:01.560 --> 00:57:05.700
and they do not give any thought
to what happens afterwards.

00:57:05.700 --> 00:57:09.700
It's like think about if you
have some chronic disease

00:57:09.700 --> 00:57:11.230
or a diabetic.

00:57:11.230 --> 00:57:13.810
You can't just take insulin
once and that's it, right?

00:57:13.810 --> 00:57:16.540
You've got to take it
continuously, otherwise,

00:57:16.540 --> 00:57:19.420
you're going to have problems.

00:57:19.420 --> 00:57:27.020
If you do a diet and you
lose 30 pounds, fantastic.

00:57:27.020 --> 00:57:31.240
But if you then just go
back to all your old habits,

00:57:31.240 --> 00:57:34.750
you're going to go back to
where you were, if not more.

00:57:34.750 --> 00:57:37.000
You can't create a new
version of yourself

00:57:37.000 --> 00:57:39.880
while dragging your old habits
and behaviors behind you.

00:57:39.880 --> 00:57:42.310
So what I'll tell people is--

00:57:42.310 --> 00:57:45.250
because people say, well,
I'm doing a carnivore diet

00:57:45.250 --> 00:57:47.140
or I'm doing this
diet or that diet.

00:57:47.140 --> 00:57:49.390
And I'll say that's fine.

00:57:49.390 --> 00:57:52.100
Do you see yourself doing that
for the rest of your life?

00:57:52.100 --> 00:57:53.883
And if the answer is
yes, if you really

00:57:53.883 --> 00:57:56.050
believe that that's going
to be sustainable for you,

00:57:56.050 --> 00:57:59.620
and plenty of people, low carb,
intermittent fasting, whatever,

00:57:59.620 --> 00:58:01.510
they say, I felt easy.

00:58:01.510 --> 00:58:03.340
I could do this forever.

00:58:03.340 --> 00:58:05.620
Great.

00:58:05.620 --> 00:58:08.560
If you're going to lose weight,
you have to invoke some form

00:58:08.560 --> 00:58:11.500
of restriction-- whether it is
a nutrient restriction like low

00:58:11.500 --> 00:58:14.440
carb, low fat, a
time restriction--

00:58:14.440 --> 00:58:17.290
intermittent fasting any form
of time restricted eating,

00:58:17.290 --> 00:58:19.300
or calorie restriction--

00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:21.040
tracking macros, whatever.

00:58:21.040 --> 00:58:24.490
So you get to pick the
form of restriction.

00:58:24.490 --> 00:58:26.860
So pick the form
of restriction that

00:58:26.860 --> 00:58:30.250
feels the least restrictive
to you as an individual,

00:58:30.250 --> 00:58:33.070
and also do not
assume that it will

00:58:33.070 --> 00:58:36.190
feel the same for everybody else
because I made this mistake.

00:58:36.190 --> 00:58:38.540
Whereas it's like,
I track things.

00:58:38.540 --> 00:58:40.900
And so I allow myself to
eat a variety of foods,

00:58:40.900 --> 00:58:43.090
I allow myself to
eat some fun foods.

00:58:43.090 --> 00:58:46.405
But I track everything, and
I'm able to modify my body

00:58:46.405 --> 00:58:48.280
composition and be in
good health doing that.

00:58:48.280 --> 00:58:51.530
Now, doesn't feel hard for me.

00:58:51.530 --> 00:58:53.530
Part of it, I've just
been doing it for so long.

00:58:53.530 --> 00:58:55.630
But to other people,
that's very stressful.

00:58:55.630 --> 00:58:56.463
They don't want to--

00:58:56.463 --> 00:58:59.530
they say, well, I'd rather
just not eat for 16 hours.

00:58:59.530 --> 00:59:02.920
If that feels easy for them,
do that because the one thing

00:59:02.920 --> 00:59:06.952
that-- there was a couple of
meta analysis on popular diets.

00:59:06.952 --> 00:59:08.410
And basically, what
they showed was

00:59:08.410 --> 00:59:11.800
they were all equally terrible
for long term weight loss.

00:59:11.800 --> 00:59:16.900
But when they stratified them
by adherence-- and none of them

00:59:16.900 --> 00:59:18.980
were better for
adherence overall.

00:59:18.980 --> 00:59:20.560
But when they
stratified people just

00:59:20.560 --> 00:59:22.750
according from lowest
adherence to best adherence,

00:59:22.750 --> 00:59:25.640
there it was a linear
effect on weight loss.

00:59:25.640 --> 00:59:28.660
So really what it
says is, what is

00:59:28.660 --> 00:59:31.210
the diet that's going to be
easiest for you to adhere to

00:59:31.210 --> 00:59:34.930
in the long term and you
should probably do that?

00:59:34.930 --> 00:59:38.440
And people-- again, this is
where I step back and take

00:59:38.440 --> 00:59:40.060
the 10,000-foot view.

00:59:40.060 --> 00:59:41.860
Somebody will say,
well, I'm going

00:59:41.860 --> 00:59:45.063
to do ketogenic because I want
to increase my fat oxidation

00:59:45.063 --> 00:59:45.980
and I want to do this.

00:59:45.980 --> 00:59:47.260
And they're talking about
all these mechanisms

00:59:47.260 --> 00:59:47.890
and everything.

00:59:47.890 --> 00:59:49.870
And that's great.

00:59:49.870 --> 00:59:52.290
Can you do it for the
rest of your life?

00:59:52.290 --> 00:59:54.530
Is this going to be something
sustainable for you?

00:59:54.530 --> 00:59:57.040
And if the answer
is, no, you probably

00:59:57.040 --> 01:00:00.535
need to rethink what your
approach is going to be.

01:00:00.535 --> 01:00:04.390
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Incredibly
important message.

01:00:04.390 --> 01:00:06.040
Basically, that.

01:00:06.040 --> 01:00:08.560
If I could highlight-- if there
was a version of highlight

01:00:08.560 --> 01:00:11.800
or boldface and underline
in the podcast space,

01:00:11.800 --> 01:00:15.730
I would highlight-- boldface and
underline what you just said.

01:00:15.730 --> 01:00:17.470
And for those of
you that heard it,

01:00:17.470 --> 01:00:19.240
listen to it twice,
and then go forward

01:00:19.240 --> 01:00:20.960
because it's absolutely key.

01:00:20.960 --> 01:00:24.670
I think it also explains a lot
of the so-called controversy

01:00:24.670 --> 01:00:25.660
that exists out there.

01:00:25.660 --> 01:00:28.550
I think it also crosses over
with the placebo effect.

01:00:28.550 --> 01:00:32.200
I almost want to say,
pick the nutrition plan

01:00:32.200 --> 01:00:35.900
that you think you can stick
to for a long period of time,

01:00:35.900 --> 01:00:36.790
ideally forever.

01:00:36.790 --> 01:00:39.550
And pick your placebo
too, because there

01:00:39.550 --> 01:00:41.170
is a lot of placebo
woven into each

01:00:41.170 --> 01:00:45.460
and every one of these things--
intermittent fasting, keto.

01:00:45.460 --> 01:00:49.045
Probably even vegan versus
omnivore versus carnivore.

01:00:49.045 --> 01:00:52.150
LAYNE NORTON: Well, they even
talk about the diet-- honeymoon

01:00:52.150 --> 01:00:55.210
period where you go into a
diet and you're all fired up

01:00:55.210 --> 01:00:56.860
about it and like
you're very adherent,

01:00:56.860 --> 01:00:59.350
and then what happens,
with every single diet

01:00:59.350 --> 01:01:01.180
without exception
in research studies

01:01:01.180 --> 01:01:03.340
is once you get past few
months, adherence just

01:01:03.340 --> 01:01:05.130
starts waiting and going off.

01:01:05.130 --> 01:01:06.922
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Here
we are really talking

01:01:06.922 --> 01:01:09.210
about a form of relationship.

01:01:09.210 --> 01:01:11.050
I'm not saying that
to be tongue in cheek.

01:01:11.050 --> 01:01:13.780
Actually, we had a guest early
on in the podcast, Dr. Karl

01:01:13.780 --> 01:01:18.220
Deisseroth, he's a psychiatrist
and a bioengineer at Stanford.

01:01:18.220 --> 01:01:19.300
Tremendously successful.

01:01:19.300 --> 01:01:20.950
Alaska award winner, et cetera.

01:01:20.950 --> 01:01:25.780
And he talked about love as a
sort of an interesting aspect

01:01:25.780 --> 01:01:28.690
of our psychology
where it's a story

01:01:28.690 --> 01:01:31.120
that you co-create with
somebody but that you live

01:01:31.120 --> 01:01:32.950
into the future of that story.

01:01:32.950 --> 01:01:35.120
When you pair up
with somebody that

01:01:35.120 --> 01:01:37.780
was referring to romantic
love, that there's

01:01:37.780 --> 01:01:41.320
this sort of mutual agreement
to create this idea that you're

01:01:41.320 --> 01:01:42.250
going to live into.

01:01:42.250 --> 01:01:44.333
So it's not just about how
you feel in the moment,

01:01:44.333 --> 01:01:46.750
it's also that you project
into the future quite a lot.

01:01:46.750 --> 01:01:49.810
I'm seeing a lot of
parallels with a highly

01:01:49.810 --> 01:01:52.390
functional and effective diet.

01:01:52.390 --> 01:01:53.140
And I love it.

01:01:53.140 --> 01:01:55.508
I'm not setting this
parallel up artificially,

01:01:55.508 --> 01:01:57.550
I'm setting up because I
think that ultimately it

01:01:57.550 --> 01:01:59.620
boils down to what you
said earlier, which

01:01:59.620 --> 01:02:01.870
is that the brain and our
decisions about what we

01:02:01.870 --> 01:02:05.440
are going to stick to are
tremendously powerful.

01:02:05.440 --> 01:02:09.130
LAYNE NORTON: I think one thing
I will say is keep in mind,

01:02:09.130 --> 01:02:12.490
when you look at the research
data, the meta analyzes on say,

01:02:12.490 --> 01:02:15.430
time-restricted eating versus
none, when calories are

01:02:15.430 --> 01:02:17.800
equated, doesn't seem to
be a difference in weight

01:02:17.800 --> 01:02:20.740
loss, fat loss, and most
biomarkers of health.

01:02:20.740 --> 01:02:23.140
Same thing for low
carb versus low fat.

01:02:23.140 --> 01:02:24.850
Fewer quick calories
and protein.

01:02:24.850 --> 01:02:28.210
There was a meta analysis done
by Kevin Hall back in 2017

01:02:28.210 --> 01:02:32.020
where they looked at the-- and
again, actual loss of body fat.

01:02:32.020 --> 01:02:34.712
And another important
point was, I think

01:02:34.712 --> 01:02:35.920
there was 22 studies in this.

01:02:35.920 --> 01:02:39.130
But all of them provided
food to the participants

01:02:39.130 --> 01:02:42.172
That's important because
that ensures that adherence

01:02:42.172 --> 01:02:43.630
can be much higher
in those studies

01:02:43.630 --> 01:02:45.940
whereas various free
living studies, sometimes,

01:02:45.940 --> 01:02:47.190
you can see funky results.

01:02:47.190 --> 01:02:49.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: People
are sneaking food

01:02:49.720 --> 01:02:51.115
or they're just not really--

01:02:51.115 --> 01:02:52.210
LAYNE NORTON: It's
very difficult--

01:02:52.210 --> 01:02:52.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--eating the way

01:02:52.480 --> 01:02:54.610
that the study would
ideally have them eat.

01:02:54.610 --> 01:02:56.170
LAYNE NORTON:
Unless the person is

01:02:56.170 --> 01:02:58.180
getting like continuous
support-- like,

01:02:58.180 --> 01:02:59.920
studies where they
have a dietician talk

01:02:59.920 --> 01:03:02.170
to people like every
week tend to actually

01:03:02.170 --> 01:03:03.410
have pretty good adherence.

01:03:03.410 --> 01:03:05.410
I mean, that's expensive
to have done the study.

01:03:05.410 --> 01:03:08.320
And again, like what limits
studies, money, money,

01:03:08.320 --> 01:03:10.990
and money.

01:03:10.990 --> 01:03:15.520
But the low carb versus low
fat, protein, and calories

01:03:15.520 --> 01:03:18.617
are equated, basically,
no difference in fat loss.

01:03:18.617 --> 01:03:20.200
Now, some people get
upset about this.

01:03:20.200 --> 01:03:21.400
But it's like--
what to me, that's

01:03:21.400 --> 01:03:24.220
like-- this is great because you
get to pick the tool you want.

01:03:24.220 --> 01:03:25.710
The one tool, it
doesn't seem to be

01:03:25.710 --> 01:03:26.960
that much better than another.

01:03:26.960 --> 01:03:28.875
So pick the one
that works for you.

01:03:28.875 --> 01:03:30.250
Whatever lever
you've got a pull,

01:03:30.250 --> 01:03:31.917
you've got a bunch
of different options.

01:03:31.917 --> 01:03:34.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You
mentioned picking something

01:03:34.710 --> 01:03:36.550
that you can stick to
for a period of time.

01:03:36.550 --> 01:03:39.200
Is there ever a case
for someone saying,

01:03:39.200 --> 01:03:43.570
look, I like to eat low carb
or even keto for six months

01:03:43.570 --> 01:03:46.060
and then switch to a
more standard omnivore

01:03:46.060 --> 01:03:49.160
caloric maintenance type
diet and then switch back?

01:03:49.160 --> 01:03:50.920
Is there any downside
to doing that

01:03:50.920 --> 01:03:53.277
for sake of health or
weight loss over time,

01:03:53.277 --> 01:03:54.610
or weight maintenance over time?

01:03:54.610 --> 01:03:57.140
Because I realize not everyone
is trying to lose weight.

01:03:57.140 --> 01:03:58.640
And I definitely
want to talk about,

01:03:58.640 --> 01:04:00.790
at some point, how to
eat to maintain weight.

01:04:00.790 --> 01:04:03.123
Because I think there are
a significant fraction

01:04:03.123 --> 01:04:05.040
of people out there who
are trying to do that.

01:04:05.040 --> 01:04:07.240
Yeah.

01:04:07.240 --> 01:04:10.030
Is there any downside
to being a dabbler?

01:04:10.030 --> 01:04:12.340
Keto for a few months
and then omnivore

01:04:12.340 --> 01:04:14.023
for a few months, et cetera?

01:04:14.023 --> 01:04:16.190
LAYNE NORTON: I think that's
actually a great thing,

01:04:16.190 --> 01:04:17.770
especially to like,
maybe, find what

01:04:17.770 --> 01:04:20.920
you feel is easiest for you.

01:04:20.920 --> 01:04:24.760
But in terms of as a
strategy, I mean, I guess,

01:04:24.760 --> 01:04:27.490
some people just might
get into dopamine

01:04:27.490 --> 01:04:29.920
but like, oh, change
and get something new.

01:04:29.920 --> 01:04:31.980
And you feel a little bit
more positive about it.

01:04:31.980 --> 01:04:32.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
do partner model.

01:04:32.650 --> 01:04:32.920
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:04:32.920 --> 01:04:33.130
Yeah.

01:04:33.130 --> 01:04:33.730
Exactly.

01:04:33.730 --> 01:04:37.630
So I don't think it's how I
would usually set things up

01:04:37.630 --> 01:04:39.980
initially for somebody,
but if somebody said, hey,

01:04:39.980 --> 01:04:42.460
I just like to have some
variety and change it up,

01:04:42.460 --> 01:04:44.605
as long as they're still like--

01:04:44.605 --> 01:04:47.050
their behaviors and they're
doing portion control

01:04:47.050 --> 01:04:49.690
or whatever it is, and
they're able to sustain

01:04:49.690 --> 01:04:52.600
a calorie deficit or depending
on whatever their goal is,

01:04:52.600 --> 01:04:54.740
I don't think there's
really any downside to it.

01:04:54.740 --> 01:04:57.250
I do think the one thing
to keep in mind is,

01:04:57.250 --> 01:04:59.480
when you look at like
going between extremes,

01:04:59.480 --> 01:05:05.370
so like low fat to low
carb or vise versa,

01:05:05.370 --> 01:05:08.130
there can be, in that
transition, period a little bit

01:05:08.130 --> 01:05:11.160
of weirdness for lack
of a better term.

01:05:11.160 --> 01:05:15.060
Like for example, if you've
been on a ketogenic diet and all

01:05:15.060 --> 01:05:17.850
of a sudden, you move
to a higher carb diet,

01:05:17.850 --> 01:05:20.790
you'll be basically
insulin resistant

01:05:20.790 --> 01:05:22.660
for just a short period of time.

01:05:22.660 --> 01:05:24.660
Now, is that going to
cause any health problems?

01:05:24.660 --> 01:05:27.510
Probably not in the long term,
especially, if you're still

01:05:27.510 --> 01:05:28.890
controlling calories.

01:05:28.890 --> 01:05:33.330
But just because your body has
like upregulated these systems,

01:05:33.330 --> 01:05:37.410
dealing mostly with fat
and glucose production

01:05:37.410 --> 01:05:39.750
rather than glucose
metabolism, so now

01:05:39.750 --> 01:05:42.928
if you start taking glucose
or carbohydrate back in,

01:05:42.928 --> 01:05:44.970
like for example, you get
somebody-- or a glucose

01:05:44.970 --> 01:05:47.430
tolerance test after
they've been on keto,

01:05:47.430 --> 01:05:49.487
they'll do pretty
terribly at it.

01:05:49.487 --> 01:05:50.820
But that doesn't last that long.

01:05:50.820 --> 01:05:51.750
ANDREW HUBERMAN: About how long?

01:05:51.750 --> 01:05:52.665
LAYNE NORTON: A few weeks.

01:05:52.665 --> 01:05:55.207
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think that's
important for people to know,

01:05:55.207 --> 01:05:57.758
because I have a feeling
during those first few weeks

01:05:57.758 --> 01:05:59.550
are the period of time
when a lot of people

01:05:59.550 --> 01:06:02.260
go running back to what
they were doing previously.

01:06:02.260 --> 01:06:05.340
Which is not to say that they
shouldn't, but I've certainly

01:06:05.340 --> 01:06:05.850
done that.

01:06:05.850 --> 01:06:08.160
I've tried very low
carbohydrate diet,

01:06:08.160 --> 01:06:09.390
and I would have assumed--

01:06:09.390 --> 01:06:11.160
and now I know I'm
completely wrong,

01:06:11.160 --> 01:06:15.030
but I assumed that I was
so carbohydrate starved

01:06:15.030 --> 01:06:18.240
for so long that my
insulin sensitivity, which

01:06:18.240 --> 01:06:19.770
is a good thing,
by the way folks,

01:06:19.770 --> 01:06:21.640
would have gone
through the roof,

01:06:21.640 --> 01:06:24.150
and I would be able to just
sponge up every bit of glucose

01:06:24.150 --> 01:06:26.150
that I would have ingested
through carbohydrate.

01:06:26.150 --> 01:06:27.670
So I did indeed switch over.

01:06:27.670 --> 01:06:29.970
And I felt like I had
pretty terrible brain fog.

01:06:29.970 --> 01:06:30.990
I even got some jitters.

01:06:30.990 --> 01:06:32.385
And I thought, what is this?

01:06:32.385 --> 01:06:34.740
My blood sugar was low
before, and now my blood sugar

01:06:34.740 --> 01:06:36.990
should be in more
moderate territory.

01:06:36.990 --> 01:06:39.030
But based on what
you just said, I'd

01:06:39.030 --> 01:06:41.460
upregulated the
enzymes and systems

01:06:41.460 --> 01:06:44.850
in the body for fat
metabolism on the keto diet

01:06:44.850 --> 01:06:46.980
and then switching over--

01:06:46.980 --> 01:06:51.833
there was basically a ramping up
of the molecules involved in--

01:06:51.833 --> 01:06:52.875
presumably in glycolysis.

01:06:52.875 --> 01:06:53.970
LAYNE NORTON: Transition period.

01:06:53.970 --> 01:06:55.830
I mean, think about if you
haven't weight trained before

01:06:55.830 --> 01:06:56.760
and you start weight
training, you're

01:06:56.760 --> 01:06:57.930
going to feel pretty terrible.

01:06:57.930 --> 01:06:59.460
Like you're going
to be sore and stiff

01:06:59.460 --> 01:07:00.585
and all that kind of stuff.

01:07:00.585 --> 01:07:03.327
But I will say, you aren't
necessarily wrong in what

01:07:03.327 --> 01:07:05.160
you said about being
more insulin sensitive,

01:07:05.160 --> 01:07:09.160
because it depends on how you
measure insulin sensitivity.

01:07:09.160 --> 01:07:14.220
So if you measure with something
like fasting blood glucose

01:07:14.220 --> 01:07:17.100
or fasting insulin
or even HOMA-IR,

01:07:17.100 --> 01:07:19.708
those tend to be pretty
good on low carb.

01:07:19.708 --> 01:07:21.750
But then if you do it all
glucose tolerance test,

01:07:21.750 --> 01:07:23.140
it tends to be pretty bad.

01:07:23.140 --> 01:07:27.270
And so it depends on
your specific measure.

01:07:27.270 --> 01:07:31.820
So I think that
the idea that keto

01:07:31.820 --> 01:07:36.290
makes you glucose intolerant
or insulin resistant,

01:07:36.290 --> 01:07:39.080
I think, again, it's
just a transition period.

01:07:39.080 --> 01:07:40.463
And I'm not too
worried about it.

01:07:40.463 --> 01:07:42.380
But it is something
important to keep in mind.

01:07:42.380 --> 01:07:44.005
And one of the reasons
like if somebody

01:07:44.005 --> 01:07:46.070
was to transition out
of keto, typically,

01:07:46.070 --> 01:07:48.140
if I'm working with them
or one of our coaches

01:07:48.140 --> 01:07:49.790
are working with
them, we'll kind of

01:07:49.790 --> 01:07:53.150
instruct them to do it like
slowly and systematically

01:07:53.150 --> 01:07:55.100
over like a four to
eight-week period.

01:07:55.100 --> 01:07:57.695
That way, hopefully, they're
not having that period

01:07:57.695 --> 01:07:59.570
of two weeks where
they're like, oh, man, why

01:07:59.570 --> 01:08:01.290
do I feel so terrible?

01:08:01.290 --> 01:08:03.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A
very important point.

01:08:03.670 --> 01:08:07.760
I want to go to the other end
literally and figuratively

01:08:07.760 --> 01:08:10.990
and talk about gut
health, because up

01:08:10.990 --> 01:08:12.190
until now, and certainly--

01:08:12.190 --> 01:08:14.148
LAYNE NORTON: I see what
you did with that pun.

01:08:14.148 --> 01:08:16.939
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And certainly
in the last few minutes,

01:08:16.939 --> 01:08:19.614
we've been talking a lot
about top-down processes.

01:08:19.614 --> 01:08:22.689
The brain, the psychology,
placebo effects,

01:08:22.689 --> 01:08:24.939
but the very real
aspects of those,

01:08:24.939 --> 01:08:28.630
not that I can imagine 2,000
calories, 1,000 calories

01:08:28.630 --> 01:08:30.520
and somehow change the
law of thermodynamics.

01:08:30.520 --> 01:08:31.103
Can't do that.

01:08:31.103 --> 01:08:33.279
But we've been
top-down in integrating

01:08:33.279 --> 01:08:36.069
a lot of different
ideas into weight loss

01:08:36.069 --> 01:08:38.050
maintenance and weight gain.

01:08:38.050 --> 01:08:41.890
But gut health, at least
the more popular studies

01:08:41.890 --> 01:08:46.270
on gut health, have blown a
lot of things out of the water.

01:08:46.270 --> 01:08:48.910
For instance, this idea that
you could take obese mice

01:08:48.910 --> 01:08:52.240
and literally give them fecal
transplants from lean mice,

01:08:52.240 --> 01:08:56.380
and yes, that sounds
like what it sounds like.

01:08:56.380 --> 01:09:00.232
Fecal transplants definitely
inserted through the same end

01:09:00.232 --> 01:09:01.149
in which it comes out.

01:09:01.149 --> 01:09:02.859
And I point that out because
a lot of people have asked me

01:09:02.859 --> 01:09:05.560
that they-- and it was kind of
scary to me, I thought, yes,

01:09:05.560 --> 01:09:08.470
this is not about
ingesting feces, this is--

01:09:08.470 --> 01:09:12.130
they literally do a transplant
of these from lean mice

01:09:12.130 --> 01:09:14.830
into obese mice and the
obese mice get lean.

01:09:14.830 --> 01:09:17.350
And yes, this has
been done in humans.

01:09:17.350 --> 01:09:20.740
Limited number of studies and
observed some pretty impressive

01:09:20.740 --> 01:09:23.770
effects on weight loss
that I have to assume

01:09:23.770 --> 01:09:25.814
could be related
to placebo effect.

01:09:25.814 --> 01:09:27.939
They might have told these
obese people, hey, look,

01:09:27.939 --> 01:09:29.740
you're going to get lean
through this fecal transplant

01:09:29.740 --> 01:09:30.609
from lean people.

01:09:30.609 --> 01:09:34.090
But more likely,
it had some effect

01:09:34.090 --> 01:09:36.279
on their core physiology.

01:09:36.279 --> 01:09:37.810
I don't know which
aspects although,

01:09:37.810 --> 01:09:39.899
I can speculate which ones.

01:09:39.899 --> 01:09:41.640
And they became leaner.

01:09:41.640 --> 01:09:42.660
They lost weight.

01:09:42.660 --> 01:09:46.649
And that is, in some sense,
miraculous, especially given

01:09:46.649 --> 01:09:49.649
the important role of psychology
and exercise and satiety

01:09:49.649 --> 01:09:52.146
signals, because I'm going to
assume that they controlled

01:09:52.146 --> 01:09:53.729
for a number of those
other variables,

01:09:53.729 --> 01:09:55.320
although no study is perfect.

01:09:55.320 --> 01:09:57.330
What are your thoughts
about gut health

01:09:57.330 --> 01:10:01.170
as it relates to metabolism,
energy utilization,

01:10:01.170 --> 01:10:02.128
and balance?

01:10:02.128 --> 01:10:02.920
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:10:02.920 --> 01:10:06.070
So the first thing I'll say is
I'm not a gut health expert,

01:10:06.070 --> 01:10:07.583
but I will--

01:10:07.583 --> 01:10:09.750
I feel relatively comfortable
talking about it based

01:10:09.750 --> 01:10:12.150
on conversations I've had
with people who are experts--

01:10:12.150 --> 01:10:16.388
one being Suzanne
Devkota who's--

01:10:16.388 --> 01:10:17.430
you're familiar with her?

01:10:17.430 --> 01:10:18.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, she's sort

01:10:18.360 --> 01:10:20.310
of a phenom in this area
from what I understood.

01:10:20.310 --> 01:10:20.910
LAYNE NORTON: So
she was actually

01:10:20.910 --> 01:10:23.638
doing her masters when I was
doing my PhD in Lehman's lab.

01:10:23.638 --> 01:10:24.930
So she was one of my lab mates.

01:10:24.930 --> 01:10:26.013
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Terrific.

01:10:26.013 --> 01:10:29.940
LAYNE NORTON: And the
other thing to say

01:10:29.940 --> 01:10:35.160
is, even gut health experts,
and Suzanne will tell you this,

01:10:35.160 --> 01:10:37.380
they're like, talk
to me in 20 years.

01:10:37.380 --> 01:10:39.270
We just know so little.

01:10:39.270 --> 01:10:41.910
I think that's an overall thing
that people don't understand

01:10:41.910 --> 01:10:46.410
is the scientific consensus
moves very, very slow, probably

01:10:46.410 --> 01:10:47.290
for good reason.

01:10:47.290 --> 01:10:50.700
Because if we just flipped
our scientific consensus based

01:10:50.700 --> 01:10:56.040
on one study, I mean,
it would be a mess.

01:10:56.040 --> 01:10:58.350
So it's going to take
time before we really

01:10:58.350 --> 01:11:04.330
understand the implications
of the gut and what it means.

01:11:04.330 --> 01:11:09.920
So when it comes to weight
loss, there probably

01:11:09.920 --> 01:11:11.000
is a role in there.

01:11:11.000 --> 01:11:13.730
I mean, we've seen that
there's something going on.

01:11:13.730 --> 01:11:21.320
Now, whether that's-- is
it something where a gut

01:11:21.320 --> 01:11:25.370
microbiome make makeup that's
more obese resistant, perhaps,

01:11:25.370 --> 01:11:28.670
it extracts less calories
out of the food you eat.

01:11:28.670 --> 01:11:31.470
Or perhaps, it's elevating BMR.

01:11:31.470 --> 01:11:33.845
Although, I think that that's
probably somewhat unlikely.

01:11:33.845 --> 01:11:35.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do you
think it could impact

01:11:35.720 --> 01:11:37.018
the way satiety signals are?

01:11:37.018 --> 01:11:38.060
LAYNE NORTON: So that's--

01:11:38.060 --> 01:11:39.530
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I mean,
back to the brain again.

01:11:39.530 --> 01:11:41.822
LAYNE NORTON: --so that's--
we know that there's a link

01:11:41.822 --> 01:11:43.610
in the gut brain axis.

01:11:43.610 --> 01:11:46.820
And so my suspicion
is that it probably

01:11:46.820 --> 01:11:50.430
is working via
appetite regulation.

01:11:50.430 --> 01:11:56.330
So I mean, if we look at
the most effective obesity

01:11:56.330 --> 01:11:59.900
treatments out there,
which is like semaglutide,

01:11:59.900 --> 01:12:03.560
I mean, you
consistently see a 15%

01:12:03.560 --> 01:12:05.630
on average loss of body
weight, which is massive

01:12:05.630 --> 01:12:07.490
and people keep it off.

01:12:07.490 --> 01:12:10.670
That is a GLP-1 mimetic
which is a gut hormone.

01:12:10.670 --> 01:12:15.260
And it, basically, just is a
very, very powerful appetite

01:12:15.260 --> 01:12:16.020
suppressant.

01:12:16.020 --> 01:12:18.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, I
guess I'm interrupting,

01:12:18.020 --> 01:12:19.940
but hopefully, with a purpose.

01:12:19.940 --> 01:12:21.650
There's this really
interesting study.

01:12:21.650 --> 01:12:24.050
And it's in mice,
admittedly, but published

01:12:24.050 --> 01:12:25.550
in a neuroscience
journal recently.

01:12:25.550 --> 01:12:28.700
And basically, the takeaway
is that like so many things

01:12:28.700 --> 01:12:32.900
in neuroscience, the GLP-1
works in two parallel pathways.

01:12:32.900 --> 01:12:34.892
In the brain, it seems
to impact neurons

01:12:34.892 --> 01:12:36.600
in the hypothalamus
that control satiety.

01:12:36.600 --> 01:12:38.030
So exactly what you're saying.

01:12:38.030 --> 01:12:41.360
And in the gut,
it seems to create

01:12:41.360 --> 01:12:44.340
an activation of the
mechanosensors in the gut.

01:12:44.340 --> 01:12:48.505
So the perception is that the
gut is full even-- or fuller.

01:12:48.505 --> 01:12:49.880
I should say, not
full, because I

01:12:49.880 --> 01:12:53.030
think people who take
semaglutide don't feel bloated.

01:12:53.030 --> 01:12:54.110
I don't know, they might.

01:12:54.110 --> 01:12:57.950
But that one feels as if
their gut is actually fuller

01:12:57.950 --> 01:13:00.117
because these mechanical
sensors that send-- stretch

01:13:00.117 --> 01:13:01.492
are sending signals
to the brain,

01:13:01.492 --> 01:13:02.790
oh, I actually have some food.

01:13:02.790 --> 01:13:03.892
I'm not empty down there.

01:13:03.892 --> 01:13:04.725
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

01:13:04.725 --> 01:13:07.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Anyway,
I'm tickled by this result

01:13:07.430 --> 01:13:11.180
mostly because every time I
hear about a drug or a molecule

01:13:11.180 --> 01:13:13.760
having an effect, we think it
has an effect at one location.

01:13:13.760 --> 01:13:16.520
But it's kind of interesting
that, especially for something

01:13:16.520 --> 01:13:19.280
like appetite regulation,
that it would be impacting

01:13:19.280 --> 01:13:20.420
body and brain in parallel.

01:13:20.420 --> 01:13:21.260
Anyway, forgive me.

01:13:21.260 --> 01:13:22.385
LAYNE NORTON: That's great.

01:13:22.385 --> 01:13:25.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You can tell
I'm really excited about this.

01:13:25.700 --> 01:13:28.490
And here you are telling
a neuroscientist, me,

01:13:28.490 --> 01:13:32.440
that a lot, perhaps,
circles back to these brain

01:13:32.440 --> 01:13:33.475
mechanisms of satiety.

01:13:33.475 --> 01:13:34.690
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:13:34.690 --> 01:13:38.370
I mean, I think that--

01:13:38.370 --> 01:13:40.890
and especially looking at
the research on leptin,

01:13:40.890 --> 01:13:46.450
you we used to think,
OK, metabolism is mostly

01:13:46.450 --> 01:13:50.560
like liver-based
and then there's

01:13:50.560 --> 01:13:53.200
metabolism in the adipocyte
and skeletal muscle.

01:13:53.200 --> 01:13:55.450
But none of this stuff
exists in isolation.

01:13:55.450 --> 01:13:57.790
There's so much crosstalk
between these pathways.

01:13:57.790 --> 01:14:01.160
And that's-- when we
get into mechanisms--

01:14:01.160 --> 01:14:04.042
though one of the things
I love mechanisms.

01:14:04.042 --> 01:14:05.500
But one of the
things I tell people

01:14:05.500 --> 01:14:08.650
is keep in mind that when
you're dealing with an outcome,

01:14:08.650 --> 01:14:10.990
like when I say
outcome-based, we're

01:14:10.990 --> 01:14:14.810
talking about physical outcomes
like weight loss, fat loss,

01:14:14.810 --> 01:14:18.080
changes in blood
markers, whatever,

01:14:18.080 --> 01:14:20.840
though that is the
summation of thousands

01:14:20.840 --> 01:14:22.530
of different mechanisms.

01:14:22.530 --> 01:14:27.050
So sure, sometimes, you can
affect a mechanistic pathway,

01:14:27.050 --> 01:14:31.010
and you get straight down the
line outcome, but not always.

01:14:31.010 --> 01:14:36.860
Whenever you make a treatment
or anything into the system,

01:14:36.860 --> 01:14:39.500
it's like throwing
a pebble in a lake.

01:14:39.500 --> 01:14:40.850
It creates ripples.

01:14:40.850 --> 01:14:45.350
And we don't always know
what those are going to be.

01:14:45.350 --> 01:14:48.740
And that's why-- I mean,
we've seen certain drugs,

01:14:48.740 --> 01:14:50.237
what works on this pathway.

01:14:50.237 --> 01:14:52.070
And then they list off
all the side effects.

01:14:52.070 --> 01:14:54.612
And you go, well, how would it
create that many side effects?

01:14:54.612 --> 01:14:56.900
Because nothing--
for the most part,

01:14:56.900 --> 01:14:59.030
they don't just
work in one place.

01:14:59.030 --> 01:15:02.120
There's multitude
of places it works.

01:15:02.120 --> 01:15:05.150
And to your point
about semaglutide

01:15:05.150 --> 01:15:08.425
and the effects on
mechanical sensors,

01:15:08.425 --> 01:15:09.800
it's probably why
a lot of people

01:15:09.800 --> 01:15:15.883
report actually low grade nausea
when they're using semaglutide.

01:15:15.883 --> 01:15:16.550
Because of that.

01:15:16.550 --> 01:15:20.210
Because if you're-- that feeling
is usually not like a real

01:15:20.210 --> 01:15:23.450
comfortable feeling, but I mean,
it will get you to not eat.

01:15:23.450 --> 01:15:28.880
So I think there's absolutely
likely a connection,

01:15:28.880 --> 01:15:31.540
but we haven't fully
elucidated how that works.

01:15:31.540 --> 01:15:34.430
And we think about how
complicated the gut is.

01:15:34.430 --> 01:15:37.130
I think I heard
something like there's

01:15:37.130 --> 01:15:41.190
more cells in our microbiome by
far than there are in our body.

01:15:41.190 --> 01:15:42.620
So we're actually more--

01:15:42.620 --> 01:15:44.840
in terms of a cell
per cell level,

01:15:44.840 --> 01:15:47.540
we're actually more bacteria
than we are eukaryote.

01:15:47.540 --> 01:15:49.325
ANDREW HUBERMAN: There's
Justin Sonnenburg

01:15:49.325 --> 01:15:51.200
who's one of the world
experts on microbiome.

01:15:51.200 --> 01:15:53.300
He's in the lab upstairs
from mine at Stanford.

01:15:53.300 --> 01:15:55.640
And he has this idea--

01:15:55.640 --> 01:15:58.940
it's just an idea that because
we are indeed more bacteria

01:15:58.940 --> 01:16:01.270
than we are cells, the
question is, who's the host

01:16:01.270 --> 01:16:02.270
and who's the passenger?

01:16:02.270 --> 01:16:03.200
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:16:03.200 --> 01:16:05.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Maybe,
we are just-- maybe

01:16:05.210 --> 01:16:08.120
they're exploiting us to take
them around and interact,

01:16:08.120 --> 01:16:10.355
because they interact
and grow on one another.

01:16:10.355 --> 01:16:12.230
And so this idea that
this freaks people out,

01:16:12.230 --> 01:16:13.355
Lex Fridman will love this.

01:16:13.355 --> 01:16:15.200
That maybe human beings
are just actually

01:16:15.200 --> 01:16:18.680
the vehicles for the microbiome
and not the other way around.

01:16:18.680 --> 01:16:22.400
Anyway, kind of a scary thought.

01:16:22.400 --> 01:16:26.030
Do you do anything specifically
to support your gut microbiome?

01:16:26.030 --> 01:16:29.475
Are you a probiotic guy or a
fermented foods guy or a fiber

01:16:29.475 --> 01:16:29.975
guy?

01:16:29.975 --> 01:16:32.773
LAYNE NORTON: So-- again, I'm
going to kind of go straight

01:16:32.773 --> 01:16:34.190
down the line from
what I've heard

01:16:34.190 --> 01:16:35.520
from Suzanne and other experts.

01:16:35.520 --> 01:16:39.660
So if you want to improve gut
health, one of the biggest

01:16:39.660 --> 01:16:40.548
levers--

01:16:40.548 --> 01:16:42.090
the three biggest
levers you can pull

01:16:42.090 --> 01:16:46.170
is not eating too many
calories, exercising--

01:16:46.170 --> 01:16:48.900
there is a connection
between exercise and the gut,

01:16:48.900 --> 01:16:50.020
and fiber.

01:16:50.020 --> 01:16:54.910
So it is-- of the
things we know,

01:16:54.910 --> 01:16:58.590
dietary fiber seems to
positively impact the gut

01:16:58.590 --> 01:17:00.810
because it is what's
called a prebiotic.

01:17:00.810 --> 01:17:05.770
So your gut microbiota can
take, especially, soluble fiber.

01:17:05.770 --> 01:17:08.340
Although, there's actually some
evidence, at least in mice,

01:17:08.340 --> 01:17:12.313
that they might be able to use
some insoluble fiber as well.

01:17:12.313 --> 01:17:14.730
I think Suzanne was doing a
study looking at hemicellulose

01:17:14.730 --> 01:17:17.100
and actually seeing that
some, like specific forms

01:17:17.100 --> 01:17:20.165
of microbiota, flourish with
hemicellulose suggesting

01:17:20.165 --> 01:17:21.540
that they may
actually be getting

01:17:21.540 --> 01:17:24.600
some kind of fuel out of it,
which is really interesting.

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:28.590
But again, in mice--
so just a huge caveat.

01:17:28.590 --> 01:17:31.920
So your gut
microbiome can produce

01:17:31.920 --> 01:17:39.610
these short chain fatty acids
by fermenting the soluble fiber.

01:17:39.610 --> 01:17:41.850
And there's quite
a bit of evidence

01:17:41.850 --> 01:17:45.240
that these volatile fatty
acids, which can be then

01:17:45.240 --> 01:17:48.120
actually reabsorbed
into the liver,

01:17:48.120 --> 01:17:50.400
that they have some
positive effects.

01:17:50.400 --> 01:17:51.600
Like for example, butyrate.

01:17:51.600 --> 01:17:53.392
When they've done
butyrate supplementation,

01:17:53.392 --> 01:17:56.110
they've actually seen positive
effects on insulin sensitivity.

01:17:56.110 --> 01:18:03.980
So what we seem to understand
is that more diversity seems

01:18:03.980 --> 01:18:07.760
to be better, fiber
seems to be positive,

01:18:07.760 --> 01:18:10.002
prebiotics seem to work
much better than probiotics.

01:18:10.002 --> 01:18:11.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Supplemented prebiotics.

01:18:11.710 --> 01:18:12.470
LAYNE NORTON: Yes.

01:18:12.470 --> 01:18:15.590
So the problem with
most of the probiotics

01:18:15.590 --> 01:18:20.120
is they're typically
not concentrated enough

01:18:20.120 --> 01:18:21.680
to actually colonize.

01:18:21.680 --> 01:18:25.430
And even if you do colonize,
what happens is like,

01:18:25.430 --> 01:18:28.730
let's say you colonize
some microbiota that you

01:18:28.730 --> 01:18:30.860
didn't really have much of.

01:18:30.860 --> 01:18:34.440
If you're not fueling it
with the appropriate fiber,

01:18:34.440 --> 01:18:36.890
it's not going to stay anyway,
because it's essentially

01:18:36.890 --> 01:18:38.160
going to starve.

01:18:38.160 --> 01:18:41.390
So the research seems to
really clearly suggest

01:18:41.390 --> 01:18:44.870
that eating enough fiber,
which is, again, a prebiotic,

01:18:44.870 --> 01:18:48.980
that that is a better way
to get a healthier gut per

01:18:48.980 --> 01:18:50.585
se than probiotic.

01:18:50.585 --> 01:18:53.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
fiber sources do you use?

01:18:53.000 --> 01:18:55.815
And I think-- I mean, I realize
there's a huge array of choices

01:18:55.815 --> 01:18:57.440
out there, but people
will want to have

01:18:57.440 --> 01:18:59.470
some ideas as to how
they could, perhaps,

01:18:59.470 --> 01:19:00.470
mimic what you're doing.

01:19:00.470 --> 01:19:01.262
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:19:01.262 --> 01:19:03.440
And I would just say diversity.

01:19:03.440 --> 01:19:08.840
So there's various evidence
from various different fiber

01:19:08.840 --> 01:19:10.220
sources--

01:19:10.220 --> 01:19:14.270
fruits and vegetables,
obviously, grains,

01:19:14.270 --> 01:19:19.170
some whole grains, some cereals,
and then various other sources.

01:19:19.170 --> 01:19:21.500
So this is one of the
things where we don't really

01:19:21.500 --> 01:19:22.730
have a good idea.

01:19:22.730 --> 01:19:24.890
If you know this
one source of fiber

01:19:24.890 --> 01:19:26.750
is better than another
source of fiber,

01:19:26.750 --> 01:19:28.940
we just know that fiber
overall is pretty good.

01:19:28.940 --> 01:19:30.315
And one thing I'll
tell people is

01:19:30.315 --> 01:19:32.240
like if you want a
longevity hack, I mean,

01:19:32.240 --> 01:19:33.920
fiber is kind of
the longevity hack.

01:19:33.920 --> 01:19:36.380
If you look at some of
these cohort studies,

01:19:36.380 --> 01:19:39.470
there was actually a recent
really large meta analysis

01:19:39.470 --> 01:19:42.840
of over a million subjects.

01:19:42.840 --> 01:19:46.970
And basically, what it showed
was that for every 10 gram

01:19:46.970 --> 01:19:50.570
increase in fiber, there
was a 10% reduction

01:19:50.570 --> 01:19:52.460
in the risk of mortality.

01:19:52.460 --> 01:19:56.360
And that extended, specifically,
also to cardiovascular disease

01:19:56.360 --> 01:19:57.870
and cancer.

01:19:57.870 --> 01:19:59.420
So one of the things
I'll tell people

01:19:59.420 --> 01:20:01.550
when they get like
really into whether it's

01:20:01.550 --> 01:20:03.560
intermittent fasting or
all these other things,

01:20:03.560 --> 01:20:04.893
say, that's great, that's great.

01:20:04.893 --> 01:20:08.000
Are you eating like over
50, 60 grams of fiber a day?

01:20:08.000 --> 01:20:10.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Conceptualize 50 or 60 grams.

01:20:10.230 --> 01:20:13.970
So if I were to eat
like a-- let's just say,

01:20:13.970 --> 01:20:18.170
a quarter plate of
broccoli and the broccoli

01:20:18.170 --> 01:20:19.380
isn't stacked to the ceiling.

01:20:19.380 --> 01:20:22.512
The broccoli is just
reasonably stacked on there.

01:20:22.512 --> 01:20:24.470
Approximately, how many
grams of fiber is that?

01:20:24.470 --> 01:20:26.317
If it's like two
cups of broccoli.

01:20:26.317 --> 01:20:26.900
There's a lot.

01:20:26.900 --> 01:20:27.692
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:20:27.692 --> 01:20:30.650
So like 200 grams of broccoli,
per se, would probably be like

01:20:30.650 --> 01:20:31.825
5, 6 grams of fiber.

01:20:31.825 --> 01:20:33.475
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And I need
to get how much per day?

01:20:33.475 --> 01:20:34.580
LAYNE NORTON:
Well, I would say--

01:20:34.580 --> 01:20:35.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Ideally.

01:20:35.330 --> 01:20:37.497
LAYNE NORTON: --typically,
what the recommended dose

01:20:37.497 --> 01:20:40.010
is is 15 grams
per 1,000 calories

01:20:40.010 --> 01:20:43.390
intake because if you're
eating low calories,

01:20:43.390 --> 01:20:45.710
it's difficult to
get enough fiber in.

01:20:45.710 --> 01:20:49.550
But based on-- and again,
these are cohort studies.

01:20:49.550 --> 01:20:52.160
But you can't do 20-year
long randomized human control

01:20:52.160 --> 01:20:54.950
trials, unfortunately.

01:20:54.950 --> 01:20:57.800
That doesn't really appear
to be a top end, at least

01:20:57.800 --> 01:20:59.120
for the benefits of fiber.

01:20:59.120 --> 01:21:01.580
It probably boils down to
how much you can tolerate

01:21:01.580 --> 01:21:03.140
without feeling uncomfortable.

01:21:03.140 --> 01:21:06.230
Because if you're eating
like a ton of fiber, I mean,

01:21:06.230 --> 01:21:08.810
at some point, it's not
going to be very comfortable.

01:21:08.810 --> 01:21:09.727
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:21:09.727 --> 01:21:11.600
Exercise becomes
uncomfortable or hazardous.

01:21:11.600 --> 01:21:12.100
Yeah.

01:21:12.100 --> 01:21:14.340
LAYNE NORTON: And I actually a--

01:21:14.340 --> 01:21:17.940
kind of touching on that
because I think it is important.

01:21:17.940 --> 01:21:19.980
A lot of people have kind of--

01:21:19.980 --> 01:21:22.710
in the carnivore community said,
well, you don't need fiber,

01:21:22.710 --> 01:21:24.340
you poop just fine without it.

01:21:24.340 --> 01:21:27.150
And I'll always say, well,
pooping is the last reason

01:21:27.150 --> 01:21:28.050
to have fiber.

01:21:28.050 --> 01:21:29.010
Like yes, it does help.

01:21:29.010 --> 01:21:32.520
It does seem to make
elimination easier,

01:21:32.520 --> 01:21:35.563
you can do it more frequently,
adds bulk to stool.

01:21:35.563 --> 01:21:37.230
But that's not why
you should eat fiber.

01:21:37.230 --> 01:21:40.800
Why should eat fiber is because
of the effects of mortality.

01:21:40.800 --> 01:21:43.230
And some of the pushback
will be, well, this

01:21:43.230 --> 01:21:45.030
is healthy user bias.

01:21:45.030 --> 01:21:46.290
And what I'll say is--

01:21:46.290 --> 01:21:47.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Meaning,
healthy people do this

01:21:47.550 --> 01:21:48.240
and therefore it's working.

01:21:48.240 --> 01:21:49.890
LAYNE NORTON: Healthy people
eat more fiber and therefore--

01:21:49.890 --> 01:21:50.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.

01:21:50.190 --> 01:21:50.460
Yeah.

01:21:50.460 --> 01:21:51.750
LAYNE NORTON: And I mean, yeah.

01:21:51.750 --> 01:21:52.833
There's something to that.

01:21:52.833 --> 01:21:54.560
But if it was just
healthy user bias,

01:21:54.560 --> 01:21:56.310
typically, you would
see some disagreement

01:21:56.310 --> 01:21:57.270
between the studies.

01:21:57.270 --> 01:21:59.370
And a great example of
that is like red meat.

01:21:59.370 --> 01:22:03.770
So not every study shows
red meat has an association

01:22:03.770 --> 01:22:05.103
with cancer and mortality.

01:22:05.103 --> 01:22:07.270
There's differences depending
on the population use,

01:22:07.270 --> 01:22:10.060
depending on what they define
as high red meat, low red meat,

01:22:10.060 --> 01:22:12.490
whether it's
processed, unprocessed.

01:22:12.490 --> 01:22:15.610
But I have not found
a study on fiber

01:22:15.610 --> 01:22:20.590
and cardiovascular disease
and cancer and mortality

01:22:20.590 --> 01:22:23.440
where it did not show
improvements from higher fiber.

01:22:23.440 --> 01:22:28.030
So to me, that suggests
that that effect is real.

01:22:28.030 --> 01:22:33.100
And so again, as much fiber
as you can get in comfortably,

01:22:33.100 --> 01:22:35.140
I would try to do
it because it seems

01:22:35.140 --> 01:22:36.640
to have some really
powerful effects

01:22:36.640 --> 01:22:39.130
and is good for
the gut microbiome.

01:22:39.130 --> 01:22:41.860
The other thing that
may be a consideration

01:22:41.860 --> 01:22:44.650
for the microbiome is
there's some evidence that

01:22:44.650 --> 01:22:47.170
saturated fat may not be
great for the microbiome.

01:22:47.170 --> 01:22:52.090
That it reduces the
prevalence of some

01:22:52.090 --> 01:22:54.220
of the more positive
strains of bacteria.

01:22:54.220 --> 01:22:57.280
And that appears to be not so
much from the saturated fat

01:22:57.280 --> 01:22:59.560
itself but from the
bile end products

01:22:59.560 --> 01:23:02.175
that combine with
saturated fat seems

01:23:02.175 --> 01:23:03.550
to have a negative
effect on some

01:23:03.550 --> 01:23:08.220
of these more healthier
forms of gut microbiota.

01:23:08.220 --> 01:23:09.990
But again, this is
really difficult

01:23:09.990 --> 01:23:13.230
because we don't
even know necessarily

01:23:13.230 --> 01:23:16.650
yet which species
of gut microbiota

01:23:16.650 --> 01:23:18.400
are positive or negative.

01:23:18.400 --> 01:23:21.150
And that's-- I mean, this gets
into some of these studies

01:23:21.150 --> 01:23:24.380
where they may
call it dysbiosis.

01:23:24.380 --> 01:23:27.052
Sounds scary, but dysbiosis
just means that the gut changed.

01:23:27.052 --> 01:23:28.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:23:28.010 --> 01:23:29.180
LAYNE NORTON: It
doesn't necessarily--

01:23:29.180 --> 01:23:31.220
it doesn't tell you
anything qualitative

01:23:31.220 --> 01:23:33.297
about whether the
change was bad or good.

01:23:33.297 --> 01:23:34.880
And so these are
just things, I think,

01:23:34.880 --> 01:23:37.130
we need to keep in mind when
we talk about this stuff.

01:23:37.130 --> 01:23:39.230
That this stuff is still
very much in its infancy.

01:23:39.230 --> 01:23:42.013
But in terms of the
big levers, I mean,

01:23:42.013 --> 01:23:43.430
it's pretty much
fits with what we

01:23:43.430 --> 01:23:44.840
know about a healthy lifestyle.

01:23:44.840 --> 01:23:49.610
Exercise, don't eat too much,
consume a good amount of fiber

01:23:49.610 --> 01:23:51.590
from diverse sources.

01:23:51.590 --> 01:23:53.150
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Fantastic.

01:23:53.150 --> 01:23:55.280
Fantastic because
it fits with what

01:23:55.280 --> 01:23:58.910
I like to think of as the
center of mass of evidence.

01:23:58.910 --> 01:24:00.950
And I'm starting to get
some window into what

01:24:00.950 --> 01:24:04.880
your process is around selection
of studies and no one study

01:24:04.880 --> 01:24:06.830
being wholly, but
when you look at,

01:24:06.830 --> 01:24:08.840
as you mentioned, all
the studies on fiber

01:24:08.840 --> 01:24:11.710
having a positive effect
to some degree or another,

01:24:11.710 --> 01:24:14.210
it's pretty hard to refute that
there isn't something really

01:24:14.210 --> 01:24:15.040
interesting there.

01:24:15.040 --> 01:24:18.790
LAYNE NORTON: And one thing
I'll tell people is like,

01:24:18.790 --> 01:24:20.240
one study--

01:24:20.240 --> 01:24:22.563
I mean, sometimes
I'll change my opinion

01:24:22.563 --> 01:24:24.230
based on a single
study when it's really

01:24:24.230 --> 01:24:26.300
well done and very powerful.

01:24:26.300 --> 01:24:27.980
But usually, one
study is just going

01:24:27.980 --> 01:24:29.188
to move me just a little bit.

01:24:29.188 --> 01:24:30.897
And then maybe, if
another one comes out,

01:24:30.897 --> 01:24:31.910
maybe a little bit more.

01:24:31.910 --> 01:24:33.993
And then like very slowly,
I'm going to get some--

01:24:33.993 --> 01:24:37.610
I mean, my experience
with LDL cholesterol,

01:24:37.610 --> 01:24:39.740
that's something I changed
my mind on a while back.

01:24:39.740 --> 01:24:44.540
When I was younger, like circa
2005 getting into grad school,

01:24:44.540 --> 01:24:47.630
the prevailing thought was,
well, it's not so much the LDL,

01:24:47.630 --> 01:24:51.770
it's the ratio of LDL to
HDL, that's what matters.

01:24:51.770 --> 01:24:54.480
And probably about
five years ago--

01:24:54.480 --> 01:24:56.840
and I was pretty strong
about that opinion,

01:24:56.840 --> 01:24:59.090
and then five years ago
looking at these Mendelian

01:24:59.090 --> 01:25:02.600
randomization studies, I went,
I can't hold this position

01:25:02.600 --> 01:25:03.470
anymore.

01:25:03.470 --> 01:25:06.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What is
your revised position on LDL?

01:25:06.520 --> 01:25:09.050
LAYNE NORTON: So if you
look at the research,

01:25:09.050 --> 01:25:11.930
HDL is important because it's
a marker of metabolic health.

01:25:11.930 --> 01:25:13.700
If you have high
HDL, it suggests

01:25:13.700 --> 01:25:15.530
that you are metabolically
quite healthy.

01:25:15.530 --> 01:25:19.410
You very rarely will you
have high HDL and high CRP,

01:25:19.410 --> 01:25:23.690
which is inflammatory marker
or dysregulated blood glucose.

01:25:23.690 --> 01:25:26.210
Almost exclusively,
people who have high HDL

01:25:26.210 --> 01:25:30.960
will have good biomarkers
of metabolic health.

01:25:30.960 --> 01:25:34.620
But if you take
drugs that raise HDL,

01:25:34.620 --> 01:25:38.130
it doesn't reduce your risk
of cardiovascular disease.

01:25:38.130 --> 01:25:40.350
In Mendelian
randomization studies,

01:25:40.350 --> 01:25:42.090
which Mendelian
randomization basically

01:25:42.090 --> 01:25:44.280
uses natural randomization.

01:25:44.280 --> 01:25:47.640
So some people are,
in the case of HDL,

01:25:47.640 --> 01:25:49.920
naturally higher secreted
or naturally lower

01:25:49.920 --> 01:25:51.450
secretions of HDL.

01:25:51.450 --> 01:25:54.270
And we talked about
how-- you can't really

01:25:54.270 --> 01:25:56.460
do a 20-year human
randomized controlled trial.

01:25:56.460 --> 01:25:58.252
And when you're trying
to examine something

01:25:58.252 --> 01:26:01.770
like heart disease, I mean, that
is a lifetime exposure issue.

01:26:01.770 --> 01:26:03.480
It's very unlikely
that you're going

01:26:03.480 --> 01:26:06.720
to pick out differences between
treatments in two years or even

01:26:06.720 --> 01:26:07.320
five years.

01:26:07.320 --> 01:26:08.850
I mean, people don't develop--

01:26:08.850 --> 01:26:10.440
typically, don't
develop heart disease

01:26:10.440 --> 01:26:14.190
until they're in their
50s, 60s, and 70s.

01:26:14.190 --> 01:26:18.190
What Mendelian randomization
allows is to say,

01:26:18.190 --> 01:26:21.820
OK, we have these people who
naturally secrete more or less.

01:26:21.820 --> 01:26:25.540
So we can stratify those and
look at what is their risk.

01:26:25.540 --> 01:26:28.350
So if you look at people
who are low series of HDL

01:26:28.350 --> 01:26:31.290
versus high secrete
of HDL withholding

01:26:31.290 --> 01:26:34.153
some of the other key
variables consistent like LDL,

01:26:34.153 --> 01:26:36.195
you don't see an effect
on heart disease, really.

01:26:36.195 --> 01:26:37.195
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Of LDL.

01:26:37.195 --> 01:26:38.130
LAYNE NORTON: Of HDL.

01:26:38.130 --> 01:26:39.297
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it, OK.

01:26:39.297 --> 01:26:41.820
LAYNE NORTON: But
when you look at LDL

01:26:41.820 --> 01:26:45.190
and you look at the
lifetime exposure to LDL,

01:26:45.190 --> 01:26:48.020
it is like a linear
effect on heart disease.

01:26:48.020 --> 01:26:50.890
And we know that it's
actually not so much LDL,

01:26:50.890 --> 01:26:54.580
but it's more
apolipoprotein B. But that

01:26:54.580 --> 01:26:57.200
tends to track with
LDL just in general.

01:26:57.200 --> 01:26:58.690
And if you look
at the mechanism--

01:26:58.690 --> 01:27:03.010
I mean, we know that LDL can
penetrate the endothelium,

01:27:03.010 --> 01:27:05.150
so there's the
mechanism is present.

01:27:05.150 --> 01:27:07.570
If we look at the
epidemiology, it

01:27:07.570 --> 01:27:09.643
supports that it's an
independent risk factor,

01:27:09.643 --> 01:27:11.560
and then, again, these
Mendelian randomization

01:27:11.560 --> 01:27:13.690
studies where we can
look at people's exposure

01:27:13.690 --> 01:27:17.710
over a lifetime, and then we
see that linear dose-dependent

01:27:17.710 --> 01:27:18.970
effect.

01:27:18.970 --> 01:27:20.980
To me, that was
convincing enough

01:27:20.980 --> 01:27:22.910
to change my mind on
that particular topic.

01:27:22.910 --> 01:27:26.440
And then if you look at
some of the Framingham data,

01:27:26.440 --> 01:27:28.720
look at high--

01:27:28.720 --> 01:27:34.100
if you like stratify, like
high HDL versus low HDL,

01:27:34.100 --> 01:27:38.010
both groups looking at
high LDL and low LDL.

01:27:38.010 --> 01:27:41.090
So if you have
high HDL, low LDL,

01:27:41.090 --> 01:27:43.790
you will still be lower
risk factor than somebody

01:27:43.790 --> 01:27:46.550
who is high HDL and high LDL.

01:27:46.550 --> 01:27:48.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
the ratio does matter.

01:27:48.380 --> 01:27:49.290
LAYNE NORTON: The
ratio does better.

01:27:49.290 --> 01:27:50.720
Same thing with inflammation.

01:27:50.720 --> 01:27:56.520
If you look at people who are
low inflammation, low LDL,

01:27:56.520 --> 01:27:58.500
they'll have a lower
risk than people who

01:27:58.500 --> 01:28:00.438
are low inflammation, high LDL.

01:28:00.438 --> 01:28:02.730
So again, that was sufficient
for me to change my mind.

01:28:02.730 --> 01:28:05.250
But it took-- it was like
not just one study came out.

01:28:05.250 --> 01:28:07.500
It was, OK, then there was
another study, and then

01:28:07.500 --> 01:28:10.042
another study, ans then another
study, and at a certain point

01:28:10.042 --> 01:28:13.350
I go, OK, well, now, I
either have to change my mind

01:28:13.350 --> 01:28:17.580
or I'm, basically, just going
to be cognitively dissonant

01:28:17.580 --> 01:28:20.130
and say, nope, I don't
believe all that.

01:28:20.130 --> 01:28:23.670
And so I think that's one of
the things to keep in mind.

01:28:23.670 --> 01:28:27.000
People will say, oh, are you
saying this is a bad study?

01:28:27.000 --> 01:28:29.460
Very rarely will I call
something, a bad study,

01:28:29.460 --> 01:28:32.130
because data is just data.

01:28:32.130 --> 01:28:35.580
But the issue becomes
how it is presented

01:28:35.580 --> 01:28:39.480
and how broadly it's applied
in the mainstream media

01:28:39.480 --> 01:28:41.310
or by people on--

01:28:41.310 --> 01:28:43.260
fitness influencers.

01:28:43.260 --> 01:28:46.050
And what I'll do
is try to step in

01:28:46.050 --> 01:28:49.930
and say, OK, let's consider
x, y and z as well.

01:28:49.930 --> 01:28:52.740
And then it's not a bad
study, but let's just

01:28:52.740 --> 01:28:55.153
be careful about how broadly
we apply the interpretation.

01:28:55.153 --> 01:28:56.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:28:56.070 --> 01:28:59.760
Well, and I think you are in
a very unique and important

01:28:59.760 --> 01:29:01.350
position to be able
to place things

01:29:01.350 --> 01:29:05.140
into their proper
context because of this,

01:29:05.140 --> 01:29:07.290
for lack of a better
word, holistic view

01:29:07.290 --> 01:29:12.120
of how the psychology
placebo effects also core

01:29:12.120 --> 01:29:15.010
physiology relate
to one another.

01:29:15.010 --> 01:29:15.725
And so on.

01:29:15.725 --> 01:29:17.850
In fact, I think that your
training as a biochemist

01:29:17.850 --> 01:29:22.020
and then training as in
nutrition with somebody who--

01:29:22.020 --> 01:29:25.000
Don Lemon, who was pushing
you to focus on outcomes,

01:29:25.000 --> 01:29:27.990
I think that's a
beautiful capture

01:29:27.990 --> 01:29:30.285
of the continuum at which
one can look at something.

01:29:30.285 --> 01:29:32.160
Because for those of
you who don't out there,

01:29:32.160 --> 01:29:34.530
you know a lot of laboratory
studies on mice and humans,

01:29:34.530 --> 01:29:36.600
for instance, in the
realm of biochemistry

01:29:36.600 --> 01:29:39.600
or in vitro studies, you'll
see a change in some molecule

01:29:39.600 --> 01:29:40.600
can be quite dramatic.

01:29:40.600 --> 01:29:41.700
And then the assumption
is, oh, you just

01:29:41.700 --> 01:29:43.453
take-- you take the
drugs that will change

01:29:43.453 --> 01:29:45.120
that molecule in a
particular direction,

01:29:45.120 --> 01:29:48.580
and then you'll get the effect
you want at the whole organism

01:29:48.580 --> 01:29:49.080
level.

01:29:49.080 --> 01:29:51.455
The person will lose weight,
the person will gain muscle,

01:29:51.455 --> 01:29:54.870
the animal will not have
Alzheimer's, et cetera.

01:29:54.870 --> 01:29:57.600
But it just doesn't work that
way because of the redundancy,

01:29:57.600 --> 01:29:59.048
and this interplay.

01:29:59.048 --> 01:30:00.840
LAYNE NORTON: Well, a
great example of that

01:30:00.840 --> 01:30:04.305
is, so my research was
actually in rodents.

01:30:04.305 --> 01:30:07.200
All my studies on protein
metabolism, and leucine,

01:30:07.200 --> 01:30:09.150
in particular, is
what we were studying.

01:30:09.150 --> 01:30:10.650
Well, we know if
you give leucine,

01:30:10.650 --> 01:30:12.900
it increases muscle
protein synthesis.

01:30:12.900 --> 01:30:15.450
But we also know if you
supplement with leucine,

01:30:15.450 --> 01:30:16.905
people don't get more muscular.

01:30:16.905 --> 01:30:17.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
was about to say--

01:30:17.610 --> 01:30:18.180
LAYNE NORTON: So as always--

01:30:18.180 --> 01:30:18.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--all you have to do

01:30:18.690 --> 01:30:19.650
is supplement with leucine?

01:30:19.650 --> 01:30:19.890
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

01:30:19.890 --> 01:30:20.432
Right, right.

01:30:20.432 --> 01:30:22.170
And so how is that possible?

01:30:22.170 --> 01:30:24.640
Muscle building is not
just protein synthesis.

01:30:24.640 --> 01:30:27.798
It's also the balance between
synthesis and degradation.

01:30:27.798 --> 01:30:30.090
And degradation just happens
to be very, very difficult

01:30:30.090 --> 01:30:31.080
to measure.

01:30:31.080 --> 01:30:33.720
But a great example--

01:30:33.720 --> 01:30:35.850
and again, one of the
cool things about my PhD

01:30:35.850 --> 01:30:37.830
was actually changed
the way I ate,

01:30:37.830 --> 01:30:39.420
which I think is interesting.

01:30:39.420 --> 01:30:41.723
So before, I had been like,
I eat eight meals a day,

01:30:41.723 --> 01:30:43.140
eat every two
hours, try to keep--

01:30:43.140 --> 01:30:43.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Eight meals a day?

01:30:43.770 --> 01:30:44.730
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah when
I got to grad school.

01:30:44.730 --> 01:30:45.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In
order to get that 30

01:30:45.630 --> 01:30:46.860
grams of protein per meal?

01:30:46.860 --> 01:30:48.817
LAYNE NORTON: Get that
amino drip going in

01:30:48.817 --> 01:30:49.650
was the idea, right?

01:30:49.650 --> 01:30:51.960
Like just have an IV
hooked up of amino acids.

01:30:51.960 --> 01:30:53.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Not really, folks.

01:30:53.430 --> 01:30:54.990
LAYNE NORTON: Not really, yeah.

01:30:54.990 --> 01:30:56.610
But that was the concept.

01:30:56.610 --> 01:31:02.670
But the first study that I did,
we, basically, looked at OK,

01:31:02.670 --> 01:31:06.840
a lot of people had measured the
amplitude of protein synthesis

01:31:06.840 --> 01:31:07.920
in response to a meal.

01:31:07.920 --> 01:31:10.500
We wanted to see, how
long does this last

01:31:10.500 --> 01:31:12.390
and where does it peak, right?

01:31:12.390 --> 01:31:14.380
And so my thought was, OK.

01:31:14.380 --> 01:31:18.450
Well, it'll probably
track with plasma leucine.

01:31:18.450 --> 01:31:21.330
For those that are
not familiar, leucine

01:31:21.330 --> 01:31:25.030
is the amino acid that
is almost exclusively

01:31:25.030 --> 01:31:28.060
responsible for increasing
muscle protein synthesis when

01:31:28.060 --> 01:31:29.000
you eat protein.

01:31:29.000 --> 01:31:31.760
So it's one of the
branched chain amino acids.

01:31:31.760 --> 01:31:35.360
So we wanted to see, OK, how
long does this effect last?

01:31:35.360 --> 01:31:38.410
So we fed these
animals whey protein,

01:31:38.410 --> 01:31:41.440
and again, I thought, OK, well,
however long plasma leucine,

01:31:41.440 --> 01:31:46.023
stay up that, will be how long
protein synthesis stays up.

01:31:46.023 --> 01:31:47.940
And so we got the protein
synthesis data back,

01:31:47.940 --> 01:31:50.370
and it was peaked at
90 minutes-- or sorry.

01:31:50.370 --> 01:31:52.980
Peaked from 45 to
90 minutes and, then

01:31:52.980 --> 01:31:55.020
was back down to
baseline by 180 minutes.

01:31:55.020 --> 01:31:58.600
And so when I went to do
the plasma leucine analysis,

01:31:58.600 --> 01:32:02.560
my shock was, at three hours,
plasma leucine was still

01:32:02.560 --> 01:32:04.310
plateaued out.

01:32:04.310 --> 01:32:07.320
And then I said,
OK, well, when I

01:32:07.320 --> 01:32:09.990
look at the initiation factors,
that will show me something.

01:32:09.990 --> 01:32:13.320
So for those not
familiar, this is part

01:32:13.320 --> 01:32:15.100
of the mTOR signaling pathway.

01:32:15.100 --> 01:32:17.430
So one of the--
two of the targets

01:32:17.430 --> 01:32:20.910
of mTOR, when it's stimulated--
and leucine stimulates mTOR.

01:32:20.910 --> 01:32:26.370
Two of the targets of mTOR are a
protein compound called 4E-BP1.

01:32:26.370 --> 01:32:31.847
And then another one is called
a ribosomal protein S6K.

01:32:31.847 --> 01:32:33.930
So I don't want to get
into the specifics about it

01:32:33.930 --> 01:32:35.513
because it's going
to be on the scope.

01:32:35.513 --> 01:32:39.390
But basically, when these things
are phosphorylated by mTOR,

01:32:39.390 --> 01:32:43.620
it increases the rate of
translation initiation, which

01:32:43.620 --> 01:32:45.420
translation initiation
is, basically,

01:32:45.420 --> 01:32:48.630
the process of the ribosome
hooking on to the mRNA

01:32:48.630 --> 01:32:51.580
and then starting
protein synthesis.

01:32:51.580 --> 01:32:55.780
So I was looking at
the phosphorylation

01:32:55.780 --> 01:32:58.372
of 4E-BP1 and RPS6.

01:32:58.372 --> 01:32:59.830
I was like, OK,
well, I'll probably

01:32:59.830 --> 01:33:02.500
see these things come
down in three hours.

01:33:02.500 --> 01:33:04.240
Still plateaued.

01:33:04.240 --> 01:33:07.050
And so then it was like,
what's going on here?

01:33:07.050 --> 01:33:09.550
So I actually kept rerunning
the data and rerunning the data

01:33:09.550 --> 01:33:10.540
and rerunning the data.

01:33:10.540 --> 01:33:12.845
And I'll never forget, I
went into Lehman's office.

01:33:12.845 --> 01:33:14.470
And this is like six
months after we've

01:33:14.470 --> 01:33:16.762
done this study, because
this analysis takes time.

01:33:16.762 --> 01:33:18.970
I was like, so where are we
with this duration study?

01:33:18.970 --> 01:33:21.178
I said, well, I just got to
run the plasma data again

01:33:21.178 --> 01:33:22.690
because it's not right.

01:33:22.690 --> 01:33:25.580
And he's like, well,
why is it not right?

01:33:25.580 --> 01:33:27.370
And I said, well,
it just doesn't

01:33:27.370 --> 01:33:28.960
make any sense you know.

01:33:28.960 --> 01:33:33.340
And I kind of went through,
he's like well, describe to me

01:33:33.340 --> 01:33:34.060
your technique.

01:33:34.060 --> 01:33:35.018
How are you doing this?

01:33:35.018 --> 01:33:36.830
And I described it,
and he said, well,

01:33:36.830 --> 01:33:37.980
how is your standard error?

01:33:37.980 --> 01:33:40.090
And I told him what the
numbers were, he said,

01:33:40.090 --> 01:33:41.470
it sounds like it's good data.

01:33:41.470 --> 01:33:44.110
He said, it sounds
like you are trying

01:33:44.110 --> 01:33:46.840
to get the data to
fit your conclusion

01:33:46.840 --> 01:33:49.810
and you need to change your
conclusion to fit the data.

01:33:49.810 --> 01:33:51.530
And that statement--

01:33:51.530 --> 01:33:53.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This
is why we do PhDs.

01:33:53.350 --> 01:33:54.250
LAYNE NORTON: This
is why, yeah--

01:33:54.250 --> 01:33:55.015
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You
need an advisor--

01:33:55.015 --> 01:33:58.150
LAYNE NORTON: This is why I
am so skeptical of everything,

01:33:58.150 --> 01:34:02.050
because I have had so many of
my ideas crushed by my own data,

01:34:02.050 --> 01:34:02.860
right?

01:34:02.860 --> 01:34:06.775
So we actually ended up--

01:34:06.775 --> 01:34:08.950
this kind of effect,
this phenomenon

01:34:08.950 --> 01:34:13.190
is called muscle protein
synthetic refractory period.

01:34:13.190 --> 01:34:16.480
So basically, like once
you trigger the system,

01:34:16.480 --> 01:34:18.010
runs for a defined
period of time

01:34:18.010 --> 01:34:21.247
and then it takes time
to, essentially, reset

01:34:21.247 --> 01:34:22.330
for lack of a better term.

01:34:22.330 --> 01:34:26.290
It's also been referred to
as the muscle full effect.

01:34:26.290 --> 01:34:28.180
So I looked at that
and said, why am I

01:34:28.180 --> 01:34:30.460
eating every two hours then?

01:34:30.460 --> 01:34:34.360
And there was even a study
out of Wolf's Lab back in '99,

01:34:34.360 --> 01:34:37.180
I think, where they infused
essential amino acids

01:34:37.180 --> 01:34:38.380
for six hours.

01:34:38.380 --> 01:34:41.000
Protein synthesis went
up, peaked at 60 minutes,

01:34:41.000 --> 01:34:43.570
came back down 120, and
never went back up again.

01:34:43.570 --> 01:34:45.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Maybe I'm being naive.

01:34:45.550 --> 01:34:50.530
But I would have thought that
if protein synthesis goes

01:34:50.530 --> 01:34:53.740
up and then comes back down,
that eating more often would

01:34:53.740 --> 01:34:56.350
be exactly the thing you would
want to do if your goal was

01:34:56.350 --> 01:34:58.510
to get increased protein
synthesis because you'd

01:34:58.510 --> 01:35:00.760
be pinging the
system periodically.

01:35:00.760 --> 01:35:03.328
LAYNE NORTON: But the problem
is, the plasma amino acids

01:35:03.328 --> 01:35:04.120
are still elevated.

01:35:04.120 --> 01:35:04.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
it's, essentially,

01:35:04.900 --> 01:35:07.090
like eating the whole way
through from the perspective

01:35:07.090 --> 01:35:07.510
of leucine.

01:35:07.510 --> 01:35:08.455
LAYNE NORTON: From
the cells, yeah.

01:35:08.455 --> 01:35:08.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

01:35:08.680 --> 01:35:10.600
LAYNE NORTON: So that was
one of those things where

01:35:10.600 --> 01:35:11.230
I said, you know what?

01:35:11.230 --> 01:35:13.397
I'm actually going to eat
less often, because if I'm

01:35:13.397 --> 01:35:16.480
eating in three hours
later, I've still got capped

01:35:16.480 --> 01:35:19.540
out plasma amino acids.

01:35:19.540 --> 01:35:21.040
And we looked at
all kinds of stuff.

01:35:21.040 --> 01:35:23.740
We looked at intracellular
leucine just to make sure that

01:35:23.740 --> 01:35:25.450
wasn't falling off, it wasn't.

01:35:25.450 --> 01:35:28.120
We looked at all the plasma,
essential amino acids

01:35:28.120 --> 01:35:31.480
because we were thinking,
well, maybe protein synthesis

01:35:31.480 --> 01:35:34.390
is sucking some of these
amino acids out of the plasma

01:35:34.390 --> 01:35:36.280
and they're dropping
and that's causing it

01:35:36.280 --> 01:35:38.020
to short circuit the system.

01:35:38.020 --> 01:35:39.560
That wasn't the case.

01:35:39.560 --> 01:35:43.120
It just-- essentially,
what the evidence suggests,

01:35:43.120 --> 01:35:45.195
I think we're the only
ones to show this so far.

01:35:45.195 --> 01:35:47.320
So I'm not ready to say
that this is a real effect,

01:35:47.320 --> 01:35:51.050
because I hold out the idea
that data artifacts do exist

01:35:51.050 --> 01:35:53.480
and you can't be totally sure.

01:35:53.480 --> 01:35:58.430
But we saw an
increase in AMP kinase

01:35:58.430 --> 01:36:02.870
around this mark where protein
synthesis started falling off.

01:36:02.870 --> 01:36:06.620
And we also saw a decrease
in intracellular ATP.

01:36:06.620 --> 01:36:10.440
And protein synthesis is
an ATP-dependent process.

01:36:10.440 --> 01:36:14.510
And so what we think
might be happening is,

01:36:14.510 --> 01:36:19.800
you're consuming
protein and you're--

01:36:19.800 --> 01:36:22.680
you're increasing muscle
protein synthesis,

01:36:22.680 --> 01:36:25.200
and then at a
certain point, it has

01:36:25.200 --> 01:36:29.640
enough effect on your energy
metabolism in your cells

01:36:29.640 --> 01:36:32.130
that it short--

01:36:32.130 --> 01:36:36.060
not short circuits it,
but it cuts it off, right?

01:36:36.060 --> 01:36:38.490
So again, we're the
only ones to show that.

01:36:38.490 --> 01:36:39.540
That I'm aware of.

01:36:39.540 --> 01:36:41.100
And that was again, in rats.

01:36:41.100 --> 01:36:44.310
So I always talk about data.

01:36:44.310 --> 01:36:47.250
Like, there's data I'm
willing to bet my toe on,

01:36:47.250 --> 01:36:49.440
my foot on, my leg
on, and my life on.

01:36:49.440 --> 01:36:52.270
I'd probably barely bet the end
of my little toe on that one.

01:36:52.270 --> 01:36:53.135
I'm not quite sure.

01:36:53.135 --> 01:36:54.510
But it's interesting
nonetheless.

01:36:54.510 --> 01:36:54.795
So--

01:36:54.795 --> 01:36:56.545
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A
proportional wait here.

01:36:56.545 --> 01:36:58.920
LAYNE NORTON: --that's a
great example of, OK, we're

01:36:58.920 --> 01:37:01.410
looking at this mechanism
of mTOR signaling,

01:37:01.410 --> 01:37:03.420
and if we just looked
at that, we'd say,

01:37:03.420 --> 01:37:05.253
oh, well, protein
synthesis is going to stay

01:37:05.253 --> 01:37:07.290
elevated for past three hours.

01:37:07.290 --> 01:37:09.120
But that's not what we saw.

01:37:09.120 --> 01:37:10.110
So yeah.

01:37:10.110 --> 01:37:13.020
I think, it's, again, that's
why I really try to get people

01:37:13.020 --> 01:37:14.070
to say, well, let's--

01:37:14.070 --> 01:37:16.257
mechanisms are important.

01:37:16.257 --> 01:37:18.090
And especially, if
you're seeing an outcome,

01:37:18.090 --> 01:37:19.770
it's important to
identify mechanisms

01:37:19.770 --> 01:37:21.360
that may explain that.

01:37:21.360 --> 01:37:23.340
But let's step back
from the mechanisms

01:37:23.340 --> 01:37:25.000
from trying to chase mechanisms.

01:37:25.000 --> 01:37:26.790
And let's look at
chasing outcomes

01:37:26.790 --> 01:37:28.965
in terms of what we
recommend to people.

01:37:28.965 --> 01:37:30.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Excellent point.

01:37:30.600 --> 01:37:33.600
In terms of chasing outcomes,
a number of people I know

01:37:33.600 --> 01:37:36.480
are interested in weight
loss or weight maintenance.

01:37:36.480 --> 01:37:39.450
And several times throughout
today's conversation,

01:37:39.450 --> 01:37:42.000
we've come back to this
issue of satiety signals.

01:37:42.000 --> 01:37:44.042
Whether or not there are
brain-based, body-based,

01:37:44.042 --> 01:37:44.895
or both.

01:37:44.895 --> 01:37:47.460
Not wanting to eat
more is a great way

01:37:47.460 --> 01:37:49.860
to maintain or lose
weight because you simply

01:37:49.860 --> 01:37:51.430
don't want to.

01:37:51.430 --> 01:37:54.520
I heard you mention
earlier that protein

01:37:54.520 --> 01:37:57.610
and maybe specific types of
protein or sources of protein

01:37:57.610 --> 01:38:00.400
may provide better
satiety signals

01:38:00.400 --> 01:38:03.130
than other macronutrients.

01:38:03.130 --> 01:38:06.610
Could you briefly talk
about how macronutrients,

01:38:06.610 --> 01:38:10.330
including protein, but also
carbohydrates and fats,

01:38:10.330 --> 01:38:11.830
impact satiety.

01:38:11.830 --> 01:38:14.650
And from the
standpoint of somebody

01:38:14.650 --> 01:38:17.290
who, for instance, would
like to quote unquote, "lose

01:38:17.290 --> 01:38:18.250
a few pounds," right?

01:38:18.250 --> 01:38:21.040
Probably would be happy to gain
a little bit of lean body mass

01:38:21.040 --> 01:38:22.930
provided it was in a
particular location

01:38:22.930 --> 01:38:25.090
on their body, that
seems to be a thing now.

01:38:25.090 --> 01:38:27.130
Directed hypertrophy,
if you will.

01:38:27.130 --> 01:38:33.590
And how much they should focus
on protein as a core component

01:38:33.590 --> 01:38:35.790
of creating this diet.

01:38:35.790 --> 01:38:37.918
Assuming everything else
is being done correctly.

01:38:37.918 --> 01:38:39.960
They're going to hit the
right number of calories

01:38:39.960 --> 01:38:45.030
relative to their
output NEAT, et cetera.

01:38:45.030 --> 01:38:47.310
How should we think about
protein and satiety signals?

01:38:47.310 --> 01:38:50.490
And are animal sources
of protein, indeed,

01:38:50.490 --> 01:38:51.870
more bioavailable?

01:38:51.870 --> 01:38:54.750
That's a tricky word.

01:38:54.750 --> 01:38:56.370
For sake of muscle
building, but also

01:38:56.370 --> 01:38:59.040
for sake of somebody who just
would like to lose body fat.

01:38:59.040 --> 01:39:00.420
They don't want to
lose muscle and they'd

01:39:00.420 --> 01:39:01.905
like to bring their
weight down a few pounds.

01:39:01.905 --> 01:39:03.150
LAYNE NORTON: So
a lot of things--

01:39:03.150 --> 01:39:03.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --or more.

01:39:03.690 --> 01:39:03.870
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:39:03.870 --> 01:39:05.790
A lot of things to unpack there.

01:39:05.790 --> 01:39:07.830
Of the macronutrients,
protein is definitely

01:39:07.830 --> 01:39:10.592
the biggest lever that you
can pull, because even if--

01:39:10.592 --> 01:39:13.050
it doesn't take a ton of protein
to get a lot of the muscle

01:39:13.050 --> 01:39:13.800
building benefits.

01:39:13.800 --> 01:39:15.570
I mean, I think
the benefits really

01:39:15.570 --> 01:39:18.630
start to plateau out around
1.6 grams per kilogram

01:39:18.630 --> 01:39:19.530
of body weight.

01:39:19.530 --> 01:39:21.570
There's some evidence
that maybe even

01:39:21.570 --> 01:39:25.080
up to like 2.4 or
2.8 grams per kilo

01:39:25.080 --> 01:39:27.492
may give a little
bit more benefit.

01:39:27.492 --> 01:39:28.950
I think it probably
looks something

01:39:28.950 --> 01:39:30.960
like an asymptote
in terms of a curve

01:39:30.960 --> 01:39:33.670
where as you put
more into the system,

01:39:33.670 --> 01:39:35.070
you always get a
little bit more,

01:39:35.070 --> 01:39:37.528
but it just gets to the point
where it's so infinitesimally

01:39:37.528 --> 01:39:41.055
small benefit that it's for all
intensive purposes, no benefit.

01:39:41.055 --> 01:39:43.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But you
mentioned 1.6 grams of protein

01:39:43.980 --> 01:39:45.780
per kilogram of body
weight, would you

01:39:45.780 --> 01:39:48.120
consider that a threshold
that most people

01:39:48.120 --> 01:39:50.130
should try and achieve daily?

01:39:50.130 --> 01:39:52.980
LAYNE NORTON: I see very few
downsides to hitting that.

01:39:52.980 --> 01:39:54.660
I mean, I know some
people-- and this

01:39:54.660 --> 01:39:56.020
is going to get into a
separate conversation.

01:39:56.020 --> 01:39:57.070
But I know some
people will say, well,

01:39:57.070 --> 01:39:59.235
I don't want to stimulate
mTOR because that's

01:39:59.235 --> 01:40:00.360
going to make me die early.

01:40:00.360 --> 01:40:02.100
And I think, one of
the things to keep

01:40:02.100 --> 01:40:05.750
in mind is if you look at--

01:40:05.750 --> 01:40:07.250
there's kind of
this thought process

01:40:07.250 --> 01:40:09.000
out there that if
you're stimulating mTOR,

01:40:09.000 --> 01:40:11.000
that protein is going
to make you die early.

01:40:11.000 --> 01:40:13.460
And first off, we have
very little human outcome

01:40:13.460 --> 01:40:15.380
data to support that claim.

01:40:15.380 --> 01:40:17.210
And the second thing
is, if you look

01:40:17.210 --> 01:40:18.760
at any macronutrient
isolation, I

01:40:18.760 --> 01:40:21.260
can make a mechanistic argument
that it's going to kill you.

01:40:21.260 --> 01:40:24.380
So fat-- if you take
in fat and it decreases

01:40:24.380 --> 01:40:25.970
flow-mediated dilation.

01:40:25.970 --> 01:40:29.450
Flow-mediated dilation is
important for heart health

01:40:29.450 --> 01:40:31.160
in the short term.

01:40:31.160 --> 01:40:33.650
Carbohydrates stimulate insulin.

01:40:33.650 --> 01:40:36.440
Insulin, you know,
pro-inflammatory

01:40:36.440 --> 01:40:37.770
and all these other things.

01:40:37.770 --> 01:40:41.390
And so I can make an argument
for any single macronutrient

01:40:41.390 --> 01:40:45.785
to be negative for longevity.

01:40:45.785 --> 01:40:46.970
I really want people--

01:40:46.970 --> 01:40:49.040
this is something that
even scientists get wrong.

01:40:49.040 --> 01:40:51.410
They look at an acute
response of something

01:40:51.410 --> 01:40:54.470
and assume that is going to
relate to long term outcomes

01:40:54.470 --> 01:40:56.680
and signaling.

01:40:56.680 --> 01:41:00.828
So let's just take
exercise, for example.

01:41:00.828 --> 01:41:02.620
If you didn't know
anything about exercise,

01:41:02.620 --> 01:41:05.078
and I said to you, Andrew,
I'm going to do something

01:41:05.078 --> 01:41:07.120
that's going to make you--
your heart rate go up,

01:41:07.120 --> 01:41:10.360
your blood pressure go up, your
inflammatory markers go up,

01:41:10.360 --> 01:41:15.330
your reactive oxygen species
increase, you're going to say--

01:41:15.330 --> 01:41:18.090
and it's going to damage your
muscles, you're going to say,

01:41:18.090 --> 01:41:18.960
I'm not doing that.

01:41:18.960 --> 01:41:21.150
That sounds horrible.

01:41:21.150 --> 01:41:23.760
But it does all those
things in the short term.

01:41:23.760 --> 01:41:26.730
But what is the long
term effect of exercise?

01:41:26.730 --> 01:41:28.470
You actually get healthier.

01:41:28.470 --> 01:41:30.360
All those things improve.

01:41:30.360 --> 01:41:33.360
Now, I'm not saying that
protein is a longevity hack

01:41:33.360 --> 01:41:35.820
or anything like that,
but what I'm saying is,

01:41:35.820 --> 01:41:37.350
I think some of
the arguments out

01:41:37.350 --> 01:41:40.050
there are based on mechanistic--

01:41:40.050 --> 01:41:43.380
this increases mTOR, therefore
we don't want to do it.

01:41:43.380 --> 01:41:46.920
I think it is a much more
complicated argument than just

01:41:46.920 --> 01:41:47.860
that.

01:41:47.860 --> 01:41:48.720
So there's that.

01:41:48.720 --> 01:41:50.130
So protein is the biggest lever.

01:41:50.130 --> 01:41:53.110
I would shoot for 1.6
grams per kilogram.

01:41:53.110 --> 01:41:54.370
If you can do more, great.

01:41:54.370 --> 01:41:56.370
There doesn't seem to be
really downsides to it.

01:41:56.370 --> 01:41:58.650
Even up to very high
levels of protein.

01:41:58.650 --> 01:42:00.900
Jose Antonio did
a study, that was

01:42:00.900 --> 01:42:03.580
a year-long randomized
control trial.

01:42:03.580 --> 01:42:04.830
And again, it's just one year.

01:42:04.830 --> 01:42:08.070
But they were looking at all
sorts of different biomarkers.

01:42:08.070 --> 01:42:11.550
And basically, even up to like
4 grams per kilogram of protein,

01:42:11.550 --> 01:42:13.800
they couldn't really find
any negative health outcomes

01:42:13.800 --> 01:42:17.310
from it other than people were
just so satiated, they ended up

01:42:17.310 --> 01:42:19.320
eating less calories.

01:42:19.320 --> 01:42:23.760
So protein is a big
lever, because one, it

01:42:23.760 --> 01:42:25.260
has a higher thermic
effect of food,

01:42:25.260 --> 01:42:27.593
so you're getting a little
bit more calorie burn per day

01:42:27.593 --> 01:42:29.735
even though it's not
a ton because TEF

01:42:29.735 --> 01:42:31.860
is a pretty small percentage
of your overall energy

01:42:31.860 --> 01:42:32.850
expenditure.

01:42:32.850 --> 01:42:34.560
But still a benefit.

01:42:34.560 --> 01:42:36.600
You're getting the
effects on lean body mass.

01:42:36.600 --> 01:42:38.640
If you're in a diet, it's going
to help preserve lean body

01:42:38.640 --> 01:42:39.960
mass, if you're at
maintenance, it's

01:42:39.960 --> 01:42:41.910
going to help build a
preserved lean body mass,

01:42:41.910 --> 01:42:43.493
and if you're in a
surplus, it's going

01:42:43.493 --> 01:42:46.250
to help build a
preserved lean body mass.

01:42:46.250 --> 01:42:47.960
Then you get the
effects on appetite.

01:42:47.960 --> 01:42:54.070
So now, I want to be careful
because appetite effects tend

01:42:54.070 --> 01:42:58.240
to be very specific
to individual foods.

01:42:58.240 --> 01:43:00.910
So you can take a
high protein food

01:43:00.910 --> 01:43:06.938
and make it not very satiating.

01:43:06.938 --> 01:43:08.980
So take, for example, like
a really tasty protein

01:43:08.980 --> 01:43:11.800
bar, which back when we
were getting into this,

01:43:11.800 --> 01:43:13.355
no such thing existed.

01:43:13.355 --> 01:43:15.980
Now, you have protein bars that
actually take pretty darn good.

01:43:15.980 --> 01:43:19.360
But if you eat one of them, I
mean, are you really satiated?

01:43:19.360 --> 01:43:21.730
I don't really feel satiated,
that's my protein bar.

01:43:21.730 --> 01:43:22.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's
my premeal snack.

01:43:22.840 --> 01:43:23.673
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

01:43:23.673 --> 01:43:24.460
Right.

01:43:24.460 --> 01:43:26.290
So why?

01:43:26.290 --> 01:43:29.020
Because, I mean, it's
processed, refined,

01:43:29.020 --> 01:43:32.200
and made to be very palatable.

01:43:32.200 --> 01:43:34.900
But take something like a
200-gram chicken breast.

01:43:34.900 --> 01:43:36.945
Very satiating, right?

01:43:36.945 --> 01:43:39.070
And that's why when people
say, well, carbohydrates

01:43:39.070 --> 01:43:41.890
aren't very satiating, it
depends on the carbohydrate.

01:43:41.890 --> 01:43:45.430
I mean, when you look
at the satiety index,

01:43:45.430 --> 01:43:48.895
a plain baked potato is about
as satiating as it gets.

01:43:48.895 --> 01:43:51.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: If I
eat a bowl of oatmeal,

01:43:51.370 --> 01:43:52.648
I feel pretty good afterward.

01:43:52.648 --> 01:43:53.440
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:43:53.440 --> 01:43:54.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
For a while, right?

01:43:54.310 --> 01:43:55.128
I mean, I usually--

01:43:55.128 --> 01:43:56.920
I'll eat that along
with some other things,

01:43:56.920 --> 01:44:01.198
but I completely agree.

01:44:01.198 --> 01:44:03.490
So you're saying that the
form that it comes in, maybe,

01:44:03.490 --> 01:44:06.020
even how much chewing
is required, how good.

01:44:06.020 --> 01:44:08.710
It smells that your
psychological associations--

01:44:08.710 --> 01:44:13.350
because to me, a steak
is an incredible meal.

01:44:13.350 --> 01:44:15.900
I mean, if I had to pick
one food that I could eat,

01:44:15.900 --> 01:44:17.490
even though I'm
not pure carnivore,

01:44:17.490 --> 01:44:19.410
for the rest of my life, it
would be that because, I think,

01:44:19.410 --> 01:44:20.670
it would get me
where I need to go

01:44:20.670 --> 01:44:22.728
and then I'd probably
have to sneak some fiber.

01:44:22.728 --> 01:44:23.520
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:44:23.520 --> 01:44:26.110
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
it's an excellent point.

01:44:26.110 --> 01:44:28.800
I have a question that I don't
want to take us off track,

01:44:28.800 --> 01:44:31.980
but I'm hoping it relates enough
that you could answer it now.

01:44:31.980 --> 01:44:34.140
In the context of
this, if I'm going

01:44:34.140 --> 01:44:37.920
to eat let's say, 2 grams per
kilogram of body weight protein

01:44:37.920 --> 01:44:40.140
and I'm not eating
multiple meals,

01:44:40.140 --> 01:44:42.390
maybe I'm eating two
or three meals per day,

01:44:42.390 --> 01:44:44.040
I'm certainly going
to be eating more

01:44:44.040 --> 01:44:46.418
than the 30-gram threshold
that was thrown around

01:44:46.418 --> 01:44:48.210
for a long time that
we can only assimilate

01:44:48.210 --> 01:44:49.692
30 grams of protein per meal.

01:44:49.692 --> 01:44:51.150
Should I just not
worry about that?

01:44:51.150 --> 01:44:53.520
Some of it is going to go
towards the thermic effect

01:44:53.520 --> 01:44:55.830
of food, some of that
might be converted

01:44:55.830 --> 01:44:58.840
into glucose of all things
through gluconeogenesis.

01:44:58.840 --> 01:45:01.140
So should I worry about
this 30-gram cut off?

01:45:01.140 --> 01:45:04.800
Because I think balancing the
1.6 gram per kilogram body

01:45:04.800 --> 01:45:06.600
weight threshold
with number of meals,

01:45:06.600 --> 01:45:08.880
with the need to exercise
and work and live my life

01:45:08.880 --> 01:45:10.500
and sleep, et
cetera, pretty soon,

01:45:10.500 --> 01:45:13.020
you run into bottlenecks
where you just can't do it

01:45:13.020 --> 01:45:15.270
or you're spending so much
time trying to focus on it.

01:45:15.270 --> 01:45:16.680
LAYNE NORTON: You can't
optimize all the things

01:45:16.680 --> 01:45:17.415
at the same time.

01:45:17.415 --> 01:45:19.582
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You'd lose
your mind and your body.

01:45:19.582 --> 01:45:25.920
So what is necessary
in terms of frequency

01:45:25.920 --> 01:45:28.170
if one is getting
enough protein?

01:45:28.170 --> 01:45:30.570
And then tied into
that question,

01:45:30.570 --> 01:45:33.300
is there any
reality to this idea

01:45:33.300 --> 01:45:36.360
that if you eat one meal
per day or you're fasting

01:45:36.360 --> 01:45:40.380
and then you eat, let's
say, 200 grams of protein

01:45:40.380 --> 01:45:44.700
in a single feeding, that you
can assimilate more because you

01:45:44.700 --> 01:45:47.070
were protein starved.

01:45:47.070 --> 01:45:48.545
Is that a real thing?

01:45:48.545 --> 01:45:52.050
LAYNE NORTON: So most of
the studies with protein

01:45:52.050 --> 01:45:56.475
are after a fast because to
assess it with stable isotope,

01:45:56.475 --> 01:45:57.850
you have to be in
a steady state.

01:45:57.850 --> 01:46:01.050
So we haven't observed that.

01:46:01.050 --> 01:46:03.750
It doesn't appear
that fasting really

01:46:03.750 --> 01:46:07.320
allows you to assimilate
more protein after a fast.

01:46:07.320 --> 01:46:09.540
So this gets into a core
of one of the things I

01:46:09.540 --> 01:46:11.790
looked at in my
PhD, which is, does

01:46:11.790 --> 01:46:13.110
protein distribution matter?

01:46:13.110 --> 01:46:17.430
Because most Americans get about
65% to 70% of their protein

01:46:17.430 --> 01:46:18.450
at dinner.

01:46:18.450 --> 01:46:21.623
Breakfast tends to be pretty
minimal protein foods.

01:46:21.623 --> 01:46:23.415
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do
any cultures, actually,

01:46:23.415 --> 01:46:26.660
eat big breakfast and not
a big lunch and dinner?

01:46:26.660 --> 01:46:28.400
We all heard that was ideal.

01:46:28.400 --> 01:46:30.400
We'll get into circadian
timing in a little bit,

01:46:30.400 --> 01:46:31.920
but does anyone
actually do that?

01:46:31.920 --> 01:46:33.510
Steak and eggs for
breakfast and then

01:46:33.510 --> 01:46:34.980
taper off the rest of the day?

01:46:34.980 --> 01:46:36.000
LAYNE NORTON: I
know German culture

01:46:36.000 --> 01:46:37.625
tends to have a big
breakfast, but also

01:46:37.625 --> 01:46:39.450
tends to be like sugary
foods and whatnot.

01:46:39.450 --> 01:46:43.990
As far as like teleological, I'm
actually not sure about that.

01:46:43.990 --> 01:46:46.560
So if you look at
that, and then you

01:46:46.560 --> 01:46:48.540
consider that protein
doesn't really

01:46:48.540 --> 01:46:50.640
have a storage mechanism.

01:46:50.640 --> 01:46:56.050
People will say, well, lean
tissue is a storage mechanism.

01:46:56.050 --> 01:46:59.950
That's like saying a house is
a storage facility for wood.

01:46:59.950 --> 01:47:01.780
Yeah, if the house
is made out of wood

01:47:01.780 --> 01:47:04.680
you could get wood out of it,
but that's not why you build.

01:47:04.680 --> 01:47:06.430
You're building the
house out of a demand.

01:47:06.430 --> 01:47:09.050
Same thing for muscle tissue.

01:47:09.050 --> 01:47:11.090
There is a free
pool of amino acids,

01:47:11.090 --> 01:47:12.178
but it's very, very small.

01:47:12.178 --> 01:47:14.470
So when you consider things
like fat, which, basically,

01:47:14.470 --> 01:47:18.700
has unlimited storage capacity,
carbohydrate, at relatively

01:47:18.700 --> 01:47:20.410
large storage
capacity, you can store

01:47:20.410 --> 01:47:24.220
400 or 500 grams of carbohydrate
between your liver and muscles.

01:47:24.220 --> 01:47:30.850
And then protein, which almost
has no storage capacity,

01:47:30.850 --> 01:47:33.340
the idea that, OK,
you could make up

01:47:33.340 --> 01:47:35.890
for a low protein at one
meal by over consuming

01:47:35.890 --> 01:47:38.600
another meal didn't
make sense to me.

01:47:38.600 --> 01:47:40.960
So one of the studies we did--

01:47:40.960 --> 01:47:46.000
and again, in rats, we took--

01:47:46.000 --> 01:47:49.550
both groups were getting whey
protein, so a high quality

01:47:49.550 --> 01:47:51.550
protein, they were getting
the exact same amount

01:47:51.550 --> 01:47:56.410
of calories, exact same amount
of nitrogen, exact same macros.

01:47:56.410 --> 01:47:57.410
Everything was the same.

01:47:57.410 --> 01:48:00.550
The only difference was,
one group, basically,

01:48:00.550 --> 01:48:04.540
got three meals of similar
amounts of protein.

01:48:04.540 --> 01:48:06.370
Dinner was a little
bit bigger, because we

01:48:06.370 --> 01:48:08.200
wanted to keep it somewhat
similar to how people eat.

01:48:08.200 --> 01:48:10.780
But each meal was going to be
over the threshold to stimulate

01:48:10.780 --> 01:48:12.920
muscle protein synthesis.

01:48:12.920 --> 01:48:15.960
Whereas the other
group, I constructed it

01:48:15.960 --> 01:48:17.780
so the first two
meals of the day

01:48:17.780 --> 01:48:20.100
should not stimulate
muscle protein synthesis.

01:48:20.100 --> 01:48:21.600
It should be under
that threshold.

01:48:21.600 --> 01:48:23.330
And then the last
meal was about 70%

01:48:23.330 --> 01:48:24.900
of their total daily protein.

01:48:24.900 --> 01:48:27.410
And so we had them eat
those for 11 weeks.

01:48:27.410 --> 01:48:28.790
And I'll never forget this.

01:48:28.790 --> 01:48:30.740
This is how obsessive
I became about it

01:48:30.740 --> 01:48:33.500
is, there were 110
animals in the study,

01:48:33.500 --> 01:48:37.730
and I made all the
diets and I weighed out

01:48:37.730 --> 01:48:40.460
exactly every single meal
for every single animal

01:48:40.460 --> 01:48:41.730
for 11 weeks.

01:48:41.730 --> 01:48:44.840
So I was in there at 6:00
AM, I was in there at noon,

01:48:44.840 --> 01:48:46.310
and I was in there at 6:00 PM.

01:48:46.310 --> 01:48:47.570
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love it.

01:48:47.570 --> 01:48:51.290
That's kind of PhD student
that professors dream of.

01:48:51.290 --> 01:48:52.685
You're sounding a dream student.

01:48:52.685 --> 01:48:55.280
LAYNE NORTON: So at
the end of 11 weeks,

01:48:55.280 --> 01:48:58.550
we looked at like lean body
mass, we looked at body fat,

01:48:58.550 --> 01:49:03.050
we looked at hind limb weights.

01:49:03.050 --> 01:49:05.960
We didn't really see
differences in lean body mass,

01:49:05.960 --> 01:49:07.695
but what we did see
was a difference

01:49:07.695 --> 01:49:08.570
in hind limb weights.

01:49:08.570 --> 01:49:11.000
It wasn't massive, but there
was a significant difference

01:49:11.000 --> 01:49:13.790
in the size of the
muscles of the hind limbs

01:49:13.790 --> 01:49:15.052
of these animals.

01:49:15.052 --> 01:49:16.760
And so it's interesting
that there wasn't

01:49:16.760 --> 01:49:18.320
a difference in lean body mass.

01:49:18.320 --> 01:49:22.370
And what we found, at least
with the liver, the animals

01:49:22.370 --> 01:49:28.490
that we're eating one meal
with really high protein,

01:49:28.490 --> 01:49:32.415
actually had bigger livers,
not like a huge amount and not

01:49:32.415 --> 01:49:34.040
something that I
would consider unsafe,

01:49:34.040 --> 01:49:37.140
but it was a statistically
significant difference.

01:49:37.140 --> 01:49:40.040
And so to me, at
least, like I'm trying

01:49:40.040 --> 01:49:42.080
to explain like no
difference in lean body mass,

01:49:42.080 --> 01:49:44.210
but a difference in
these hind limb weights,

01:49:44.210 --> 01:49:49.670
maybe there's some
sequestering of--

01:49:49.670 --> 01:49:52.640
that's fueling a little
bit more protein synthesis

01:49:52.640 --> 01:49:55.057
of the splenic
tissues rather than--

01:49:55.057 --> 01:49:57.140
because you're capping out
skeletal muscle protein

01:49:57.140 --> 01:49:58.130
synthesis.

01:49:58.130 --> 01:50:00.260
And we do know that the
splanchnic tissues are

01:50:00.260 --> 01:50:02.630
more sensitive or
have a greater rate

01:50:02.630 --> 01:50:03.860
of protein synthesis per day.

01:50:03.860 --> 01:50:06.050
Like, the rate of skeletal
muscle protein synthesis

01:50:06.050 --> 01:50:09.150
in humans is about
like 1% per day.

01:50:09.150 --> 01:50:12.920
So it takes like 100 days to
turn over skeletal muscle.

01:50:12.920 --> 01:50:15.740
Whereas like your entire
gut, your entire GI

01:50:15.740 --> 01:50:18.140
will turn itself over in
like two to three days.

01:50:18.140 --> 01:50:21.620
So really-- and the liver
also has a very high amount

01:50:21.620 --> 01:50:23.970
of protein synthesis, which
is one of the reasons.

01:50:23.970 --> 01:50:27.560
It's actually one of the most
metabolically active organs.

01:50:27.560 --> 01:50:30.110
So all that to say, it has not--

01:50:30.110 --> 01:50:33.882
there's been one human study
that showed something similar.

01:50:33.882 --> 01:50:36.090
And then there's been a
couple of others that didn't.

01:50:36.090 --> 01:50:39.140
And then in the intermittent
fasting studies, which is maybe

01:50:39.140 --> 01:50:44.400
a good tool to look at
compared to continuous feeding,

01:50:44.400 --> 01:50:47.510
one thing I will say is
it looks like the 16/8

01:50:47.510 --> 01:50:49.100
intermittent fasting style.

01:50:49.100 --> 01:50:52.250
There's been a couple of
studies with Grant Tinsley.

01:50:52.250 --> 01:50:54.520
And this is something I've
changed my mind on as well.

01:50:54.520 --> 01:50:56.270
Grant Tinsley has done
a couple of studies

01:50:56.270 --> 01:50:58.370
where they did use
the 16/8 protocol.

01:50:58.370 --> 01:51:01.370
They had them train during
their feeding window,

01:51:01.370 --> 01:51:02.510
and they had them eat--

01:51:02.510 --> 01:51:04.948
I think it was at least three
protein-containing meals

01:51:04.948 --> 01:51:05.990
during those eight hours.

01:51:05.990 --> 01:51:06.545
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
These are humans?

01:51:06.545 --> 01:51:07.880
LAYNE NORTON: These are humans.

01:51:07.880 --> 01:51:10.070
And they saw no difference
in lean body mass

01:51:10.070 --> 01:51:11.820
at the end of the study
compared to people

01:51:11.820 --> 01:51:16.440
who were eating as many times as
they wanted throughout the day.

01:51:16.440 --> 01:51:21.930
Now, if you look at some of the
more extreme forms of fasting,

01:51:21.930 --> 01:51:27.810
like alternate day fasting
or like 20/2 or 20/4,

01:51:27.810 --> 01:51:30.390
there are some studies where
you do start to see differences

01:51:30.390 --> 01:51:31.380
in lean body mass.

01:51:31.380 --> 01:51:35.760
So my suspicion is,
and I'm just guessing.

01:51:35.760 --> 01:51:37.440
So this is tenuous.

01:51:37.440 --> 01:51:39.270
My suspicion is,
probably if you're

01:51:39.270 --> 01:51:44.820
getting like two to three high
quality protein meals in a day,

01:51:44.820 --> 01:51:47.700
you're getting the vast majority
of the benefits of protein.

01:51:47.700 --> 01:51:50.520
The most important thing
is getting enough total.

01:51:50.520 --> 01:51:53.340
And then secondarily,
trying to get at least two

01:51:53.340 --> 01:51:58.090
or three meals with
high quality protein in.

01:51:58.090 --> 01:52:00.510
But if you're going pretty
extreme with like alternate day

01:52:00.510 --> 01:52:04.200
fasting or maybe
only one meal a day,

01:52:04.200 --> 01:52:07.020
then I think there may be some
effects on lean body mass.

01:52:07.020 --> 01:52:09.570
But again, these can be
mitigated as well if you're

01:52:09.570 --> 01:52:11.520
doing hard resistance training.

01:52:11.520 --> 01:52:14.930
Typically, that is
the biggest lever

01:52:14.930 --> 01:52:16.130
in terms of lean body mass.

01:52:16.130 --> 01:52:18.515
Yes, protein distribution
may make a difference,

01:52:18.515 --> 01:52:20.390
but I'm trying to put
it in context so people

01:52:20.390 --> 01:52:23.850
don't feel like they need to go
out and eat four meals a day.

01:52:23.850 --> 01:52:26.900
But again, so what I would say
is like some of the more milder

01:52:26.900 --> 01:52:29.150
forms of time-restricted
eating appear

01:52:29.150 --> 01:52:32.195
to be fine for lean
body mass, now,

01:52:32.195 --> 01:52:35.500
the caveat is the following.

01:52:35.500 --> 01:52:38.020
One of the nice things
about animal studies

01:52:38.020 --> 01:52:40.300
is when you
consider-- if you want

01:52:40.300 --> 01:52:44.690
to have a high subject
number, high level of control

01:52:44.690 --> 01:52:48.410
in a long duration, it's
pretty much your only option.

01:52:48.410 --> 01:52:52.460
So in our research
review reps, I

01:52:52.460 --> 01:52:54.470
created a Venn diagram,
which basically

01:52:54.470 --> 01:52:57.770
is like three circles
crossing over.

01:52:57.770 --> 01:53:03.110
One is study duration,
one is level of control,

01:53:03.110 --> 01:53:05.450
and the other one
is subject number.

01:53:05.450 --> 01:53:08.600
And to get all three of
those circles to cross over,

01:53:08.600 --> 01:53:10.265
it almost has to be an animal's.

01:53:10.265 --> 01:53:11.825
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:53:11.825 --> 01:53:14.060
And REPS, it's a
newsletter or a book?

01:53:14.060 --> 01:53:16.760
LAYNE NORTON: So it's our
monthly research review.

01:53:16.760 --> 01:53:21.020
So every month we review like
five studies that come out

01:53:21.020 --> 01:53:22.880
in fitness and nutrition.

01:53:22.880 --> 01:53:25.790
We'll usually do at least
one nutrition, one training,

01:53:25.790 --> 01:53:27.302
and one supplement per month.

01:53:27.302 --> 01:53:28.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
We will put a link

01:53:28.760 --> 01:53:30.260
to where people can sign up.

01:53:30.260 --> 01:53:31.800
This is a sign up format?

01:53:31.800 --> 01:53:32.300
Great.

01:53:32.300 --> 01:53:33.092
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

01:53:33.092 --> 01:53:35.060
So it's a
subscription-based service.

01:53:35.060 --> 01:53:41.527
So basically, when-- people
might look at my study, well,

01:53:41.527 --> 01:53:43.360
why did you see difference
in muscle weights

01:53:43.360 --> 01:53:46.780
whereas some of these other
studies don't see a difference?

01:53:46.780 --> 01:53:50.410
I weighed out every
single meal for 11 weeks,

01:53:50.410 --> 01:53:54.340
and keep in mind that 11
weeks in a rodent's lifespan

01:53:54.340 --> 01:53:56.470
is a really long time.

01:53:56.470 --> 01:53:59.050
That's about an 1/8 of
their total lifespan.

01:53:59.050 --> 01:54:03.580
So is it that there's
no effect or is it

01:54:03.580 --> 01:54:06.430
that the effect is
relatively small

01:54:06.430 --> 01:54:09.820
and would take a really long
time and very high level

01:54:09.820 --> 01:54:12.460
of control to see in humans?

01:54:12.460 --> 01:54:13.240
I don't know.

01:54:13.240 --> 01:54:17.730
But I think what I would say
relatively confidently is

01:54:17.730 --> 01:54:20.700
if you're going to do like
a 16/8 intermittent fasting,

01:54:20.700 --> 01:54:21.780
you're probably fine.

01:54:21.780 --> 01:54:25.140
Especially-- and again,
what is the goal?

01:54:25.140 --> 01:54:26.730
Like if you're a
bodybuilder looking

01:54:26.730 --> 01:54:29.640
to be the most massive
person you possibly can

01:54:29.640 --> 01:54:31.995
or you're a football
player or you're

01:54:31.995 --> 01:54:36.360
in some field that having as
much lean body mass as possible

01:54:36.360 --> 01:54:38.678
is really important for
you, then I would say,

01:54:38.678 --> 01:54:40.470
well, you're not really
gaining a whole lot

01:54:40.470 --> 01:54:42.240
by doing some form of
time-restricted eating.

01:54:42.240 --> 01:54:44.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think most
of the people listening to this

01:54:44.130 --> 01:54:45.345
do not fall into that category.

01:54:45.345 --> 01:54:45.720
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

01:54:45.720 --> 01:54:47.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think most
people want to maintain or lose

01:54:47.610 --> 01:54:48.240
weight.

01:54:48.240 --> 01:54:50.700
They'd like to,
perhaps, add a bit of,

01:54:50.700 --> 01:54:53.310
quote unquote, shape or muscle
to specific areas of their body

01:54:53.310 --> 01:54:54.277
and lose body fat.

01:54:54.277 --> 01:54:55.110
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

01:54:55.110 --> 01:54:57.357
And I think your normal
forms of time-restricted,

01:54:57.357 --> 01:54:59.190
and you're probably
perfectly fine for that.

01:54:59.190 --> 01:55:02.040
And again, I don't want
somebody to think, well,

01:55:02.040 --> 01:55:05.235
I do alternate day
fasting, there's

01:55:05.235 --> 01:55:07.110
no point to me resisting
training because I'm

01:55:07.110 --> 01:55:08.220
going to lose muscle mass.

01:55:08.220 --> 01:55:08.720
No.

01:55:08.720 --> 01:55:09.780
No, no, no, no.

01:55:09.780 --> 01:55:11.515
You can still build
muscle doing that.

01:55:11.515 --> 01:55:13.140
You just might not
build as much muscle

01:55:13.140 --> 01:55:15.610
as you would if you were eating
in a more traditional format.

01:55:15.610 --> 01:55:18.030
But if that's something that
works for you and your goals,

01:55:18.030 --> 01:55:20.940
and especially, if it's fat loss
or controlling your calories,

01:55:20.940 --> 01:55:24.420
then again, it's about the
hierarchy of what's important.

01:55:24.420 --> 01:55:28.920
So to answer your question, I do
think that timing and frequency

01:55:28.920 --> 01:55:30.840
matters a little--
not so much frequency,

01:55:30.840 --> 01:55:33.240
but distribution more so.

01:55:33.240 --> 01:55:34.740
So I think the
distribution matters,

01:55:34.740 --> 01:55:37.470
but it's a much smaller
lever than just getting

01:55:37.470 --> 01:55:39.030
enough total protein in.

01:55:39.030 --> 01:55:42.980
And then as far as
animal versus plant,

01:55:42.980 --> 01:55:45.170
I used to be in the
camp of, there's

01:55:45.170 --> 01:55:46.910
no way somebody can
build as much muscle

01:55:46.910 --> 01:55:48.050
on a plant-based diet.

01:55:48.050 --> 01:55:50.750
And now, I think I've
come back to, you can,

01:55:50.750 --> 01:55:53.000
it just requires a
little bit more planning.

01:55:53.000 --> 01:55:58.450
And I don't want to say
always, but it's very difficult

01:55:58.450 --> 01:56:01.132
to do without an isolated
source of protein.

01:56:01.132 --> 01:56:03.340
So unless you're going to
supplement with an isolated

01:56:03.340 --> 01:56:05.410
plant source of protein,
it's very difficult

01:56:05.410 --> 01:56:08.950
to get enough without going
over on total calories

01:56:08.950 --> 01:56:10.780
because you can figure
that, especially,

01:56:10.780 --> 01:56:13.420
like take somebody who may
be calorically restricted,

01:56:13.420 --> 01:56:16.000
trying to get enough protein
from whole intact plant

01:56:16.000 --> 01:56:16.520
sources.

01:56:16.520 --> 01:56:18.910
So you've got a few different
things working against you.

01:56:18.910 --> 01:56:21.700
One, the sources of protein
you're consuming also

01:56:21.700 --> 01:56:24.300
have carbohydrate and/or fat.

01:56:24.300 --> 01:56:27.760
Two, it's a less bioavailable
form of protein, and three,

01:56:27.760 --> 01:56:30.370
it's a lower quality of protein
in terms of it has, typically,

01:56:30.370 --> 01:56:32.470
less leucine, less
branched amino acids,

01:56:32.470 --> 01:56:34.590
and less essential amino acids.

01:56:34.590 --> 01:56:36.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You answer
the question that I almost

01:56:36.840 --> 01:56:38.257
interrupted you
to ask, which was,

01:56:38.257 --> 01:56:40.170
does it boil down to
the leucine content?

01:56:40.170 --> 01:56:43.350
And it sounds like that
is one of the components.

01:56:43.350 --> 01:56:46.020
And that a lot of the
vegan and vegetarian

01:56:46.020 --> 01:56:50.040
sources of excellent protein,
that excellent protein

01:56:50.040 --> 01:56:54.060
vegetarian or vegan source
is co-packaged with calories

01:56:54.060 --> 01:56:55.590
from carbohydrates
and/or fat that

01:56:55.590 --> 01:56:59.130
make it hard to stay under
the caloric threshold.

01:56:59.130 --> 01:57:00.210
Whereas a steak is--

01:57:00.210 --> 01:57:01.800
I'm not-- and
obviously, people might

01:57:01.800 --> 01:57:03.090
want to avoid that
for ethical reasons.

01:57:03.090 --> 01:57:03.600
LAYNE NORTON: Sure.

01:57:03.600 --> 01:57:05.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But that's
a different matter entirely.

01:57:05.010 --> 01:57:07.930
But a steak or a piece of
chicken or an egg is-- well,

01:57:07.930 --> 01:57:11.010
an egg has a yolk which
is, there's fat there.

01:57:11.010 --> 01:57:14.183
But is almost a pure
protein, fat source.

01:57:14.183 --> 01:57:15.975
There's no carbohydrate
along for the ride.

01:57:16.230 --> 01:57:17.063
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

01:57:17.063 --> 01:57:19.740
So I think what I would
say is that, you can do it.

01:57:19.740 --> 01:57:21.240
Takes a little more planning.

01:57:21.240 --> 01:57:24.867
And you're almost always-- if
you're a vegan, especially,

01:57:24.867 --> 01:57:26.700
you're going to be
better off like something

01:57:26.700 --> 01:57:30.060
with some isolated form
of protein or vegan

01:57:30.060 --> 01:57:30.760
form of protein.

01:57:30.760 --> 01:57:32.800
Now, this word gets
into people say, well,

01:57:32.800 --> 01:57:34.450
what about the
limiting amino acids

01:57:34.450 --> 01:57:36.180
and those sorts of things?

01:57:36.180 --> 01:57:38.310
It's a consideration.

01:57:38.310 --> 01:57:41.730
Some of the better
forms of vegan protein

01:57:41.730 --> 01:57:45.060
in terms of amino acid
content are like soy.

01:57:45.060 --> 01:57:47.670
Now, I hear everybody
screaming online

01:57:47.670 --> 01:57:49.230
about their testosterone levels.

01:57:49.230 --> 01:57:52.680
In terms of actual outcomes
and looking at testosterone,

01:57:52.680 --> 01:57:55.920
there was a recent meta
analysis looking at soy.

01:57:55.920 --> 01:58:00.030
And I think if it's your
only source of protein,

01:58:00.030 --> 01:58:01.680
then maybe the
dosage is high enough

01:58:01.680 --> 01:58:04.042
to cause some weird effects.

01:58:04.042 --> 01:58:06.250
But if you're just using it
like once or twice a day,

01:58:06.250 --> 01:58:08.292
it doesn't seem to have
an effect on testosterone

01:58:08.292 --> 01:58:12.060
or estrogen. So that can be
a decent source of protein,

01:58:12.060 --> 01:58:14.580
because it is a
complete protein source.

01:58:14.580 --> 01:58:18.150
It does have a PD cause of one,
which PD cause is basically

01:58:18.150 --> 01:58:23.250
a measure of protein
quality based on,

01:58:23.250 --> 01:58:26.290
does it provide enough
of all the amino acids

01:58:26.290 --> 01:58:27.550
so that none are limiting?

01:58:27.550 --> 01:58:30.480
And so soy is one of the only
vegan sources that does that.

01:58:30.480 --> 01:58:32.880
Interestingly, potato
protein isolate

01:58:32.880 --> 01:58:36.570
actually has a similar essential
amino acid content to whey.

01:58:36.570 --> 01:58:37.780
So isolated potato protein.

01:58:37.780 --> 01:58:39.030
It's just really hard to find.

01:58:39.030 --> 01:58:39.705
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

01:58:39.705 --> 01:58:40.620
LAYNE NORTON: I've
been trying to source--

01:58:40.620 --> 01:58:41.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
vegans take note,

01:58:41.490 --> 01:58:43.110
vegetarians take note, because--

01:58:43.110 --> 01:58:45.930
or vegans rather, because
whey is vegetarian,

01:58:45.930 --> 01:58:48.752
and whey is a very high
quality protein as far as--

01:58:48.752 --> 01:58:50.460
LAYNE NORTON: Very
high quality proteins.

01:58:50.460 --> 01:58:54.960
And actually-- they're actually
creating whey now out of--

01:58:54.960 --> 01:58:56.340
I think it's--

01:58:56.340 --> 01:58:57.493
I may mischaracterize this.

01:58:57.493 --> 01:58:59.910
But I believe they're able to
produce it out of like yeast

01:58:59.910 --> 01:59:00.840
or something like that.

01:59:00.840 --> 01:59:01.882
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Amazing.

01:59:01.882 --> 01:59:03.630
LAYNE NORTON: That--
so for vegans, now,

01:59:03.630 --> 01:59:05.220
this is a great
option because you

01:59:05.220 --> 01:59:07.980
can have whey that's
not animal-based

01:59:07.980 --> 01:59:11.370
that is going to be every bit
as good as an animal-based whey.

01:59:11.370 --> 01:59:14.300
So I think that's great.

01:59:14.300 --> 01:59:16.070
Getting to the leucine--

01:59:16.070 --> 01:59:17.090
let me go back, sorry.

01:59:17.090 --> 01:59:21.380
So another reason that using an
isolated protein can be helpful

01:59:21.380 --> 01:59:24.440
is because it's
more bioavailable

01:59:24.440 --> 01:59:26.360
as well when it's
been isolated out.

01:59:26.360 --> 01:59:29.960
When the protein bound up in
the actual plant material,

01:59:29.960 --> 01:59:31.550
it tends to be
less bioavailable.

01:59:31.550 --> 01:59:34.970
Now, cooking can help
increase the bioavailability

01:59:34.970 --> 01:59:37.070
because it breaks some of
those bonds and whatnot.

01:59:37.070 --> 01:59:39.750
But it still seems to be lower.

01:59:39.750 --> 01:59:42.570
And you don't-- it's
really interesting.

01:59:42.570 --> 01:59:47.190
There was a recent study where
they did a corn, wheat, and pea

01:59:47.190 --> 01:59:49.950
blend of protein versus whey.

01:59:49.950 --> 01:59:54.030
And basically, the
outcome was, 30 grams

01:59:54.030 --> 01:59:59.010
of each stimulated protein
synthesis to a similar degree.

01:59:59.010 --> 02:00:04.950
But the plasma amino acids
in the plant-based protein

02:00:04.950 --> 02:00:06.900
still did not get as
high as with whey.

02:00:06.900 --> 02:00:09.823
Now, it may be that that's
just-- it doesn't matter

02:00:09.823 --> 02:00:11.490
because once you get
to a certain level,

02:00:11.490 --> 02:00:13.737
you get all the benefits.

02:00:13.737 --> 02:00:15.570
But I still found it
interesting nonetheless

02:00:15.570 --> 02:00:17.028
that they didn't
quite get as high.

02:00:19.300 --> 02:00:22.390
The other thing to consider with
the vegan sources of protein

02:00:22.390 --> 02:00:23.600
is the leucine content.

02:00:23.600 --> 02:00:26.050
So one of the studies
we did was we looked

02:00:26.050 --> 02:00:30.150
at wheat, soy, egg, and whey.

02:00:30.150 --> 02:00:32.430
Isonitrogenous,
meaning we equated

02:00:32.430 --> 02:00:35.910
protein between the
groups, isocaloric,

02:00:35.910 --> 02:00:37.620
we equated calories.

02:00:37.620 --> 02:00:40.440
And we looked at muscle
protein synthesis.

02:00:40.440 --> 02:00:44.730
And I think this was-- the
meals were 15% of total energy

02:00:44.730 --> 02:00:45.352
from protein.

02:00:45.352 --> 02:00:50.250
So like your food guide
pyramid level of protein.

02:00:50.250 --> 02:00:53.280
And we saw that in the
wheat and soy group,

02:00:53.280 --> 02:00:55.440
they did not increase
muscle protein synthesis,

02:00:55.440 --> 02:00:58.230
but the egg and whey group
increased muscle protein

02:00:58.230 --> 02:01:00.130
synthesis.

02:01:00.130 --> 02:01:04.160
Now, what's really
interesting is we went back

02:01:04.160 --> 02:01:08.030
and we took wheat and added
free leucine to it to match

02:01:08.030 --> 02:01:11.180
the leucine content of whey and
the protein synthetic response

02:01:11.180 --> 02:01:12.600
was identical.

02:01:12.600 --> 02:01:16.520
So again, I don't like to
simplify things too much,

02:01:16.520 --> 02:01:21.680
but leucine appears to
really be driving this ship.

02:01:21.680 --> 02:01:25.640
And I'll never forget, Layman
called me into his office

02:01:25.640 --> 02:01:29.660
one day, and he would always do
these thought experiments of--

02:01:29.660 --> 02:01:32.182
he liked to think
about why something

02:01:32.182 --> 02:01:33.140
occurs the way it does.

02:01:33.140 --> 02:01:34.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Dangerous territory.

02:01:34.280 --> 02:01:35.072
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

02:01:35.072 --> 02:01:36.310
So he would say--

02:01:36.310 --> 02:01:37.810
one day, I'll never
forget, he said,

02:01:37.810 --> 02:01:40.880
Layne, why do you think the body
evolved to just sense leucine

02:01:40.880 --> 02:01:42.830
for muscle protein synthesis?

02:01:42.830 --> 02:01:46.700
And of course, I'm
like, I don't know, man.

02:01:46.700 --> 02:01:48.260
I just work here like.

02:01:48.260 --> 02:01:51.050
I'm just trying to get my PhD.

02:01:51.050 --> 02:01:52.760
And he said, well,
think about it.

02:01:52.760 --> 02:01:55.190
You would want something that
really wasn't extensively

02:01:55.190 --> 02:01:56.960
metabolized by
the gut and liver,

02:01:56.960 --> 02:01:59.990
because you would want to
show up in the blood in values

02:01:59.990 --> 02:02:02.000
that reflect what you just ate.

02:02:02.000 --> 02:02:03.140
Makes sense.

02:02:03.140 --> 02:02:05.833
And you would want it to
have passive diffusion

02:02:05.833 --> 02:02:07.250
across the muscle
cell because you

02:02:07.250 --> 02:02:08.910
want to be
concentration-dependent,

02:02:08.910 --> 02:02:10.095
which it is.

02:02:10.095 --> 02:02:10.595
So--

02:02:10.595 --> 02:02:12.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Get into
the tissues and cells

02:02:12.860 --> 02:02:13.610
that need it most.

02:02:13.610 --> 02:02:14.443
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

02:02:14.443 --> 02:02:17.963
So not having active transport,
but rather passive transport.

02:02:17.963 --> 02:02:19.880
So yeah, I thought that
was really interesting

02:02:19.880 --> 02:02:21.005
the way he broke that down.

02:02:21.005 --> 02:02:25.260
So few different options for
the vegan folks out there.

02:02:25.260 --> 02:02:27.487
You can use an isolated
source of protein.

02:02:27.487 --> 02:02:29.820
And again, like there's going
to be good options coming,

02:02:29.820 --> 02:02:31.410
because this plant-based
whey is going

02:02:31.410 --> 02:02:34.980
to be a great option for folks.

02:02:34.980 --> 02:02:37.860
You can add free leucine
to it, to whatever

02:02:37.860 --> 02:02:39.180
your source of protein is.

02:02:39.180 --> 02:02:41.400
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Just by
supplemented leucine powder.

02:02:41.400 --> 02:02:43.903
LAYNE NORTON: Now,
it tastes horrible.

02:02:43.903 --> 02:02:44.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:02:44.820 --> 02:02:45.820
I think I've heard that.

02:02:45.820 --> 02:02:46.920
Maybe I've even tried it.

02:02:46.920 --> 02:02:48.360
LAYNE NORTON: It's completely
non-polar, it does not not

02:02:48.360 --> 02:02:49.770
dissolve in anything
And it is gross.

02:02:49.770 --> 02:02:50.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Can it
be put into capsules?

02:02:50.630 --> 02:02:51.960
LAYNE NORTON: It can
be put in the capsules.

02:02:51.960 --> 02:02:52.140
Yeah.

02:02:52.140 --> 02:02:53.307
So you could take a capsule.

02:02:53.307 --> 02:02:56.490
Like for example, if you're
eating your normal meal,

02:02:56.490 --> 02:02:58.860
you could just take a capsule
of like 1 gram of leucine.

02:02:58.860 --> 02:03:01.440
Is probably going to bump
you up enough that you're

02:03:01.440 --> 02:03:03.880
going to be good to go.

02:03:03.880 --> 02:03:07.620
And then there's
options like blends--

02:03:07.620 --> 02:03:08.640
especially, with corn.

02:03:08.640 --> 02:03:10.590
Corn is actually
very high in leucine.

02:03:10.590 --> 02:03:12.330
That's a percentage
of its protein.

02:03:12.330 --> 02:03:14.670
Now, you've got to remember
like, you go eat corn

02:03:14.670 --> 02:03:17.170
on the cob and you're getting
like 2 grams of total protein.

02:03:17.170 --> 02:03:18.420
So it's not that much leucine.

02:03:18.420 --> 02:03:21.690
But if you isolate out the
protein, put it into a powder,

02:03:21.690 --> 02:03:25.020
well, now, when you're getting
like 80%, 90% of the weight

02:03:25.020 --> 02:03:27.660
is now protein, corn
is actually about 12%

02:03:27.660 --> 02:03:29.920
leucine in terms of the protein.

02:03:29.920 --> 02:03:33.270
So a great source of leucine, it
is like almost frank deficient

02:03:33.270 --> 02:03:34.555
in some other amino acids.

02:03:34.555 --> 02:03:36.930
But you can blend it with a
few other sources of protein.

02:03:36.930 --> 02:03:40.020
Like you could blend
it with a soy, a pea,

02:03:40.020 --> 02:03:42.690
and you can create these
complementary blends

02:03:42.690 --> 02:03:44.690
that would actually have
quite a bit of leucine,

02:03:44.690 --> 02:03:46.732
but also some of the other
essential amino acids.

02:03:46.732 --> 02:03:48.990
So there are options out
there for plant-based folks.

02:03:48.990 --> 02:03:51.510
And I mean, we have
seen people who

02:03:51.510 --> 02:03:54.810
are plant-based build
impressive amounts of muscle.

02:03:54.810 --> 02:03:57.060
There's quite a few bodybuilders
that are plant-based.

02:03:57.060 --> 02:03:59.602
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And a lot of
the endurance athletes like it.

02:03:59.602 --> 02:04:01.980
And even though-- when
we talk about muscle,

02:04:01.980 --> 02:04:04.530
we think about muscle
building often.

02:04:04.530 --> 02:04:06.480
Performance in endurance sports.

02:04:06.480 --> 02:04:08.880
And also just performance
for the typical person

02:04:08.880 --> 02:04:10.650
who's doing some
cardiovascular training,

02:04:10.650 --> 02:04:12.150
hopefully, some
resistance training.

02:04:12.150 --> 02:04:13.920
Also and just living life.

02:04:13.920 --> 02:04:16.050
I mean, many more
people now, it seems

02:04:16.050 --> 02:04:19.830
are vegan or at least avoiding
meat, in particular red meat.

02:04:19.830 --> 02:04:21.610
I'm not one of those people.

02:04:21.610 --> 02:04:23.340
I limit the amount,
and I certainly

02:04:23.340 --> 02:04:25.560
focus on the quality
of what I eat.

02:04:25.560 --> 02:04:28.050
But I do eat red
meat, which brings me

02:04:28.050 --> 02:04:33.810
to a question about just
generally in terms of food

02:04:33.810 --> 02:04:39.270
choice, can we come up with
a relatively short summary

02:04:39.270 --> 02:04:40.200
of the following?

02:04:40.200 --> 02:04:42.510
Tell me if this
is correct or not.

02:04:42.510 --> 02:04:46.020
That most of us
should be focused on--

02:04:46.020 --> 02:04:49.410
for sake of health
span and lifespan,

02:04:49.410 --> 02:04:54.330
should be focused on ingesting
minimally non-processed

02:04:54.330 --> 02:04:56.310
and minimally processed foods.

02:04:56.310 --> 02:04:58.080
Maybe even cooking our own food.

02:04:58.080 --> 02:04:59.310
I realize that's heresy now.

02:04:59.310 --> 02:05:01.020
But ideally, we would
do some of that.

02:05:01.020 --> 02:05:05.250
And really trying to avoid
foods that are highly processed

02:05:05.250 --> 02:05:06.510
and have lots of sugar.

02:05:06.510 --> 02:05:09.000
And I'm using this as a
segue to get into a question

02:05:09.000 --> 02:05:11.160
that I really want
your answer to.

02:05:11.160 --> 02:05:16.620
I've been dying to ask you this,
which is if sugar intake is not

02:05:16.620 --> 02:05:20.940
actually going up as much as
people think it is, why are

02:05:20.940 --> 02:05:23.550
people getting so much fatter?

02:05:23.550 --> 02:05:27.540
So what do you think about
just a general statement

02:05:27.540 --> 02:05:32.340
that we should try and eat
foods that are low to no--

02:05:32.340 --> 02:05:35.760
minimally to not processed
for about 80% of our foods.

02:05:35.760 --> 02:05:37.020
Is that a reasonable number?

02:05:37.020 --> 02:05:37.200
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

02:05:37.200 --> 02:05:39.870
It's hard to actually get
completely unprocessed food

02:05:39.870 --> 02:05:41.370
because almost everything
goes through some form

02:05:41.370 --> 02:05:41.750
of processing.

02:05:41.750 --> 02:05:43.667
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So I'm
thinking like anything

02:05:43.667 --> 02:05:45.752
that wouldn't survive long
without refrigeration--

02:05:45.752 --> 02:05:46.710
LAYNE NORTON: On shelf?

02:05:46.710 --> 02:05:46.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:05:46.860 --> 02:05:48.990
On a shelf, like an
apple or a banana.

02:05:48.990 --> 02:05:51.265
So like oatmeal, like
ground oats to me

02:05:51.265 --> 02:05:53.140
as long as there isn't
a bunch of other stuff

02:05:53.140 --> 02:05:54.930
and they would be
minimally processed.

02:05:54.930 --> 02:05:56.910
A steak is not really
processed, although it's

02:05:56.910 --> 02:05:58.740
cut off the animal, et cetera.

02:05:58.740 --> 02:06:00.600
So there's a few steps in there.

02:06:00.600 --> 02:06:02.568
But that's what I mean.

02:06:02.568 --> 02:06:03.360
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

02:06:03.360 --> 02:06:03.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:06:03.720 --> 02:06:06.150
LAYNE NORTON: And I think
everybody gets the gist.

02:06:06.150 --> 02:06:07.623
I'm probably a
little bit pedantic

02:06:07.623 --> 02:06:08.790
when it comes to this stuff.

02:06:08.790 --> 02:06:10.207
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
No, this is good.

02:06:10.207 --> 02:06:11.880
Actually, one of the
things I appreciate

02:06:11.880 --> 02:06:14.520
about you is something that
I get teased a lot by people

02:06:14.520 --> 02:06:17.850
close to me, which is the
caveats and the insistence

02:06:17.850 --> 02:06:20.670
on precision is really
important because especially

02:06:20.670 --> 02:06:23.250
with online
communications these days,

02:06:23.250 --> 02:06:25.320
it's like a runaway train.

02:06:25.320 --> 02:06:27.330
People will--

02:06:27.330 --> 02:06:29.160
LAYNE NORTON: It's too
easy to misinterpret

02:06:29.160 --> 02:06:29.660
what you're saying.

02:06:29.660 --> 02:06:30.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's very
easy to misinterpret it.

02:06:30.900 --> 02:06:32.520
And the misinterpretations
are often

02:06:32.520 --> 02:06:35.550
used to leverage whole new ideas
about what is and isn't true.

02:06:35.550 --> 02:06:37.620
Mostly about what is true.

02:06:37.620 --> 02:06:39.660
So I really
appreciate the nuance.

02:06:39.660 --> 02:06:42.435
And this is what a longform
podcast really allows us to do,

02:06:42.435 --> 02:06:44.850
is catch every curve, you know?

02:06:44.850 --> 02:06:47.880
LAYNE NORTON: So I would 100%
agree with what you said.

02:06:47.880 --> 02:06:51.120
That if you were going to
make a broad stroke, that

02:06:51.120 --> 02:06:53.250
trying to focus on
minimally processed foods

02:06:53.250 --> 02:06:54.960
is very important.

02:06:54.960 --> 02:06:57.180
The one caveat I would
say is, I think it's

02:06:57.180 --> 02:06:58.680
important to understand why.

02:06:58.680 --> 02:07:01.170
Because otherwise, people can
make this weird association

02:07:01.170 --> 02:07:04.680
that, if I eat any minimally--
or any processed food,

02:07:04.680 --> 02:07:05.850
it's going to kill me.

02:07:05.850 --> 02:07:07.650
Or like every time
I eat it, it's

02:07:07.650 --> 02:07:09.960
like I'm smoking a
cigarette and my health--

02:07:09.960 --> 02:07:12.830
my longevity is declining.

02:07:12.830 --> 02:07:16.960
Based on the studies we have,
it's mostly about the energy.

02:07:16.960 --> 02:07:22.340
That processed food just gets
people to spontaneously eat

02:07:22.340 --> 02:07:23.860
more.

02:07:23.860 --> 02:07:27.613
And Kevin Hall showed this
in his study that was very--

02:07:27.613 --> 02:07:29.780
I mean, he designed some
of the most elegant studies

02:07:29.780 --> 02:07:30.200
in nutrition.

02:07:30.200 --> 02:07:31.367
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He's great.

02:07:31.367 --> 02:07:33.710
LAYNE NORTON: And they,
basically, took people

02:07:33.710 --> 02:07:36.410
from a minimally
processed food diet,

02:07:36.410 --> 02:07:39.540
and then gave them access
to ultra processed foods.

02:07:39.540 --> 02:07:40.910
Very few instructions.

02:07:40.910 --> 02:07:42.440
Just eat to you feel satisfied.

02:07:42.440 --> 02:07:44.648
And they spontaneously
increased their calorie intake

02:07:44.648 --> 02:07:46.160
by 500 calories a day.

02:07:46.160 --> 02:07:47.915
I mean, that's massive.

02:07:50.940 --> 02:07:52.530
And we haven't quite
figured it out.

02:07:52.530 --> 02:07:55.180
People say, well, it's sugar.

02:07:55.180 --> 02:07:59.040
It doesn't appear to be sugar
in terms of just an isolation.

02:07:59.040 --> 02:07:59.845
Well, it's fats.

02:07:59.845 --> 02:08:01.470
Doesn't appear to be
fats in isolation.

02:08:01.470 --> 02:08:04.100
Well, it's the combination
of sugar and fat.

02:08:04.100 --> 02:08:05.840
Partly, well, it's
the combination

02:08:05.840 --> 02:08:08.220
of sugar, fat and salt. Partly.

02:08:08.220 --> 02:08:11.060
But there's some
kind of overall magic

02:08:11.060 --> 02:08:13.430
to the texture and
the mouthfeel and just

02:08:13.430 --> 02:08:15.043
the overall
palatability of stuff.

02:08:15.043 --> 02:08:16.460
Which is always
why I say, there's

02:08:16.460 --> 02:08:18.320
like right and wrong ways
to these different diets.

02:08:18.320 --> 02:08:21.090
Like, for example, like there's
the right way to do plant-based

02:08:21.090 --> 02:08:24.260
and then there's like what's
in some of these documentaries

02:08:24.260 --> 02:08:26.660
where they're eating like
plant-based mac and cheese.

02:08:26.660 --> 02:08:28.730
And again, I love a
good mac and cheese,

02:08:28.730 --> 02:08:30.735
but that should not form--

02:08:30.735 --> 02:08:32.610
that should not be
pitched as a healthy diet.

02:08:32.610 --> 02:08:34.790
just because it's "plant-based."

02:08:34.790 --> 02:08:37.490
Because I mean, you're eating
a highly processed food

02:08:37.490 --> 02:08:39.940
that's very palatable
and easy to overeat.

02:08:39.940 --> 02:08:40.870
Same thing for keto.

02:08:40.870 --> 02:08:42.490
You've now got like
keto ice creams

02:08:42.490 --> 02:08:45.550
and you've got keto cookies
and all these sorts of things.

02:08:45.550 --> 02:08:48.328
And I'm like, yeah, and
if you look at them,

02:08:48.328 --> 02:08:50.620
they actually have more
calories than the normal stuff.

02:08:50.620 --> 02:08:52.570
And I'm like, yeah,
this is completely

02:08:52.570 --> 02:08:53.620
missing the point here.

02:08:53.620 --> 02:08:55.990
Like you're actually
just taking yourself--

02:08:55.990 --> 02:08:59.260
the whole point of those
diets is the reason

02:08:59.260 --> 02:09:01.630
you tend to lose
weight is originally

02:09:01.630 --> 02:09:04.930
like good luck 10
years ago, doing a keto

02:09:04.930 --> 02:09:07.420
diet, eating processed food.

02:09:07.420 --> 02:09:10.270
Like you just couldn't
do it, really.

02:09:10.270 --> 02:09:11.943
Now, you can.

02:09:11.943 --> 02:09:13.360
But the problem
is, it's not going

02:09:13.360 --> 02:09:15.443
to work because you're
going to be still consuming

02:09:15.443 --> 02:09:18.027
too many calories, because
even though it's keto,

02:09:18.027 --> 02:09:18.860
what are they doing?

02:09:18.860 --> 02:09:20.200
Well, they're trying to
make it more palatable,

02:09:20.200 --> 02:09:21.992
they're trying to make
it better mouthfeel,

02:09:21.992 --> 02:09:24.940
which, I guess, if you're being
keto for the sake of being

02:09:24.940 --> 02:09:28.510
keto, great, but if you have
hopes of body composition

02:09:28.510 --> 02:09:32.710
modification, it's going to
really negatively impact.

02:09:32.710 --> 02:09:36.100
So yes, I think minimizing
the amount of processed foods

02:09:36.100 --> 02:09:38.270
you consume can be important.

02:09:38.270 --> 02:09:40.750
Now that being said, it
depends on the individual

02:09:40.750 --> 02:09:41.620
and their goals.

02:09:41.620 --> 02:09:45.370
If your goal is to, for example,
build muscle or maintain a high

02:09:45.370 --> 02:09:47.410
body weight for a
sport, for example,

02:09:47.410 --> 02:09:50.830
like an NFL offensive lineman
or something of that nature,

02:09:50.830 --> 02:09:52.630
or if you're--

02:09:52.630 --> 02:09:56.480
I worked with an NBA
team, they were kind of--

02:09:56.480 --> 02:09:58.480
I can't disclose anything,
but they were looking

02:09:58.480 --> 02:10:01.660
at drafting a certain player.

02:10:01.660 --> 02:10:07.270
And like for them, processed
foods may actually be a tool.

02:10:07.270 --> 02:10:08.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Or a teenagers.

02:10:08.660 --> 02:10:12.250
We all want young people to
eat more healthily, I think.

02:10:12.250 --> 02:10:13.270
Develop great habits.

02:10:13.270 --> 02:10:15.420
But some of them, their
caloric needs are so high--

02:10:15.420 --> 02:10:16.030
LAYNE NORTON: So high.

02:10:16.030 --> 02:10:18.322
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --that if
they were eating what I eat,

02:10:18.322 --> 02:10:20.790
they're going to dissolve into--

02:10:20.790 --> 02:10:21.790
they'll just waste away.

02:10:21.790 --> 02:10:23.568
LAYNE NORTON: So I
describe this, again,

02:10:23.568 --> 02:10:24.610
with a financial example.

02:10:24.610 --> 02:10:25.930
It's like a budget.

02:10:25.930 --> 02:10:29.320
So if I make a million
dollars a year,

02:10:29.320 --> 02:10:34.610
for example, is it OK for me to
buy like a $100,000 sports car?

02:10:34.610 --> 02:10:36.430
Let's assume that
loans don't exist.

02:10:36.430 --> 02:10:39.640
Is it OK for me to buy
a $100,000 sports car

02:10:39.640 --> 02:10:43.930
if I still am able to pay my
mortgage and pay my utilities

02:10:43.930 --> 02:10:45.910
and take care of my
responsibilities--

02:10:45.910 --> 02:10:47.620
the things I should do.

02:10:47.620 --> 02:10:50.377
Is OK if I do that if it makes
me feel good and it's fun?

02:10:50.377 --> 02:10:51.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK by me.

02:10:51.460 --> 02:10:51.760
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

02:10:51.760 --> 02:10:52.360
It's fun.

02:10:52.360 --> 02:10:55.290
Like it fits in your budget.

02:10:55.290 --> 02:10:58.330
If I make 50 grand
a year, should I

02:10:58.330 --> 02:11:00.640
be going out and
buying a sports car?

02:11:00.640 --> 02:11:02.140
Probably not,
because I'm not going

02:11:02.140 --> 02:11:03.520
to be able to pay
my mortgage and all

02:11:03.520 --> 02:11:04.728
these other responsibilities.

02:11:04.728 --> 02:11:08.050
So your protein, your,
fiber your micronutrients.

02:11:08.050 --> 02:11:09.730
These are your responsibilities.

02:11:09.730 --> 02:11:11.470
But those become
much easier to hit

02:11:11.470 --> 02:11:12.880
when you have higher calories.

02:11:12.880 --> 02:11:16.120
So if you're eating
4,000 calories a day

02:11:16.120 --> 02:11:19.120
for whatever goal you
have, you're probably

02:11:19.120 --> 02:11:22.870
going to have some left
over and like good luck

02:11:22.870 --> 02:11:26.680
eating 4,000 calories from
minimally processed foods,

02:11:26.680 --> 02:11:28.210
quite frankly,
you'll be miserable

02:11:28.210 --> 02:11:30.640
because you're going
to have such gut feel

02:11:30.640 --> 02:11:32.990
that you're going to feel
like you can't even move.

02:11:32.990 --> 02:11:37.120
And so again, now,
it becomes OK,

02:11:37.120 --> 02:11:39.130
well, is there something
inherent to that food

02:11:39.130 --> 02:11:39.880
processing?

02:11:39.880 --> 02:11:42.478
Is there something
that we can pick out

02:11:42.478 --> 02:11:44.020
that we know, OK,
well, this is going

02:11:44.020 --> 02:11:45.580
to be a negative
effect on health,

02:11:45.580 --> 02:11:48.700
even like body
composition stuff aside.

02:11:48.700 --> 02:11:50.410
And I would say,
there's not really

02:11:50.410 --> 02:11:52.120
great evidence of that so far.

02:11:52.120 --> 02:11:54.850
And a great example
of that is sugar.

02:11:54.850 --> 02:11:58.300
I mean, I actually just
wrote a really long article

02:11:58.300 --> 02:12:01.390
on my website about why I
think sugar was not the root

02:12:01.390 --> 02:12:03.160
cause of the obesity epidemic.

02:12:03.160 --> 02:12:05.350
And you mentioned sugar
intake in the last 20 years

02:12:05.350 --> 02:12:06.850
has actually gone
down a little bit.

02:12:06.850 --> 02:12:10.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Alcohol intake,
well, if you look on the whole

02:12:10.540 --> 02:12:12.040
it, might have gone
up a little bit,

02:12:12.040 --> 02:12:14.950
but certain certainly
in the male sector,

02:12:14.950 --> 02:12:16.420
it's gone way down drinking.

02:12:16.420 --> 02:12:18.280
Used to be-- there
was a 5 o'clock.

02:12:18.280 --> 02:12:20.020
People were drinking all day.

02:12:20.020 --> 02:12:23.410
People are smoking a lot less.

02:12:23.410 --> 02:12:24.580
I think it's a real puzzle.

02:12:24.580 --> 02:12:27.407
I'd love to know what
your hypotheses are.

02:12:27.407 --> 02:12:27.940
LAYNE NORTON: Well,
smoking could actually

02:12:27.940 --> 02:12:29.820
be in opposition because
nicotine is actually

02:12:29.820 --> 02:12:30.820
an appetite suppressant.

02:12:30.820 --> 02:12:32.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Appetite
suppressant and also increases

02:12:32.020 --> 02:12:32.520
focus.

02:12:32.520 --> 02:12:34.488
The problem is it often
arrives in a delivery

02:12:34.488 --> 02:12:35.530
device that can kill you.

02:12:35.530 --> 02:12:36.322
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

02:12:36.322 --> 02:12:39.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But nicotine
itself is a powerful agent.

02:12:39.020 --> 02:12:42.910
It also can offset age-related
cognitive decline not entirely,

02:12:42.910 --> 02:12:45.610
but it makes the
brain work better.

02:12:45.610 --> 02:12:48.970
LAYNE NORTON: I've got a buddy
who doesn't like caffeine.

02:12:48.970 --> 02:12:52.240
And he just takes those
nicotine pouches and, basically,

02:12:52.240 --> 02:12:53.988
has one in almost
all day because he's

02:12:53.988 --> 02:12:56.530
like-- he has a very stressful
job and he's a high performer.

02:12:56.530 --> 02:12:57.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:12:57.460 --> 02:12:59.210
You have to be careful
how you deliver it,

02:12:59.210 --> 02:13:02.380
but there's a Nobel Prize
winning neuroscientist that

02:13:02.380 --> 02:13:05.540
will chew five or six
pieces of Nicorette an hour,

02:13:05.540 --> 02:13:08.420
which I do not recommend,
but when he quit smoking,

02:13:08.420 --> 02:13:10.270
he just simply couldn't
function as well.

02:13:10.270 --> 02:13:11.645
And he was the
one who pointed me

02:13:11.645 --> 02:13:13.300
to do the literature
on offsetting

02:13:13.300 --> 02:13:15.130
age-related cognitive decline.

02:13:15.130 --> 02:13:17.710
Even neuron maintenance,
and it's pretty interesting.

02:13:17.710 --> 02:13:20.288
LAYNE NORTON: Well, it's a
pretty impressive nootropic

02:13:20.288 --> 02:13:20.830
to be honest.

02:13:23.163 --> 02:13:24.580
Now, the first
thing to realize is

02:13:24.580 --> 02:13:27.390
when we're talking
about consumption data,

02:13:27.390 --> 02:13:31.110
this is based on actual
production, basically.

02:13:31.110 --> 02:13:33.150
They're assuming that,
OK, we're producing

02:13:33.150 --> 02:13:34.560
this amount of
these foods, so we

02:13:34.560 --> 02:13:36.685
can assume the consumption
is going to follow that.

02:13:36.685 --> 02:13:39.240
So it's not a
direct measurement.

02:13:39.240 --> 02:13:42.390
But it has been validated
in a few different studies.

02:13:42.390 --> 02:13:44.180
We know that oil
consumption has gone up.

02:13:44.180 --> 02:13:45.680
Like that that's, yeah.

02:13:45.680 --> 02:13:47.570
That's one of the big ones.

02:13:47.570 --> 02:13:49.250
And this forms the
crux of-- like,

02:13:49.250 --> 02:13:51.290
the seed oils are
like the root of--

02:13:51.290 --> 02:13:52.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
definitely the question

02:13:52.070 --> 02:13:53.320
that I'm going to ping you on.

02:13:53.320 --> 02:13:55.460
LAYNE NORTON: They're going
to come into your house

02:13:55.460 --> 02:13:59.700
and kick your dog and punch your
mom, and all kinds of stuff.

02:13:59.700 --> 02:14:03.590
And I'm happy to address those.

02:14:03.590 --> 02:14:06.070
So calories have still gone up.

02:14:06.070 --> 02:14:07.820
There's some people
who claim that they've

02:14:07.820 --> 02:14:10.970
gone-- they've plateaued.

02:14:10.970 --> 02:14:14.060
I think the data seems to
suggest that calorie intake is

02:14:14.060 --> 02:14:15.320
still increasing.

02:14:15.320 --> 02:14:17.630
And the other thing
to keep in mind

02:14:17.630 --> 02:14:20.690
is, even if it's
plateaued, it's still

02:14:20.690 --> 02:14:22.768
at a high enough level
that obesity is probably

02:14:22.768 --> 02:14:24.560
going to continue to
increase up to a point

02:14:24.560 --> 02:14:26.893
where it'll probably plateau
if calories have plateaued.

02:14:26.893 --> 02:14:28.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
about energy output?

02:14:28.670 --> 02:14:31.310
Leaving aside NEAT, because
that sounds highly individual,

02:14:31.310 --> 02:14:33.020
I mean, people are--

02:14:33.020 --> 02:14:34.882
the people we know are
focusing on exercise.

02:14:34.882 --> 02:14:37.340
But there are a lot of folks
out there that don't exercise.

02:14:37.340 --> 02:14:40.820
LAYNE NORTON: And energy output
has gone down over the years.

02:14:40.820 --> 02:14:42.560
I mean, it's very
obvious when you

02:14:42.560 --> 02:14:44.120
look at how people
work now compared

02:14:44.120 --> 02:14:46.550
to even 30, 40 years ago.

02:14:46.550 --> 02:14:47.590
It's much different.

02:14:47.590 --> 02:14:48.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Less walking.

02:14:48.840 --> 02:14:52.070
Also, I learned recently
that kids in high school

02:14:52.070 --> 02:14:54.980
don't take PE class
in many schools.

02:14:54.980 --> 02:14:57.290
But we had to suit up
and run and suit up.

02:14:57.290 --> 02:14:59.540
And if you didn't bring
your change of clothes

02:14:59.540 --> 02:15:01.915
or you didn't wash them in
which case you'd be better off

02:15:01.915 --> 02:15:04.580
just not wearing them, nothing
like the smell of a boys locker

02:15:04.580 --> 02:15:07.340
room after a weekend,
I can still remember it

02:15:07.340 --> 02:15:09.140
and it's not pleasant.

02:15:09.140 --> 02:15:12.290
But you had to run and do your
pushups with everybody else

02:15:12.290 --> 02:15:15.770
or play volleyball in your
regular school day clothes.

02:15:15.770 --> 02:15:20.300
So my understanding is that,
physical education is not

02:15:20.300 --> 02:15:22.302
part of the basic
education any longer.

02:15:22.302 --> 02:15:24.260
LAYNE NORTON: It probably
depends on the state,

02:15:24.260 --> 02:15:26.677
but I know many states have
done away with it just because

02:15:26.677 --> 02:15:27.510
of budget cuts.

02:15:27.510 --> 02:15:28.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
activity is going down,

02:15:28.550 --> 02:15:29.810
caloric intake is going up.

02:15:29.810 --> 02:15:30.125
Maybe--

02:15:30.125 --> 02:15:31.220
LAYNE NORTON: Plateauing
a little bit, yeah.

02:15:31.220 --> 02:15:32.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--maybe plateauing.

02:15:32.720 --> 02:15:35.382
Is that sufficient to
explain the obesity epidemic?

02:15:35.382 --> 02:15:36.965
LAYNE NORTON: Based
on what I've seen,

02:15:36.965 --> 02:15:38.185
I think it's pretty sufficient.

02:15:38.185 --> 02:15:39.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So it
might not be that big

02:15:39.230 --> 02:15:40.430
of a mystery after all?

02:15:40.430 --> 02:15:42.555
LAYNE NORTON: No, I don't
think it's a big mystery.

02:15:42.555 --> 02:15:48.670
I think that people don't like
the concept of energy balance,

02:15:48.670 --> 02:15:51.490
and I think because
they insert judgment

02:15:51.490 --> 02:15:56.350
into it, which is, OK, if
you're gaining weight over time,

02:15:56.350 --> 02:15:59.380
at a fundamental level,
it means you are eating--

02:15:59.380 --> 02:16:02.880
you're consuming more energy
than you're expending.

02:16:02.880 --> 02:16:06.930
People insert the judgment,
which is, you're lazy,

02:16:06.930 --> 02:16:10.030
you're a sloth,
or whatever it is.

02:16:10.030 --> 02:16:12.150
And I think there's a
lot of people out there

02:16:12.150 --> 02:16:13.350
who actually think
that, I actually

02:16:13.350 --> 02:16:14.610
remember talking to
somebody who was like, well,

02:16:14.610 --> 02:16:16.470
I would never hire an
obese person for a job

02:16:16.470 --> 02:16:18.510
because it's just obvious
that they're lazy.

02:16:18.510 --> 02:16:22.440
And I just remember
going, are you serious?

02:16:22.440 --> 02:16:24.570
Like, there are
plenty of very, very

02:16:24.570 --> 02:16:27.120
smart high-achieving
people who are obese.

02:16:27.120 --> 02:16:28.440
And like it's not--

02:16:28.440 --> 02:16:31.230
this is what happens when you
just put people in buckets.

02:16:31.230 --> 02:16:34.520
People are much more
complicated than this.

02:16:34.520 --> 02:16:36.200
Yes.

02:16:36.200 --> 02:16:38.180
There is some personal
responsibility.

02:16:38.180 --> 02:16:40.610
But then when you look through
the data and you take--

02:16:40.610 --> 02:16:43.000
there was a study
done in obese women

02:16:43.000 --> 02:16:44.750
where they found that
women who were obese

02:16:44.750 --> 02:16:48.500
were 50% more likely to have
had some form of sexual assault

02:16:48.500 --> 02:16:51.530
trauma in their past.

02:16:51.530 --> 02:16:54.230
We know that people
from lower income areas

02:16:54.230 --> 02:16:56.600
are more prone to be obese.

02:16:56.600 --> 02:17:00.629
There's several people who have
a higher ACE score, I believe,

02:17:00.629 --> 02:17:03.707
which of measures like
traumatic childhood events.

02:17:03.707 --> 02:17:05.540
I believe, there was a
study showing they're

02:17:05.540 --> 02:17:06.750
more likely to be obese.

02:17:06.750 --> 02:17:13.160
So there's-- yes, it is an
energy imbalance problem,

02:17:13.160 --> 02:17:16.129
but just saying,
eat less, move more,

02:17:16.129 --> 02:17:18.049
that's like telling
broke people, well,

02:17:18.049 --> 02:17:21.580
just earn more money
than you spend.

02:17:21.580 --> 02:17:25.120
It's technically right,
but it's very unhelpful.

02:17:25.120 --> 02:17:28.870
What is more helpful is
to describe and implement

02:17:28.870 --> 02:17:30.459
the habits and
behaviors that will

02:17:30.459 --> 02:17:33.250
allow them to achieve that.

02:17:33.250 --> 02:17:35.830
So I realized we go
off track a little bit,

02:17:35.830 --> 02:17:42.750
but circling back to
sugar, circa 2005,

02:17:42.750 --> 02:17:45.570
I believe that sugar
was fattening and bad

02:17:45.570 --> 02:17:47.980
for your health independent
of any other variable.

02:17:47.980 --> 02:17:49.990
So independent risk factor.

02:17:49.990 --> 02:17:52.799
And again, I want to be very
clear about what independent

02:17:52.799 --> 02:17:53.610
means.

02:17:53.610 --> 02:17:57.280
Independent means independent
of all of the variables.

02:17:57.280 --> 02:18:01.176
This thing is bad for your
health and body composition.

02:18:01.176 --> 02:18:03.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So on its own,
independent of whether or not,

02:18:03.990 --> 02:18:06.554
for instance, it increases
hunger and appetite.

02:18:06.554 --> 02:18:07.514
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

02:18:07.514 --> 02:18:10.290
Or caloric intake.

02:18:10.290 --> 02:18:12.389
I was at a graduate
school mixer.

02:18:12.389 --> 02:18:14.190
And one of the
professors there was

02:18:14.190 --> 02:18:16.950
somebody who had done
research on high fructose corn

02:18:16.950 --> 02:18:19.650
syrup and fructose,
specifically, as well.

02:18:19.650 --> 02:18:22.049
And he was talking
to another professor

02:18:22.049 --> 02:18:27.459
and he had done this study in
rodents where he had fed like--

02:18:27.459 --> 02:18:29.459
I think it was like 60%
or 70% of their calories

02:18:29.459 --> 02:18:30.330
from fructose.

02:18:30.330 --> 02:18:32.370
And they saw some
really weird things

02:18:32.370 --> 02:18:34.225
happen in the liver
with lipogenesis

02:18:34.225 --> 02:18:35.350
and all this kind of stuff.

02:18:35.350 --> 02:18:37.868
And the other professor
is saying to him, yeah,

02:18:37.868 --> 02:18:39.660
it's pretty obvious
that high fructose corn

02:18:39.660 --> 02:18:41.219
syrup is fattening.

02:18:41.219 --> 02:18:43.350
And this professor who
had done this research

02:18:43.350 --> 02:18:46.537
said, yeah, because
it's people overeat.

02:18:46.537 --> 02:18:48.120
And he's like, don't
you think there's

02:18:48.120 --> 02:18:49.540
something inherent to it?

02:18:49.540 --> 02:18:51.600
And he said, no, I think
it's just calories.

02:18:51.600 --> 02:18:53.100
People are eating
too many calories.

02:18:53.100 --> 02:18:56.700
He's like, we did a proof of
concept looking at could we--

02:18:56.700 --> 02:18:59.700
like, try eating 70% of your
daily calories from fructose.

02:18:59.700 --> 02:19:01.230
You actually can't do it.

02:19:01.230 --> 02:19:04.500
High fructose, corn syrup
is only 55% fructose.

02:19:04.500 --> 02:19:06.828
So if you ate nothing but
high fructose, corn syrup,

02:19:06.828 --> 02:19:08.370
you would still not
get to this level

02:19:08.370 --> 02:19:11.530
that they fed in this study.

02:19:11.530 --> 02:19:13.959
So that got me kind of
like questioning my beliefs

02:19:13.959 --> 02:19:14.459
about it.

02:19:14.459 --> 02:19:17.219
So then I went through and I
said, OK, look, let's take out

02:19:17.219 --> 02:19:18.510
the epidemiology.

02:19:18.510 --> 02:19:20.129
Not that epidemiology
is useless,

02:19:20.129 --> 02:19:22.920
but people who eat
more sugar are also

02:19:22.920 --> 02:19:24.340
likely to eat more calories.

02:19:24.340 --> 02:19:27.000
So then I looked for the
randomized controlled trials

02:19:27.000 --> 02:19:31.469
where they match calories
and vary the amount of sugar.

02:19:31.469 --> 02:19:33.750
And it doesn't seem
to make a difference.

02:19:33.750 --> 02:19:35.645
At least from fat
loss or fat gain--

02:19:35.645 --> 02:19:37.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
what about health?

02:19:37.270 --> 02:19:39.240
So for instance, if somebody--

02:19:39.240 --> 02:19:44.910
and I know somebody like this
who loves sweets, is thin,

02:19:44.910 --> 02:19:47.070
get some exercise, not a ton.

02:19:47.070 --> 02:19:50.340
But my concern is that,
a significant fraction

02:19:50.340 --> 02:19:53.190
of their calories are coming
from these sugary foods

02:19:53.190 --> 02:19:55.890
and therefore they're not
getting enough fiber--

02:19:55.890 --> 02:19:57.630
maybe protein, et cetera.

02:19:57.630 --> 02:20:01.170
LAYNE NORTON: So let's look
at epidemiology for a second,

02:20:01.170 --> 02:20:03.450
then I'll address
this more directly.

02:20:03.450 --> 02:20:05.160
When we look at
epidemiology, people

02:20:05.160 --> 02:20:09.240
who eat higher amounts of
sugar tend to be more obese,

02:20:09.240 --> 02:20:14.020
they tend to have worse
biomarkers of health.

02:20:14.020 --> 02:20:16.480
The people who eat fruit--
a lot of fruit sugar,

02:20:16.480 --> 02:20:18.740
don't have those
same associations.

02:20:18.740 --> 02:20:19.700
So why is that?

02:20:19.700 --> 02:20:22.000
Well, because fruit
has fiber with it.

02:20:22.000 --> 02:20:27.130
So I started to believe,
based on the data

02:20:27.130 --> 02:20:31.240
I was looking at, that
high sugar intake was not

02:20:31.240 --> 02:20:32.530
the problem per se.

02:20:32.530 --> 02:20:35.050
The problem was that high
sugary foods, typically,

02:20:35.050 --> 02:20:37.060
are very low in fiber.

02:20:37.060 --> 02:20:41.870
But if you're getting enough
fiber, is sugar a problem?

02:20:41.870 --> 02:20:45.022
So there was a classic
study by Surwit in 1997.

02:20:45.022 --> 02:20:46.480
I think it's still
the best studies

02:20:46.480 --> 02:20:47.810
of this day looking at this.

02:20:47.810 --> 02:20:49.660
And I know those people who
say, well, it was done in 1997.

02:20:49.660 --> 02:20:50.493
It has no relevance.

02:20:50.493 --> 02:20:53.050
You know, I know, if it's a
good study, it's a good study.

02:20:53.050 --> 02:20:54.640
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Some
studies are timeless.

02:20:54.640 --> 02:20:55.820
In fact, they have
greater relevance--

02:20:55.820 --> 02:20:57.100
LAYNE NORTON: You're not
going to go back and undo

02:20:57.100 --> 02:20:59.440
the discovery of DNA
because it was 60 years ago

02:20:59.440 --> 02:21:00.190
or whatever it is.

02:21:00.190 --> 02:21:01.023
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No.

02:21:01.023 --> 02:21:05.470
LAYNE NORTON: So they looked
at an 1,100-calorie diet,

02:21:05.470 --> 02:21:07.330
so low calorie diet.

02:21:07.330 --> 02:21:10.840
One group was eating over
110 grams of sugar a day,

02:21:10.840 --> 02:21:12.430
like sucrose.

02:21:12.430 --> 02:21:15.040
The other group was eating
about 10 grams of sugar per day.

02:21:15.040 --> 02:21:19.360
Calories, protein,
carbs, fats all matched.

02:21:19.360 --> 02:21:21.850
And they provided all the
meals to these participants.

02:21:21.850 --> 02:21:23.230
So very tightly controlled.

02:21:23.230 --> 02:21:24.820
And it was over six weeks.

02:21:24.820 --> 02:21:27.353
Both groups lost the exact
same amount of body fat.

02:21:27.353 --> 02:21:29.770
So it doesn't seem to matter
for body composition in terms

02:21:29.770 --> 02:21:31.270
of sugar per se.

02:21:31.270 --> 02:21:33.610
Then they also looked at
some biomarkers of health

02:21:33.610 --> 02:21:37.090
like blood lipids and blood
sugar and some other things.

02:21:37.090 --> 02:21:39.910
Again, there was no
real differences.

02:21:39.910 --> 02:21:41.740
The only difference was--

02:21:41.740 --> 02:21:44.560
so all their biomarkers
improved in both groups.

02:21:44.560 --> 02:21:48.110
The only real difference was
a small difference in LDL.

02:21:48.110 --> 02:21:51.160
So the group eating low sugar
had a better improvement

02:21:51.160 --> 02:21:53.237
slightly in LDL.

02:21:53.237 --> 02:21:55.570
But that's probably because
they were eating more fiber.

02:21:55.570 --> 02:21:59.260
And we know fiber can bind
to cholesterol and lower LDL

02:21:59.260 --> 02:22:00.350
cholesterol.

02:22:00.350 --> 02:22:05.290
So now, I want to caveat this.

02:22:05.290 --> 02:22:08.570
Sugar probably doesn't have
any positive health effects.

02:22:08.570 --> 02:22:10.150
So there's that.

02:22:10.150 --> 02:22:11.897
And nutrition is an exchange.

02:22:11.897 --> 02:22:13.480
If you're eating one
thing, you're not

02:22:13.480 --> 02:22:15.340
eating another thing, right?

02:22:15.340 --> 02:22:18.460
So but what I would
tell people is,

02:22:18.460 --> 02:22:23.030
focus probably less on
sugar, focus more on fiber.

02:22:23.030 --> 02:22:29.170
So if you're eating 30, 40,
50, 60 grams of fiber a day,

02:22:29.170 --> 02:22:32.632
but your sugar is,
80, 90 grams, I

02:22:32.632 --> 02:22:34.090
would not be that
worried about it,

02:22:34.090 --> 02:22:37.440
especially, if you're
controlling calories as well.

02:22:37.440 --> 02:22:39.190
What I would be worried
about is if you're

02:22:39.190 --> 02:22:42.490
eating just a decent
amount of calories

02:22:42.490 --> 02:22:45.910
and not getting enough
fiber and in general, right?

02:22:45.910 --> 02:22:48.310
And even in studies,
there's a few meta

02:22:48.310 --> 02:22:51.580
analyzes out now looking
at isoenergetic exchange

02:22:51.580 --> 02:22:54.790
of different carbohydrates
with sugar carbohydrates.

02:22:54.790 --> 02:22:57.425
So fructose and
glucose and sucrose.

02:22:57.425 --> 02:22:58.550
Now, why is this important?

02:22:58.550 --> 02:23:00.610
Well, again, if
you're not equate--

02:23:00.610 --> 02:23:03.100
when I say isoenergetic,
that means equal in energy,

02:23:03.100 --> 02:23:04.390
equal in calories.

02:23:04.390 --> 02:23:08.800
So basically, when they exchange
either sucrose or glucose

02:23:08.800 --> 02:23:12.430
or fructose for other
forms of carbohydrate,

02:23:12.430 --> 02:23:15.940
do they see differences in
these markers of health?

02:23:15.940 --> 02:23:22.510
Like HbA1c, fasting blood
glucose, blood lipids,

02:23:22.510 --> 02:23:25.600
with rare exceptions, and I
can't remember all the data

02:23:25.600 --> 02:23:27.070
points exactly.

02:23:27.070 --> 02:23:30.130
But the take home
is, doesn't really

02:23:30.130 --> 02:23:31.660
seem to make a difference?

02:23:31.660 --> 02:23:34.810
Now, for anybody out there,
straw man is my argument,

02:23:34.810 --> 02:23:37.360
I am not advocating
for sugar consumption.

02:23:37.360 --> 02:23:39.370
But I think it's
important for people

02:23:39.370 --> 02:23:41.620
to not create weird
associations in their minds

02:23:41.620 --> 02:23:43.787
because one of the things
I've observed, especially,

02:23:43.787 --> 02:23:46.150
in the fitness industry,
is when people feel

02:23:46.150 --> 02:23:48.520
like they can't eat something--

02:23:48.520 --> 02:23:51.880
it's one thing if you say, I
am choosing not to eat this

02:23:51.880 --> 02:23:54.012
just because I'm choosing to.

02:23:54.012 --> 02:23:56.220
But it's a very different
thing when you're purposely

02:23:56.220 --> 02:23:59.220
restricting because you
feel like something is bad.

02:23:59.220 --> 02:24:02.700
And this-- I mean, you
know the human brain is,

02:24:02.700 --> 02:24:06.540
in many ways, amazing and
in many ways, really dumb.

02:24:06.540 --> 02:24:09.690
So when you purposely try
to restrict something,

02:24:09.690 --> 02:24:12.820
what tends to happen is you're
more prone to binge on it.

02:24:12.820 --> 02:24:15.240
So people who will
try to-- well,

02:24:15.240 --> 02:24:17.610
I'm never going to eat
you know sugar again

02:24:17.610 --> 02:24:19.830
or I'm going to try
and limit sugar.

02:24:19.830 --> 02:24:22.020
And this isn't the
case for everybody.

02:24:22.020 --> 02:24:26.700
But they have actually
shown now in studies, people

02:24:26.700 --> 02:24:30.740
who are purposely restricting
a specific nutrient,

02:24:30.740 --> 02:24:32.720
they tend to crave
more of that nutrient.

02:24:32.720 --> 02:24:36.110
And if they do get exposed to
it, they're more likely to have

02:24:36.110 --> 02:24:38.540
what's called a disinhibition
reflex, which is basically

02:24:38.540 --> 02:24:40.160
a binge response.

02:24:40.160 --> 02:24:43.305
Because the thinking
goes, well, this is bad,

02:24:43.305 --> 02:24:47.390
and there's no context on
dosage making the poison.

02:24:47.390 --> 02:24:48.990
This is just bad in general.

02:24:48.990 --> 02:24:51.380
So if I have it, I've
already screwed up.

02:24:51.380 --> 02:24:53.870
I might as well just
have as much as I want.

02:24:53.870 --> 02:24:57.170
And I like Spencer Nadolsky's
comparison to this.

02:24:57.170 --> 02:24:59.840
That's like getting a flat and
then going out and slashing

02:24:59.840 --> 02:25:02.730
your other three tires
because you might as well.

02:25:02.730 --> 02:25:04.010
So I really--

02:25:04.010 --> 02:25:06.320
I try to come from that
perspective of, I've

02:25:06.320 --> 02:25:09.290
seen so many people
struggle with, maybe,

02:25:09.290 --> 02:25:12.560
not an eating disorder, but
disordered eating patterns

02:25:12.560 --> 02:25:16.230
because of these associations
they've made in their mind.

02:25:16.230 --> 02:25:19.100
And so that's why I'm so
pedantic and a stickler

02:25:19.100 --> 02:25:21.080
about saying, OK,
yes, it's a good idea

02:25:21.080 --> 02:25:24.170
to eat mentally
processed food and try

02:25:24.170 --> 02:25:26.060
to avoid processed foods.

02:25:26.060 --> 02:25:29.930
But not because processed
foods are bad per se,

02:25:29.930 --> 02:25:34.070
but what the outcome tends to
be from a lot of processed food

02:25:34.070 --> 02:25:36.320
consumption, which is
overconsuming calories and then

02:25:36.320 --> 02:25:39.465
therefore energy toxicity
negatively contributing

02:25:39.465 --> 02:25:40.090
to your health.

02:25:40.090 --> 02:25:41.007
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:25:41.007 --> 02:25:44.840
It seems like it, again,
returns to the potential

02:25:44.840 --> 02:25:48.170
for a positive, negative, or
neutral behavioral change.

02:25:48.170 --> 02:25:51.080
And perceptual change
of like craving of food

02:25:51.080 --> 02:25:54.620
all the time that you
can have is terrible.

02:25:54.620 --> 02:25:56.180
That's a terrible
state to be in.

02:25:56.180 --> 02:25:59.360
And this, I think, is a
perfect segue way for something

02:25:59.360 --> 02:26:04.790
that first brought
us together, which

02:26:04.790 --> 02:26:06.830
was this thing about
artificial sweeteners.

02:26:06.830 --> 02:26:11.160
And let me just, for the
record, be very clear.

02:26:11.160 --> 02:26:14.870
I have long ingested foods
with artificial sweeteners.

02:26:14.870 --> 02:26:19.130
So throughout graduate school,
I didn't have the best habits.

02:26:19.130 --> 02:26:21.180
They're healthier now
than they were back then.

02:26:21.180 --> 02:26:23.780
But I would drink a Diet
Coke or two per day.

02:26:23.780 --> 02:26:25.970
I still have the
occasional Diet Coke,

02:26:25.970 --> 02:26:30.800
I'm not completely
averse to drink something

02:26:30.800 --> 02:26:33.410
that has artificial sweetener.

02:26:33.410 --> 02:26:35.540
Although, I do avoid
sucralose for reasons

02:26:35.540 --> 02:26:38.630
that maybe I can get
into a little bit later.

02:26:38.630 --> 02:26:40.550
But a lot of the
things I consume

02:26:40.550 --> 02:26:43.820
contain stevia, which
is not artificial

02:26:43.820 --> 02:26:46.490
but it is a plant-based
non-caloric-- or low caloric

02:26:46.490 --> 02:26:47.660
sweetener.

02:26:47.660 --> 02:26:50.240
And I don't have a
problem with that.

02:26:50.240 --> 02:26:53.750
I became very interested in
artificial sweeteners because

02:26:53.750 --> 02:26:56.780
of the animal data
pointing to the idea

02:26:56.780 --> 02:26:59.540
that they may disrupt
the gut microbiome,

02:26:59.540 --> 02:27:02.000
and then disrupt the gut
microbiome, as you pointed out,

02:27:02.000 --> 02:27:03.260
is a very broad statement.

02:27:03.260 --> 02:27:05.090
We don't really
know the percentage

02:27:05.090 --> 02:27:10.520
of lactobacillus, exobacilius,
or whatever ilius in there--

02:27:10.520 --> 02:27:12.890
they all seem to end in ilius--

02:27:12.890 --> 02:27:13.808
is ideal.

02:27:13.808 --> 02:27:15.350
And in fact, a lot
of these companies

02:27:15.350 --> 02:27:17.870
that are having people
send in their stool samples

02:27:17.870 --> 02:27:19.380
for analysis of the microbiome.

02:27:19.380 --> 02:27:21.590
I mean, take note,
we don't really

02:27:21.590 --> 02:27:23.540
know what a healthy
microbiome looks like,

02:27:23.540 --> 02:27:26.250
but we know what an unhealthy
microbiome might look like.

02:27:26.250 --> 02:27:28.820
And it's one that doesn't have
a lot of diversity in there.

02:27:28.820 --> 02:27:30.555
So I was interested
in that, then

02:27:30.555 --> 02:27:32.930
there's the recent human study
which we should definitely

02:27:32.930 --> 02:27:33.860
get into.

02:27:33.860 --> 02:27:36.710
But I was mostly interested
in artificial sweeteners

02:27:36.710 --> 02:27:41.733
for the reason that there is
this food conditioning effect.

02:27:41.733 --> 02:27:43.400
And you see it in
animals and you see it

02:27:43.400 --> 02:27:46.070
in humans that if you ingest--

02:27:46.070 --> 02:27:47.810
well, coffee is a
really good example.

02:27:47.810 --> 02:27:49.518
Coffee doesn't actually
taste good folks,

02:27:49.518 --> 02:27:50.435
even though I like it.

02:27:50.435 --> 02:27:52.310
But when you taste coffee
for the first time,

02:27:52.310 --> 02:27:54.440
most people think it's
bitter and disgusting.

02:27:54.440 --> 02:27:56.682
Most, everybody,
like 95% of people

02:27:56.682 --> 02:27:57.890
say, this doesn't taste good.

02:27:57.890 --> 02:27:59.060
LAYNE NORTON: Wine,
beer are same thing.

02:27:59.060 --> 02:27:59.300
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:27:59.300 --> 02:28:01.592
But people have learned to
associate the state of being

02:28:01.592 --> 02:28:03.770
caffeinated, which most
people like in order

02:28:03.770 --> 02:28:05.930
to just feel normal,
right, caffeine

02:28:05.930 --> 02:28:10.130
is one of the few drugs we
ingest just to feel ourselves

02:28:10.130 --> 02:28:12.170
enough that soon--

02:28:12.170 --> 02:28:14.570
myself included,
really look forward to

02:28:14.570 --> 02:28:16.310
and enjoy a cup of coffee.

02:28:16.310 --> 02:28:19.110
So it's a powerful
example, in my opinion,

02:28:19.110 --> 02:28:20.448
of the food conditioning effect.

02:28:20.448 --> 02:28:21.740
So it's like a Pavlovian thing.

02:28:21.740 --> 02:28:23.240
Instead of
salivating, you crave.

02:28:23.240 --> 02:28:24.315
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

02:28:24.315 --> 02:28:27.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And it did
seem that this study from Dana

02:28:27.680 --> 02:28:30.530
Small's lab, which admittedly
was a small no pun intended

02:28:30.530 --> 02:28:32.480
study itself, not
very many subjects,

02:28:32.480 --> 02:28:34.820
showed that if you ingested
artificial sweeteners along

02:28:34.820 --> 02:28:38.090
with food that contained
glucose, that you could maybe

02:28:38.090 --> 02:28:40.700
even get a heightened
glucose response just

02:28:40.700 --> 02:28:43.160
from the artificial
sweeteners after a while.

02:28:43.160 --> 02:28:46.220
You and I connected over
this study on social media.

02:28:46.220 --> 02:28:50.075
You pointed out that the
design, the study wasn't superb.

02:28:50.075 --> 02:28:52.497
There was co-consumption
of glucose,

02:28:52.497 --> 02:28:53.580
which made it complicated.

02:28:53.580 --> 02:28:54.690
We can go into that.

02:28:54.690 --> 02:28:56.990
But the reason I'm spouting
off all this context

02:28:56.990 --> 02:28:59.055
is, artificial sweeteners
are many things.

02:28:59.055 --> 02:29:00.680
So I'd like to talk
about their effects

02:29:00.680 --> 02:29:03.710
on blood sugar in
the acute sense

02:29:03.710 --> 02:29:06.650
and according to what we
might ingest them with

02:29:06.650 --> 02:29:08.870
and how they might be
changing blood sugar

02:29:08.870 --> 02:29:11.420
regulation at the level
of brain and/or body.

02:29:11.420 --> 02:29:13.730
And then the gut
microbiome data, I think,

02:29:13.730 --> 02:29:15.980
are interesting
enough to discuss.

02:29:15.980 --> 02:29:19.490
And I have changed my view
on artificial sweeteners

02:29:19.490 --> 02:29:21.020
based on what you've taught me.

02:29:21.020 --> 02:29:22.730
So this is a case
where I've completely

02:29:22.730 --> 02:29:26.480
changed my view,
which is that, now, I

02:29:26.480 --> 02:29:29.210
don't have any problem
with them whatsoever based

02:29:29.210 --> 02:29:32.820
on the current data, which is
not to say that I'm gulping

02:29:32.820 --> 02:29:38.070
down cup fulls of sucralose,
but I feel OK ingesting

02:29:38.070 --> 02:29:41.550
some stevia and some aspartame
and I'm not too worried

02:29:41.550 --> 02:29:42.120
about it.

02:29:42.120 --> 02:29:42.912
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

02:29:42.912 --> 02:29:50.080
So I think stepping
back from a broad view,

02:29:50.080 --> 02:29:53.260
we have to think about, again,
the hierarchy of importance.

02:29:53.260 --> 02:29:55.750
And what are you replacing with?

02:29:55.750 --> 02:30:00.700
So there is no situation
where it is not a net positive

02:30:00.700 --> 02:30:02.950
to take somebody who drinks
sugar sweetened beverages

02:30:02.950 --> 02:30:04.420
and have them drink
an artificially

02:30:04.420 --> 02:30:06.187
sweetened beverage.

02:30:06.187 --> 02:30:08.770
In the meta analysis, there was
actually a recent network meta

02:30:08.770 --> 02:30:15.100
analysis looking at markers
of adiposity, HbA1c,

02:30:15.100 --> 02:30:16.600
a bunch of different
health markers,

02:30:16.600 --> 02:30:20.590
and when you substitute,
we'll call it

02:30:20.590 --> 02:30:23.800
non-nutritive sweeteners,
and stevia is not artificial.

02:30:23.800 --> 02:30:28.150
But so when you substitute
NNS for the sugar-sweetened

02:30:28.150 --> 02:30:32.295
beverages, you see improvements
in a lot of different things.

02:30:32.295 --> 02:30:34.420
What was really interesting
about this network meta

02:30:34.420 --> 02:30:38.080
analysis was they also
looked at water substitution

02:30:38.080 --> 02:30:40.240
in place of
sugar-sweetened beverages.

02:30:40.240 --> 02:30:42.628
And the effect wasn't
as powerful as--

02:30:42.628 --> 02:30:44.420
and these are randomized
controlled trials.

02:30:44.420 --> 02:30:46.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So artificial
sweetener containing beverages

02:30:46.990 --> 02:30:48.340
are more beneficial--

02:30:48.340 --> 02:30:51.220
LAYNE NORTON: Were
better for adiposity--

02:30:51.220 --> 02:30:54.820
for improving adiposity, and
then in the health markers,

02:30:54.820 --> 02:30:57.340
it was kind of a wash.

02:30:57.340 --> 02:31:02.290
Water and non-nutritive
sweetener, beverages

02:31:02.290 --> 02:31:03.172
performed similar.

02:31:03.172 --> 02:31:05.380
But there were better than
sugar-sweetened beverages,

02:31:05.380 --> 02:31:06.880
obviously.

02:31:06.880 --> 02:31:11.280
So they then based on--

02:31:11.280 --> 02:31:13.350
a network meta
analysis is where you

02:31:13.350 --> 02:31:17.560
can compare to things that
didn't get compared directly.

02:31:17.560 --> 02:31:20.580
So there's not many studies
comparing NNS versus water

02:31:20.580 --> 02:31:21.430
directly.

02:31:21.430 --> 02:31:23.370
But if you have a
common comparator,

02:31:23.370 --> 02:31:25.950
so if you compare A to B
and B gets compared to C,

02:31:25.950 --> 02:31:29.670
you compare A to C based on
how they interacted with B.

02:31:29.670 --> 02:31:31.590
Butchering it a
little, bit but that's

02:31:31.590 --> 02:31:33.910
the crux of a network
meta analysis.

02:31:33.910 --> 02:31:38.280
So they looked at
NNS versus water

02:31:38.280 --> 02:31:41.520
and found that actually,
in NNS was slightly

02:31:41.520 --> 02:31:43.308
better for improving adiposity.

02:31:43.308 --> 02:31:45.975
ANDREW HUBERMAN: NNS, of course,
being non-nutritive sweeteners.

02:31:45.975 --> 02:31:46.166
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

02:31:46.166 --> 02:31:47.083
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:31:47.083 --> 02:31:51.090
LAYNE NORTON: So now, again,
if you like drinking water

02:31:51.090 --> 02:31:52.080
and you don't want to--

02:31:52.080 --> 02:31:54.900
I'm not trying to convince
anybody to do that.

02:31:54.900 --> 02:31:57.060
What that seems to
suggest is there

02:31:57.060 --> 02:31:59.100
is a little bit of an
appetite suppressant

02:31:59.100 --> 02:32:01.260
effect from these
artificial sweeteners

02:32:01.260 --> 02:32:03.000
or non-nutritive sweeteners.

02:32:03.000 --> 02:32:05.190
Now, this gets a little
bit more complicated

02:32:05.190 --> 02:32:07.140
because if these
were people drinking

02:32:07.140 --> 02:32:08.670
sugar-sweetened beverages,
maybe they've already

02:32:08.670 --> 02:32:10.628
developed a sweet taste
and try to go to water,

02:32:10.628 --> 02:32:12.017
it's too much of
a jump for them.

02:32:12.017 --> 02:32:14.100
And so going to having
something like intermediate

02:32:14.100 --> 02:32:14.850
is a little bit better.

02:32:14.850 --> 02:32:16.320
Like, there's a lot
wrapped up in this.

02:32:16.320 --> 02:32:18.250
But these are the randomized
controlled trials,

02:32:18.250 --> 02:32:20.458
which are a little bit more
tightly controlled, which

02:32:20.458 --> 02:32:22.290
I tend to default
to a little bit more

02:32:22.290 --> 02:32:25.440
than I do the epidemiology,
which epidemiology is just

02:32:25.440 --> 02:32:28.710
so messy, because sure,
non-nutritive sweetener

02:32:28.710 --> 02:32:31.080
consumption may be associated
with different things,

02:32:31.080 --> 02:32:34.500
but there's also a whole other
set of lifestyle and habits

02:32:34.500 --> 02:32:36.160
that are tied up in that.

02:32:36.160 --> 02:32:38.190
So I tend to hang my
hat a little bit more

02:32:38.190 --> 02:32:39.700
on the randomized
controlled trials.

02:32:39.700 --> 02:32:40.980
So understanding that.

02:32:40.980 --> 02:32:42.450
OK.

02:32:42.450 --> 02:32:47.020
Now, all things being
equal, understanding

02:32:47.020 --> 02:32:49.600
that this is a tool that
may help some people,

02:32:49.600 --> 02:32:51.760
and whenever I post about
non-nutritive sweeteners

02:32:51.760 --> 02:32:55.240
in the comments, there's
always one or two or three

02:32:55.240 --> 02:32:58.390
people who say, all I
did was cut out soda.

02:32:58.390 --> 02:33:01.010
And I drank diet soda
instead, and I lost 50 pounds.

02:33:01.010 --> 02:33:02.110
Or I lost 75 pounds.

02:33:02.110 --> 02:33:02.860
I even had 1%.

02:33:02.860 --> 02:33:03.640
I lost 100 pounds.

02:33:03.640 --> 02:33:04.480
That's the only thing I did.

02:33:04.480 --> 02:33:04.930
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Wow.

02:33:04.930 --> 02:33:06.790
LAYNE NORTON: I mean, that's a
pretty massive lever to pull.

02:33:06.790 --> 02:33:09.207
If you consider somebody who
might be having like, I mean,

02:33:09.207 --> 02:33:13.198
five or six Cokes a
day, I mean, we're

02:33:13.198 --> 02:33:14.740
talking a serious
amount of calories.

02:33:14.740 --> 02:33:16.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And that also means

02:33:16.270 --> 02:33:19.030
that by replacing it with
artificial sweetener-containing

02:33:19.030 --> 02:33:23.363
beverages, they did not
replace the soda with food.

02:33:23.363 --> 02:33:24.280
LAYNE NORTON: Correct.

02:33:24.280 --> 02:33:27.730
So now, let's talk about, right?

02:33:27.730 --> 02:33:30.010
This is where we can get
into the micro analysis,

02:33:30.010 --> 02:33:35.070
but is that obese person who
lost 100 pounds by doing that?

02:33:35.070 --> 02:33:37.650
Do I really care about,
maybe, a small alteration

02:33:37.650 --> 02:33:38.970
to their gut microbiome?

02:33:38.970 --> 02:33:41.670
No, because their gut microbiome
is actually much more healthy

02:33:41.670 --> 02:33:45.820
now by them having lost all
that excess adipose tissue.

02:33:45.820 --> 02:33:50.470
So again, the ranking of
what I'm worried about

02:33:50.470 --> 02:33:53.190
can change depending on
the specific situation.

02:33:53.190 --> 02:33:58.200
Now, let's take somebody like
me who's lean and doesn't really

02:33:58.200 --> 02:34:00.480
have any health problems
that I'm aware of.

02:34:03.750 --> 02:34:06.110
What about artificial
sweeteners for me?

02:34:06.110 --> 02:34:10.640
Well, for me, I got using them
because of bodybuilding contest

02:34:10.640 --> 02:34:12.620
prep, because it was
about the only appetite

02:34:12.620 --> 02:34:15.720
suppressant that worked for me.

02:34:15.720 --> 02:34:20.130
But do I think that
they are healthful?

02:34:20.130 --> 02:34:22.020
Probably not.

02:34:22.020 --> 02:34:25.000
Do I think they're unhealthy?

02:34:25.000 --> 02:34:26.920
I would say based
on the current data,

02:34:26.920 --> 02:34:29.200
I don't think that
they're unhealthy.

02:34:29.200 --> 02:34:33.440
Now, the information
on blood glucose.

02:34:33.440 --> 02:34:36.430
So there's-- some of the
problems with some of these

02:34:36.430 --> 02:34:40.240
meta analyzes or these
reviews is they lump all

02:34:40.240 --> 02:34:43.228
the non-nutritive sweeteners
together and then they may say,

02:34:43.228 --> 02:34:45.770
well, there's no effect on this
or there's an effect on this.

02:34:45.770 --> 02:34:48.730
Well, the problem is these
are different molecules

02:34:48.730 --> 02:34:50.530
and they can
interact differently.

02:34:50.530 --> 02:34:53.380
Aspartame, very clearly,
seems to have no effect

02:34:53.380 --> 02:34:55.450
on blood sugar or insulin.

02:34:55.450 --> 02:34:57.595
That has been repeatedly shown.

02:35:00.130 --> 02:35:02.500
Stevia doesn't appear
to have much effect.

02:35:02.500 --> 02:35:05.800
Saccharin and sucralose,
the jury is mixed.

02:35:05.800 --> 02:35:07.810
Now, there was the
study that we first

02:35:07.810 --> 02:35:11.350
connected on which, I
think, their primary outcome

02:35:11.350 --> 02:35:14.230
measure was actually they were
looking at the sweet taste.

02:35:14.230 --> 02:35:16.480
Like how it affected
sweet taste.

02:35:16.480 --> 02:35:22.180
So what they did was, the group
that was getting the sucralose

02:35:22.180 --> 02:35:25.030
was also paired
with maltodextrin.

02:35:25.030 --> 02:35:28.220
The control group
was getting sucrose,

02:35:28.220 --> 02:35:32.670
which is an appropriate
way to compare

02:35:32.670 --> 02:35:34.920
the sweet taste because
maltodextrin is not

02:35:34.920 --> 02:35:36.310
as sweet as sucrose.

02:35:36.310 --> 02:35:39.503
So when you're trying to
combine sucralose, which

02:35:39.503 --> 02:35:41.670
is already sweet, with
another form of carbohydrate,

02:35:41.670 --> 02:35:45.000
you'd want something less
sweet compared to your control.

02:35:45.000 --> 02:35:51.540
But for the outcome measure
of insulin and blood glucose,

02:35:51.540 --> 02:35:53.970
probably, not as
appropriate because we know

02:35:53.970 --> 02:35:58.950
maltodextrin has a much higher
glycemic index than sucrose.

02:35:58.950 --> 02:36:01.710
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So they
appropriately controlled

02:36:01.710 --> 02:36:04.740
for taste, but not for the
effect of the sweeteners.

02:36:04.740 --> 02:36:07.440
And I think that that
was a key component.

02:36:07.440 --> 02:36:09.570
And I think, yeah, the
part of that study that

02:36:09.570 --> 02:36:13.920
intrigued me actually was
in a talk version of that

02:36:13.920 --> 02:36:16.800
because that study drove
me to watch a talk that--

02:36:16.800 --> 02:36:19.720
and we'll get Dana Small on
the podcast at some point,

02:36:19.720 --> 02:36:22.603
hopefully-- was that they
had kids do this study.

02:36:22.603 --> 02:36:24.270
And they actually had
to cease the study

02:36:24.270 --> 02:36:26.310
because a couple of the
kids became prediabetic.

02:36:26.310 --> 02:36:29.388
I mean, it seemed like there
was something hazardous about--

02:36:29.388 --> 02:36:30.930
this was the Yale
School of Medicine.

02:36:30.930 --> 02:36:31.680
It's a good place.

02:36:31.680 --> 02:36:34.710
I mean, there's a
range everywhere.

02:36:34.710 --> 02:36:38.580
But it just seemed like there's
something about sweet taste

02:36:38.580 --> 02:36:41.040
that if taken to
the extreme might

02:36:41.040 --> 02:36:43.650
be able to impact blood sugar.

02:36:43.650 --> 02:36:48.390
This has impacted my behavior.

02:36:48.390 --> 02:36:53.580
And I try to avoid really
sweet things, unless they're

02:36:53.580 --> 02:36:55.980
exceptionally delicious or
the occasion calls for them

02:36:55.980 --> 02:36:57.960
because I do think
that it increases

02:36:57.960 --> 02:36:59.640
my craving for sweet things.

02:36:59.640 --> 02:37:02.400
LAYNE NORTON: Well, it might
not be necessarily a craving,

02:37:02.400 --> 02:37:06.060
but it just programs you.

02:37:06.060 --> 02:37:08.370
Your taste buds are
extremely adaptable.

02:37:08.370 --> 02:37:12.570
So take, for
example, Indian food.

02:37:12.570 --> 02:37:15.432
If you bring Indian
people over to America

02:37:15.432 --> 02:37:16.890
and have them eat
some of our food,

02:37:16.890 --> 02:37:19.770
they think it tastes extremely
bland because they're

02:37:19.770 --> 02:37:22.920
used to such spicy
food that unless they

02:37:22.920 --> 02:37:25.680
have a certain level of spice,
they can hardly even taste it.

02:37:25.680 --> 02:37:27.630
If you've ever done
a high-sodium diet

02:37:27.630 --> 02:37:31.155
and then gone to a low-sodium
diet, it feels very bland.

02:37:31.155 --> 02:37:33.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's
where I started from.

02:37:33.030 --> 02:37:36.720
LAYNE NORTON: But over time,
your taste buds adjust.

02:37:36.720 --> 02:37:38.040
So sweet is the same thing.

02:37:38.040 --> 02:37:40.420
If you're used to
eating a lot of sweet,

02:37:40.420 --> 02:37:42.030
you get desensitized to it.

02:37:42.030 --> 02:37:44.400
And then if you go to
something less sweet,

02:37:44.400 --> 02:37:47.940
it can taste-bland at first.

02:37:47.940 --> 02:37:50.940
Over time, it'll get better.

02:37:50.940 --> 02:37:52.500
So I think it's
one of those things

02:37:52.500 --> 02:37:55.710
that, again, it depends
on the situation.

02:37:55.710 --> 02:37:57.450
If somebody's obese
and they said, well,

02:37:57.450 --> 02:37:59.580
this is going to
help me eliminate

02:37:59.580 --> 02:38:01.687
sugar-sweetened
beverage, why would you

02:38:01.687 --> 02:38:03.270
want to take that
tool away from them?

02:38:03.270 --> 02:38:04.920
That's a great lever to pull.

02:38:04.920 --> 02:38:07.920
I mean, if somebody can
lose literally 100 pounds

02:38:07.920 --> 02:38:10.710
from just one change in
lifestyle that's not even

02:38:10.710 --> 02:38:16.120
really that inconvenient of
a change, that is powerful.

02:38:16.120 --> 02:38:19.320
But again, is it the most
healthy thing they could do?

02:38:19.320 --> 02:38:23.330
And I think that's what
tends to get asked.

02:38:23.330 --> 02:38:25.090
We don't know.

02:38:25.090 --> 02:38:27.770
Is it healthier than water?

02:38:27.770 --> 02:38:31.370
Probably not maybe
as healthy as it.

02:38:31.370 --> 02:38:34.400
Who knows?

02:38:34.400 --> 02:38:38.150
But I really make
all those caveats

02:38:38.150 --> 02:38:41.840
because you don't want to
have people who could use this

02:38:41.840 --> 02:38:44.540
as a tool to think, well, no,
I can't do this because it's

02:38:44.540 --> 02:38:46.780
actually bad for me.

02:38:46.780 --> 02:38:49.480
If it helps you lose
50 pounds or 75 pounds

02:38:49.480 --> 02:38:51.855
or whatever it is, trust me,
it's not bad for you, right?

02:38:51.855 --> 02:38:53.313
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, it does seem

02:38:53.313 --> 02:38:54.793
to increase your
satiety signals.

02:38:54.793 --> 02:38:56.710
What do you think about
the microbiome effects

02:38:56.710 --> 02:38:57.753
in this recent study?

02:38:57.753 --> 02:38:59.170
Because the recent
study, I think,

02:38:59.170 --> 02:39:00.760
had some nice features to it.

02:39:00.760 --> 02:39:04.262
And you've done a detailed
description of the study.

02:39:04.262 --> 02:39:05.470
So for those that want that--

02:39:05.470 --> 02:39:06.850
LAYNE NORTON: Is this the
two-week study or the 10-week?

02:39:06.850 --> 02:39:07.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah,
the two-week study.

02:39:07.780 --> 02:39:08.280
Yeah.

02:39:08.280 --> 02:39:09.790
And we will provide a link.

02:39:09.790 --> 02:39:12.490
You did an excellent video
on your YouTube channel

02:39:12.490 --> 02:39:13.840
that really parses each piece.

02:39:13.840 --> 02:39:17.620
But they compared the
various artificial sweeteners

02:39:17.620 --> 02:39:21.098
and looked at the glucose
response, looked at microbiome,

02:39:21.098 --> 02:39:22.390
a number of different measures.

02:39:22.390 --> 02:39:23.740
What was your general takeaway?

02:39:23.740 --> 02:39:25.750
And this was in
humans for, I think,

02:39:25.750 --> 02:39:28.090
the first time looking
at microbiome in humans

02:39:28.090 --> 02:39:29.470
due to artificial sweetener--

02:39:29.470 --> 02:39:31.588
LAYNE NORTON: There
are a few studies

02:39:31.588 --> 02:39:33.880
on the microbiome in humans
with artificial sweeteners.

02:39:33.880 --> 02:39:37.142
The first two that came out
showed pretty much no effect,

02:39:37.142 --> 02:39:39.100
but they were a little
bit shorter in duration.

02:39:39.100 --> 02:39:40.267
They were two to four weeks.

02:39:40.267 --> 02:39:43.630
And again, it depends on what
bacteria are getting measured.

02:39:43.630 --> 02:39:46.940
There's many different
kinds of bacteria,

02:39:46.940 --> 02:39:49.790
so they could just be measuring
one that didn't change.

02:39:49.790 --> 02:39:51.790
And then there was a
10-week study that came out

02:39:51.790 --> 02:39:53.770
that got a lot of press.

02:39:53.770 --> 02:39:55.360
And they showed--

02:39:55.360 --> 02:39:57.220
I think it was sucralose.

02:39:57.220 --> 02:39:58.540
I think.

02:39:58.540 --> 02:40:03.080
They showed an effect of
change on the gut microbiome.

02:40:03.080 --> 02:40:05.170
Now, what was
interesting is when

02:40:05.170 --> 02:40:07.960
I went into the
species that changed,

02:40:07.960 --> 02:40:11.230
the species that changed the
most compared to the control

02:40:11.230 --> 02:40:13.325
was a species called--

02:40:13.325 --> 02:40:14.950
I'm going to butcher
the name, but it's

02:40:14.950 --> 02:40:16.960
like Blautia coccoides,
I think it's called.

02:40:16.960 --> 02:40:18.502
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
must say for those

02:40:18.502 --> 02:40:21.730
that work on the microbiome,
it's so difficult to pronounce.

02:40:21.730 --> 02:40:23.980
I mean, you need a
nomenclature committee,

02:40:23.980 --> 02:40:25.090
and you need acronyms.

02:40:25.090 --> 02:40:25.600
I'm sorry.

02:40:25.600 --> 02:40:26.290
Just do it.

02:40:26.290 --> 02:40:27.010
LAYNE NORTON: [LAUGHS]

02:40:27.010 --> 02:40:28.343
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Enough already.

02:40:28.343 --> 02:40:29.093
You're killing us.

02:40:29.093 --> 02:40:30.385
LAYNE NORTON: We'll call it BC.

02:40:30.385 --> 02:40:31.060
We call it BC.

02:40:31.060 --> 02:40:31.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: BC, thank you.

02:40:31.510 --> 02:40:33.070
We're going to start the
nomenclature committee

02:40:33.070 --> 02:40:34.840
without you if you
don't do it soon.

02:40:34.840 --> 02:40:38.650
LAYNE NORTON: So they noticed
that this went up by, like,

02:40:38.650 --> 02:40:39.610
three- to four-fold.

02:40:39.610 --> 02:40:42.020
So I kind of went down
the rabbit hole on this.

02:40:42.020 --> 02:40:45.490
So interestingly, that
particular species of bacteria

02:40:45.490 --> 02:40:48.790
is actually associated with
lower adiposity, better insulin

02:40:48.790 --> 02:40:49.720
sensitivity.

02:40:49.720 --> 02:40:52.840
And people who are obese
and children who are obese

02:40:52.840 --> 02:40:54.560
tend to have less of it.

02:40:54.560 --> 02:40:56.170
So I said, well,
based on that study,

02:40:56.170 --> 02:40:58.780
you can actually argue that
maybe sucralose actually

02:40:58.780 --> 02:41:00.227
improves the gut microbiome.

02:41:00.227 --> 02:41:01.810
Now, again, I'm not
making that claim.

02:41:01.810 --> 02:41:05.320
Because we have a hard
time understanding

02:41:05.320 --> 02:41:09.470
what a healthy microbiome
looks like already.

02:41:09.470 --> 02:41:15.810
This last study that came out,
my biggest take-home was I

02:41:15.810 --> 02:41:18.210
think it's safe to say that
some of these non-nutritive

02:41:18.210 --> 02:41:20.910
sweeteners are not
metabolically inert.

02:41:20.910 --> 02:41:22.980
There are some effects.

02:41:22.980 --> 02:41:26.580
Now, are those effects
good, bad, or neutral

02:41:26.580 --> 02:41:29.280
I think has yet to
be fully elucidated.

02:41:29.280 --> 02:41:34.470
Now, I focused more on the
blood-glucose responses

02:41:34.470 --> 02:41:35.980
in my analysis.

02:41:35.980 --> 02:41:40.020
So in that 10-week study, they
did oral-glucose tolerance

02:41:40.020 --> 02:41:41.100
test.

02:41:41.100 --> 02:41:46.350
And their conclusion I
didn't really feel like

02:41:46.350 --> 02:41:47.650
fit their data.

02:41:47.650 --> 02:41:49.830
So their conclusion
was that-- and again,

02:41:49.830 --> 02:41:51.480
I think it was sucralose--

02:41:51.480 --> 02:41:56.410
that it elevated blood glucose.

02:41:56.410 --> 02:41:58.100
[SIGHS] And this
is where statistics

02:41:58.100 --> 02:41:59.670
can get kind of tricky.

02:41:59.670 --> 02:42:04.058
So my take-home was the
area under the curve,

02:42:04.058 --> 02:42:05.850
the incremental area
under the curve, which

02:42:05.850 --> 02:42:08.100
is looking at basically
the entire glucose

02:42:08.100 --> 02:42:10.920
response, was not different
between the control

02:42:10.920 --> 02:42:12.300
and the sucralose group.

02:42:12.300 --> 02:42:14.310
To me, that's the
biggest take-home.

02:42:14.310 --> 02:42:16.410
There was one time
point at the end

02:42:16.410 --> 02:42:19.080
of the study in the sucralose
group, the 30-minute time

02:42:19.080 --> 02:42:22.350
point, that was statistically
significantly higher blood

02:42:22.350 --> 02:42:27.623
glucose than the control group.

02:42:27.623 --> 02:42:29.290
It's kind of one of
those things where I

02:42:29.290 --> 02:42:31.900
go, OK, it was one time point.

02:42:31.900 --> 02:42:33.630
It's statistically significant.

02:42:33.630 --> 02:42:36.030
But even then, we've seen
things be statistically

02:42:36.030 --> 02:42:37.950
significant that end
up being data artifacts

02:42:37.950 --> 02:42:39.302
because they're not reproduced.

02:42:39.302 --> 02:42:41.260
So I'm not saying that's
what's happening here.

02:42:41.260 --> 02:42:43.560
But again, the overall
area under the curve

02:42:43.560 --> 02:42:44.310
was not different.

02:42:44.310 --> 02:42:46.018
So to me, that was
the biggest take-home.

02:42:46.018 --> 02:42:48.420
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And papers,
we should probably mention,

02:42:48.420 --> 02:42:52.500
are published because
of effects, generally.

02:42:52.500 --> 02:42:54.985
[CHUCKLES] Lack of
effect, harder to publish.

02:42:54.985 --> 02:42:56.610
LAYNE NORTON: Null
hypothesis doesn't--

02:42:56.610 --> 02:42:59.700
it's actually really unfortunate
because a null hypothesis

02:42:59.700 --> 02:43:03.630
is just as useful data as
the non-null hypothesis.

02:43:03.630 --> 02:43:04.500
But you're right.

02:43:04.500 --> 02:43:07.080
There is a very strong
publication bias

02:43:07.080 --> 02:43:09.485
towards showing an
effect versus not.

02:43:09.485 --> 02:43:10.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, unless you

02:43:10.860 --> 02:43:14.310
can flip a field on its head
entirely by showing something

02:43:14.310 --> 02:43:18.510
did not happen, typically
the positive result out

02:43:18.510 --> 02:43:21.060
does the negative
result, positive meaning

02:43:21.060 --> 02:43:24.600
you see a result. And then
of course, it's one study.

02:43:24.600 --> 02:43:25.320
And--

02:43:25.320 --> 02:43:26.070
LAYNE NORTON: Yes.

02:43:26.070 --> 02:43:29.340
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --I think that,
as you talked about earlier,

02:43:29.340 --> 02:43:32.370
the center of mass of
data in a given field

02:43:32.370 --> 02:43:36.660
are probably the best basis for
what we should do in terms of--

02:43:36.660 --> 02:43:39.810
and so I'm not changing my
behavior around the intake

02:43:39.810 --> 02:43:42.060
of artificial sweeteners.

02:43:42.060 --> 02:43:44.970
I personally am still going to
consume stevia and aspartame

02:43:44.970 --> 02:43:46.260
in relatively small amounts.

02:43:46.260 --> 02:43:47.052
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

02:43:47.052 --> 02:43:50.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But
now I'm thinking, well,

02:43:50.010 --> 02:43:51.510
OK, if something
contains sucralose,

02:43:51.510 --> 02:43:53.910
I don't have to perhaps
actively avoid it.

02:43:53.910 --> 02:43:55.005
Whereas before I was.

02:43:55.005 --> 02:43:56.130
I was actively avoiding it.

02:43:56.130 --> 02:43:58.830
LAYNE NORTON: So the
new study I thought

02:43:58.830 --> 02:44:02.410
was very elegantly--
very involved.

02:44:02.410 --> 02:44:04.980
I mean, to be quite
frank, some of the animal

02:44:04.980 --> 02:44:07.587
stuff they did was
extremely impressive.

02:44:07.587 --> 02:44:09.420
So there was actually
two arms to the study.

02:44:09.420 --> 02:44:10.200
One was the human arm.

02:44:10.200 --> 02:44:11.158
One was the animal arm.

02:44:11.158 --> 02:44:14.920
I focused much more on
the human side of it.

02:44:14.920 --> 02:44:16.920
So basically, this
was a two-week study.

02:44:16.920 --> 02:44:20.490
And the really unique aspect
of this, which I think

02:44:20.490 --> 02:44:22.620
is both a strength
and a weakness,

02:44:22.620 --> 02:44:27.690
they had almost 1,400
people apply for this study.

02:44:27.690 --> 02:44:31.290
And they only had 120, I think,
that actually went into it.

02:44:31.290 --> 02:44:35.363
Because they did a very detailed
food analysis of these folks.

02:44:35.363 --> 02:44:36.780
All of these people
said that they

02:44:36.780 --> 02:44:40.050
avoided artificial sweeteners
or didn't consume them.

02:44:40.050 --> 02:44:43.200
And I think people don't realize
how ubiquitous sweeteners are.

02:44:43.200 --> 02:44:44.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Prior to the study,

02:44:44.700 --> 02:44:46.830
these people were like--
it was like jury selection.

02:44:46.830 --> 02:44:47.310
They'd never--

02:44:47.310 --> 02:44:47.550
LAYNE NORTON: Yes.

02:44:47.550 --> 02:44:48.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
like not ever hearing

02:44:48.690 --> 02:44:50.280
of the plaintiff
and the defendant.

02:44:50.280 --> 02:44:52.443
These are these mutant
people who have never

02:44:52.443 --> 02:44:53.610
had an artificial sweetener.

02:44:53.610 --> 02:44:56.580
LAYNE NORTON: Right,
so the strength is now

02:44:56.580 --> 02:45:00.870
you don't have a lot
of preexisting effects

02:45:00.870 --> 02:45:03.120
that may be clouding
what would actually

02:45:03.120 --> 02:45:04.230
happen when you add it in.

02:45:04.230 --> 02:45:06.188
For example, if you have
people who are already

02:45:06.188 --> 02:45:09.300
consuming artificial
sweeteners and then you

02:45:09.300 --> 02:45:11.640
have them consume
artificial sweeteners,

02:45:11.640 --> 02:45:15.110
the likelihood things are going
to change is pretty low, right?

02:45:15.110 --> 02:45:16.890
So I think that
that's a strength.

02:45:16.890 --> 02:45:18.500
It's also a weakness.

02:45:18.500 --> 02:45:20.690
And I want to be really
careful because I

02:45:20.690 --> 02:45:23.000
think people took my words
a little bit too far, which

02:45:23.000 --> 02:45:25.250
means I probably didn't do
a good job of being nuanced

02:45:25.250 --> 02:45:27.230
enough.

02:45:27.230 --> 02:45:29.580
There is the possibility
for a placebo effect here.

02:45:29.580 --> 02:45:34.760
So to me, if somebody has gone
through that much painstaking

02:45:34.760 --> 02:45:37.010
care to avoid
artificial sweeteners,

02:45:37.010 --> 02:45:38.870
it's likely they have
a preconceived notion

02:45:38.870 --> 02:45:41.360
that those are bad for
you, because they're

02:45:41.360 --> 02:45:43.082
difficult to avoid.

02:45:43.082 --> 02:45:45.290
Yes, it's possible if they're
eating a very minimally

02:45:45.290 --> 02:45:47.498
processed diet that they're
just not exposed to them.

02:45:47.498 --> 02:45:49.770
And that's very true, as well.

02:45:49.770 --> 02:45:51.680
But the other thing
that the researchers

02:45:51.680 --> 02:45:54.772
acknowledged was they weren't
able to blind the study.

02:45:54.772 --> 02:45:57.230
Because if you've never had an
artificial sweetener before,

02:45:57.230 --> 02:45:59.180
you're only used
to regular sugar

02:45:59.180 --> 02:46:01.380
and you have an artificial
sweetener, you know.

02:46:01.380 --> 02:46:02.645
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You taste it.

02:46:02.645 --> 02:46:03.440
LAYNE NORTON: You know.

02:46:03.440 --> 02:46:04.160
It's still sweet.

02:46:04.160 --> 02:46:05.120
But it's not the same sweet.

02:46:05.120 --> 02:46:07.495
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And there's
an interesting effect there,

02:46:07.495 --> 02:46:09.950
where a lot of people don't
like the taste of aspartame

02:46:09.950 --> 02:46:11.570
the first time.

02:46:11.570 --> 02:46:14.750
I actually quit drinking
diet soda for a while,

02:46:14.750 --> 02:46:16.940
thinking I should,
and then had one.

02:46:16.940 --> 02:46:18.390
It tasted really--

02:46:18.390 --> 02:46:21.110
I can only describe it as
kind of artificial, chemical.

02:46:21.110 --> 02:46:23.210
And then pretty soon,
it tasted great again.

02:46:23.210 --> 02:46:23.510
LAYNE NORTON: Yep.

02:46:23.510 --> 02:46:25.843
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And so there
is some attenuation there.

02:46:25.843 --> 02:46:27.680
And whether or not
that's central,

02:46:27.680 --> 02:46:30.140
meaning within the brain,
or peripheral, I don't know.

02:46:30.140 --> 02:46:32.210
But very interesting.

02:46:32.210 --> 02:46:37.430
Well, I see you as playing
a critical role in defining

02:46:37.430 --> 02:46:39.470
what is and what
isn't, what still needs

02:46:39.470 --> 02:46:41.960
to be determined in
terms of this landscape

02:46:41.960 --> 02:46:44.420
and the entire landscape
really of nutrition.

02:46:44.420 --> 02:46:47.150
LAYNE NORTON: And that study did
change my opinion in terms of,

02:46:47.150 --> 02:46:52.370
OK, I think we can clearly say
now that these aren't neutral.

02:46:52.370 --> 02:46:54.953
Or sorry, that they're
not inert, right?

02:46:54.953 --> 02:46:56.870
That was the thought
process before, was well.

02:46:56.870 --> 02:47:00.590
They're not digested or
whatnot, so they must be inert.

02:47:00.590 --> 02:47:03.780
That doesn't appear
to be the case.

02:47:03.780 --> 02:47:10.060
But again, when we look at the
blood glucose data, there's--

02:47:10.060 --> 02:47:11.790
and I'm not saying
this is what happened.

02:47:11.790 --> 02:47:12.790
I want to be very clear.

02:47:12.790 --> 02:47:14.332
I'm not saying this
is what happened.

02:47:14.332 --> 02:47:17.600
I'm saying it's
possible this happened.

02:47:17.600 --> 02:47:20.410
And so this is why we need
more studies to verify.

02:47:20.410 --> 02:47:22.300
If these people had
a preconceived notion

02:47:22.300 --> 02:47:24.350
that artificial sweeteners
were bad for them,

02:47:24.350 --> 02:47:27.700
it's possible, knowing they're
ingesting artificial sweeteners

02:47:27.700 --> 02:47:30.100
that they could have had
a blood glucose response.

02:47:30.100 --> 02:47:34.840
Now, my pushback on my
own point there would be,

02:47:34.840 --> 02:47:38.350
then we should have expected to
see it in all the non-nutritive

02:47:38.350 --> 02:47:39.730
sweeteners, which they didn't.

02:47:39.730 --> 02:47:41.620
It was just in
sucralose and saccharin.

02:47:41.620 --> 02:47:41.950
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, it was kind

02:47:41.950 --> 02:47:44.500
of a graded effect, where
sucralose and saccharin showed

02:47:44.500 --> 02:47:46.075
the most dramatic change.

02:47:46.075 --> 02:47:47.115
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

02:47:47.115 --> 02:47:50.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And stevia and
a few of the others did not.

02:47:50.920 --> 02:47:52.670
LAYNE NORTON: And
the other issue

02:47:52.670 --> 02:47:54.670
I took with it-- maybe
it's a ticky-tack thing--

02:47:54.670 --> 02:47:57.100
was their primary
outcome measure

02:47:57.100 --> 02:48:00.730
was blood glucose, the
oral-glucose tolerance test.

02:48:00.730 --> 02:48:02.938
But they had people administer
their own oral-glucose

02:48:02.938 --> 02:48:05.188
tolerance tests, which
basically they gave them-- they

02:48:05.188 --> 02:48:06.370
said, OK, drink this drink.

02:48:06.370 --> 02:48:08.453
And they were wearing
continuous glucose monitors,

02:48:08.453 --> 02:48:10.640
which should have been fine.

02:48:10.640 --> 02:48:12.490
But again, to me--

02:48:12.490 --> 02:48:14.020
and I'm being ticky tacky.

02:48:14.020 --> 02:48:17.180
And again, I know all studies
are limited by funding.

02:48:17.180 --> 02:48:19.750
So I think overall,
this was a great study.

02:48:19.750 --> 02:48:21.220
But I would have
liked to have seen

02:48:21.220 --> 02:48:27.640
them monitor the
oral-glucose tolerance

02:48:27.640 --> 02:48:28.840
tests to administer it.

02:48:28.840 --> 02:48:29.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, and
what they did before or after.

02:48:29.770 --> 02:48:31.720
You want to know that
they didn't ingest this

02:48:31.720 --> 02:48:32.750
or did ingest that.

02:48:32.750 --> 02:48:33.165
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

02:48:33.165 --> 02:48:33.580
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:48:33.580 --> 02:48:34.872
LAYNE NORTON: Those two things.

02:48:34.872 --> 02:48:38.908
But one other caveat is
it was a two-week study.

02:48:38.908 --> 02:48:41.200
So we've got to be really
careful how much we interpret

02:48:41.200 --> 02:48:43.930
in this because it's
also possible that this

02:48:43.930 --> 02:48:46.060
is a transient effect, right?

02:48:46.060 --> 02:48:47.660
And maybe it goes
away over time.

02:48:47.660 --> 02:48:49.290
We don't know.

02:48:49.290 --> 02:48:52.580
But again, I think
we can clearly

02:48:52.580 --> 02:48:55.340
say it's not inert, right?

02:48:55.340 --> 02:49:00.470
Now, how much emphasis we put
on that, on a two-week study,

02:49:00.470 --> 02:49:05.790
I still will say, OK,
maybe if you're worried,

02:49:05.790 --> 02:49:08.190
don't consume sucralose.

02:49:08.190 --> 02:49:12.600
But if you're 100
pounds overweight

02:49:12.600 --> 02:49:15.930
and you want to use some
sucralose as a replacement

02:49:15.930 --> 02:49:18.212
to help you lose
weight, I would say,

02:49:18.212 --> 02:49:19.920
don't let this study
deter you from doing

02:49:19.920 --> 02:49:21.840
that because the
net effect is still

02:49:21.840 --> 02:49:25.600
going to be more positive than
you not losing the weight,

02:49:25.600 --> 02:49:26.100
right?

02:49:26.100 --> 02:49:28.320
So if it's a tool
that helps you, fine.

02:49:28.320 --> 02:49:31.230
But I do hold open the
idea that, well, there

02:49:31.230 --> 02:49:33.820
could be negative
effects from it, as well.

02:49:33.820 --> 02:49:38.040
But again, we're looking at,
what is the overall outcome?

02:49:38.040 --> 02:49:42.420
And then they examined some
of the different things

02:49:42.420 --> 02:49:45.870
that were increased with
these different sweeteners.

02:49:45.870 --> 02:49:48.482
And again, this word
gets messy because one

02:49:48.482 --> 02:49:50.190
of the things they
saw was a big increase

02:49:50.190 --> 02:49:53.610
in butyrate production from the
change in the gut microbiome

02:49:53.610 --> 02:49:54.692
presumably.

02:49:54.692 --> 02:49:56.400
Well, as we discussed
earlier, butyrate's

02:49:56.400 --> 02:49:58.653
actually associated
with positive outcomes

02:49:58.653 --> 02:50:00.570
in terms of insulin
sensitivity, inflammation,

02:50:00.570 --> 02:50:02.260
and some other things.

02:50:02.260 --> 02:50:04.597
So I want to be real cautious
before people say, well,

02:50:04.597 --> 02:50:06.180
if there's a change
in the microbiome,

02:50:06.180 --> 02:50:07.770
it must be a bad change.

02:50:07.770 --> 02:50:09.100
We don't know.

02:50:09.100 --> 02:50:10.110
It's possible.

02:50:10.110 --> 02:50:13.590
And again, if we have 10 more
studies come out and start

02:50:13.590 --> 02:50:17.340
to show this, then I will start
to shift my personal opinion

02:50:17.340 --> 02:50:18.462
of artificial sweeteners.

02:50:18.462 --> 02:50:19.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So in anticipation

02:50:19.920 --> 02:50:23.010
of sitting down
today, I did solicit

02:50:23.010 --> 02:50:25.540
for questions on social media.

02:50:25.540 --> 02:50:30.150
And one of the questions
that got a lot of upvotes,

02:50:30.150 --> 02:50:33.720
likes if you will,
was one that I think

02:50:33.720 --> 02:50:36.310
raises interesting questions
about short-term and long-term

02:50:36.310 --> 02:50:36.810
health.

02:50:36.810 --> 02:50:37.900
And it's the following.

02:50:37.900 --> 02:50:40.520
I think it's a common scenario.

02:50:40.520 --> 02:50:42.980
A number of people
want to know, what

02:50:42.980 --> 02:50:47.540
is the healthiest way to
approach a rapid weight loss?

02:50:47.540 --> 02:50:49.040
And here what I
think is happening

02:50:49.040 --> 02:50:51.380
is somebody has
an event coming up

02:50:51.380 --> 02:50:55.220
or they're just tired
of being the weight they

02:50:55.220 --> 02:50:58.040
are, carrying the amount
of adipose tissue they are.

02:50:58.040 --> 02:51:00.440
And they wanted to
know whether or not

02:51:00.440 --> 02:51:04.130
it is safe to,
for instance, lose

02:51:04.130 --> 02:51:07.100
three pounds a week for a
few weeks in anticipation

02:51:07.100 --> 02:51:10.910
of a wedding or some other
event and whether or not

02:51:10.910 --> 02:51:14.090
straight caloric restriction
and increasing activity

02:51:14.090 --> 02:51:16.160
is the best way
to approach that,

02:51:16.160 --> 02:51:18.530
with the understanding that
they may gain back a little

02:51:18.530 --> 02:51:19.670
afterwards.

02:51:19.670 --> 02:51:22.550
I think ideally they'd like
to maintain it afterwards.

02:51:22.550 --> 02:51:25.730
But what do you think
of that approach,

02:51:25.730 --> 02:51:30.050
cutting caloric intake
in half, for instance,

02:51:30.050 --> 02:51:33.570
and then also doubling
your physical output?

02:51:33.570 --> 02:51:35.720
LAYNE NORTON: So
it's interesting

02:51:35.720 --> 02:51:38.600
because you might
be surprised by what

02:51:38.600 --> 02:51:41.720
I'm going to say, which is the
research data actually tends

02:51:41.720 --> 02:51:43.970
to suggest that
people who are obese,

02:51:43.970 --> 02:51:46.850
who lose a lot
more weight early,

02:51:46.850 --> 02:51:50.220
are more likely to
keep it off, which

02:51:50.220 --> 02:51:52.110
seems a little bit
contradictory, right?

02:51:52.110 --> 02:51:54.450
Like, well, that doesn't
seem very sustainable.

02:51:54.450 --> 02:51:56.728
But again, you're
weighing competing things.

02:51:56.728 --> 02:51:58.270
So there's the
sustainability aspect.

02:51:58.270 --> 02:52:02.430
But then there's also-- buy-in
is huge for sustainability.

02:52:02.430 --> 02:52:05.070
So for a lot of overweight
or obese people,

02:52:05.070 --> 02:52:08.880
if they start a diet and they
don't see something quickly,

02:52:08.880 --> 02:52:11.550
they bail on it because
it's not working.

02:52:11.550 --> 02:52:14.250
Whereas if they see some
rapid results pretty quickly,

02:52:14.250 --> 02:52:16.470
they buy in even harder.

02:52:16.470 --> 02:52:19.530
And so I think that the
conversation, especially

02:52:19.530 --> 02:52:22.470
for if there's any coaches
or trainers out there,

02:52:22.470 --> 02:52:26.015
is just presenting that as the--

02:52:26.015 --> 02:52:28.140
one of my favorite lines
is there are no solutions.

02:52:28.140 --> 02:52:29.730
There's only trade-offs.

02:52:29.730 --> 02:52:32.320
I think Thomas Sowell said that.

02:52:32.320 --> 02:52:35.335
So you're having
a trade-off here.

02:52:38.680 --> 02:52:41.140
Yes, you're going
to lose fat faster.

02:52:41.140 --> 02:52:42.850
You might lose lean
mass a little bit

02:52:42.850 --> 02:52:44.900
faster too, which
can be a problem.

02:52:44.900 --> 02:52:49.280
But I will say, the more
adipose tissue you have,

02:52:49.280 --> 02:52:51.070
the more aggressively
you can diet

02:52:51.070 --> 02:52:53.110
without negative consequences.

02:52:53.110 --> 02:52:55.540
Somebody like me doing
a really aggressive diet

02:52:55.540 --> 02:52:57.610
is not going to be
good for my lean mass.

02:52:57.610 --> 02:53:00.220
One, I have a higher
lean mass than normal.

02:53:00.220 --> 02:53:02.770
Two, I have a lower
body fat than normal.

02:53:02.770 --> 02:53:06.550
As your body fat goes
down, the percentage

02:53:06.550 --> 02:53:09.190
of weight loss from
lean mass goes up.

02:53:09.190 --> 02:53:13.420
So people who are very
obese, because they

02:53:13.420 --> 02:53:15.580
have so much adipose
tissue to pull from,

02:53:15.580 --> 02:53:17.350
there's very little
reason for the body

02:53:17.350 --> 02:53:19.430
to metabolize lean tissue.

02:53:19.430 --> 02:53:24.580
Now, that being said, if you
go on a-- people misinterpret,

02:53:24.580 --> 02:53:28.990
like, well, I got an
in-body done or a DEXA done

02:53:28.990 --> 02:53:31.750
and I've lost 2
pounds of lean mass,

02:53:31.750 --> 02:53:34.000
and they've lost
20 pounds overall.

02:53:34.000 --> 02:53:38.680
Well, keep in mind, adipose
tissue itself is 13% lean mass.

02:53:38.680 --> 02:53:41.410
So there's actually
a protein component

02:53:41.410 --> 02:53:43.630
to-- the structural component
of the adipose tissue.

02:53:43.630 --> 02:53:45.100
And it does have some water.

02:53:45.100 --> 02:53:46.810
So it's about 87% lipid.

02:53:46.810 --> 02:53:49.150
But the other part is lean.

02:53:49.150 --> 02:53:53.410
So at minimum, you should expect
a 13% reduction in lean mass

02:53:53.410 --> 02:53:54.460
when you diet.

02:53:54.460 --> 02:53:57.550
And then when you consider like,
you lose body water overall,

02:53:57.550 --> 02:54:00.850
which registers as
lean mass, and you

02:54:00.850 --> 02:54:03.880
lose your splenic tissues
can shrink a little bit.

02:54:03.880 --> 02:54:09.370
So it's normal for the average
person to lose 25% or 30%

02:54:09.370 --> 02:54:12.340
of the weight that they
lose from "lean mass."

02:54:12.340 --> 02:54:15.650
But that doesn't mean
skeletal muscle tissue.

02:54:15.650 --> 02:54:18.410
And again, the more
adipose you have,

02:54:18.410 --> 02:54:21.070
the more aggressively
you can approach the diet

02:54:21.070 --> 02:54:24.460
without really negative
long-term consequences

02:54:24.460 --> 02:54:26.720
to lean mass or
your overall health.

02:54:26.720 --> 02:54:30.095
But balance that with, OK,
if I'm going to do this,

02:54:30.095 --> 02:54:31.720
I need to understand
that I'm not going

02:54:31.720 --> 02:54:33.640
to be dieting this way forever.

02:54:33.640 --> 02:54:36.100
I'm doing this to give myself
a boost at the beginning.

02:54:36.100 --> 02:54:37.607
And I have to be
OK at some point

02:54:37.607 --> 02:54:39.190
with transitioning
to something that's

02:54:39.190 --> 02:54:41.355
a little bit more sustainable.

02:54:41.355 --> 02:54:43.230
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Based
on what you just said,

02:54:43.230 --> 02:54:47.550
it reminds me of the satiety
signal effect of exercise

02:54:47.550 --> 02:54:49.170
you mentioned earlier,
that exercising

02:54:49.170 --> 02:54:54.130
can improve our sense of
when we've had enough to eat.

02:54:54.130 --> 02:54:57.060
I just want to briefly mention
that when Alia Crum was

02:54:57.060 --> 02:55:00.360
on the podcast, she
mentioned that they'd

02:55:00.360 --> 02:55:01.620
been doing a study that--

02:55:01.620 --> 02:55:04.620
I have to pair you two
and hear the conversation

02:55:04.620 --> 02:55:05.550
as a fly on the wall.

02:55:05.550 --> 02:55:07.170
Because what she
was telling me was

02:55:07.170 --> 02:55:11.910
that if people believe that a
food is nutritious for them,

02:55:11.910 --> 02:55:16.350
then eating less of it
registers as more satiating.

02:55:16.350 --> 02:55:20.580
Whereas if people view dieting
as a deprivation system,

02:55:20.580 --> 02:55:24.330
like, oh, dieting is
hard, and the food sucks,

02:55:24.330 --> 02:55:26.727
and it's terrible,
well, then they crave

02:55:26.727 --> 02:55:27.810
all sorts of other things.

02:55:27.810 --> 02:55:30.540
Whereas they actually
observe in their studies

02:55:30.540 --> 02:55:33.030
where people report reduced
craving if they are told,

02:55:33.030 --> 02:55:35.850
for instance, a chicken breast
and broccoli and some olive oil

02:55:35.850 --> 02:55:37.830
and rice is actually
quite nourishing.

02:55:37.830 --> 02:55:39.385
It's actually
really good for you.

02:55:39.385 --> 02:55:40.260
Then people eat that.

02:55:40.260 --> 02:55:42.840
And they feel like they've
actually eaten more.

02:55:42.840 --> 02:55:44.650
The satiety signaling goes up.

02:55:44.650 --> 02:55:46.615
So it's just a point
that Alia made.

02:55:46.615 --> 02:55:47.490
Those aren't my data.

02:55:47.490 --> 02:55:50.460
LAYNE NORTON: Satiety is
so impressive because even

02:55:50.460 --> 02:55:52.620
the rate at which
you eat and right

02:55:52.620 --> 02:55:57.930
down to the size of the plate
and the color of the plate,

02:55:57.930 --> 02:55:59.235
the contrast in color--

02:55:59.235 --> 02:56:00.655
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Really?

02:56:00.655 --> 02:56:02.280
LAYNE NORTON: I can't
remember exactly.

02:56:02.280 --> 02:56:08.310
I think it's if the plate is
a similar color to the food,

02:56:08.310 --> 02:56:10.170
I think people eat more.

02:56:10.170 --> 02:56:13.450
Whereas if it's a bigger
contrast, they eat less.

02:56:13.450 --> 02:56:16.840
So even plate color can make a
difference on how much you eat.

02:56:16.840 --> 02:56:19.260
So again, human brain,
very amazing, but also

02:56:19.260 --> 02:56:20.577
very dumb in some ways, right?

02:56:20.577 --> 02:56:22.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Not
an optimized algorithm.

02:56:22.410 --> 02:56:24.270
LAYNE NORTON: I always
joke with people.

02:56:24.270 --> 02:56:26.250
I'm like, just look at
how stupid humans are.

02:56:26.250 --> 02:56:28.837
You put some water in front
of them, like the ocean,

02:56:28.837 --> 02:56:31.170
they're like, oh, yeah, I'll
pay 10 times more for this.

02:56:31.170 --> 02:56:35.100
[CHUCKLES] But we're
kind of wired that way.

02:56:35.100 --> 02:56:39.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The reward
signaling pathways in the brain

02:56:39.480 --> 02:56:44.110
run one chemical mainly,
dopamine-- there are others,

02:56:44.110 --> 02:56:44.940
of course--

02:56:44.940 --> 02:56:46.380
and very few algorithms.

02:56:46.380 --> 02:56:47.940
It's sort of like an--

02:56:47.940 --> 02:56:51.480
intermittent reinforcement
is one, random reinforcement.

02:56:51.480 --> 02:56:53.440
But in the end, there
aren't many algorithms.

02:56:53.440 --> 02:56:54.990
And we are probably not
optimized-- certainly

02:56:54.990 --> 02:56:56.948
not optimized for our
own health because people

02:56:56.948 --> 02:56:59.580
will eat themselves to death,
drug themselves to death,

02:56:59.580 --> 02:57:01.710
et cetera, simply because
something felt good

02:57:01.710 --> 02:57:02.850
at one point.

02:57:02.850 --> 02:57:03.725
It proves your point.

02:57:03.725 --> 02:57:04.558
LAYNE NORTON: Right.

02:57:04.558 --> 02:57:06.060
One of the things
I tell people--

02:57:06.060 --> 02:57:08.010
I said this on--

02:57:08.010 --> 02:57:12.060
for someone's podcast
was, interestingly,

02:57:12.060 --> 02:57:14.460
the dichotomy of
life is if you do

02:57:14.460 --> 02:57:17.700
what's easy in the short
term, your life will be hard.

02:57:17.700 --> 02:57:19.650
If you do what's hard
in the short term,

02:57:19.650 --> 02:57:21.000
your life will get easier.

02:57:21.000 --> 02:57:22.050
It's very strange.

02:57:22.050 --> 02:57:25.110
And actually, Ethan Suplee
had a great example of this.

02:57:25.110 --> 02:57:27.240
When he was over
500 pounds, he said,

02:57:27.240 --> 02:57:31.710
the amount of work I had to
do to construct my life that I

02:57:31.710 --> 02:57:35.640
could just live was
so much more work

02:57:35.640 --> 02:57:39.690
than just going to the gym
for a couple hours a day.

02:57:39.690 --> 02:57:41.730
He's like, the gym work is hard.

02:57:41.730 --> 02:57:44.340
He's like, but when I
look back at how much work

02:57:44.340 --> 02:57:47.610
I had to do to sustain that
lifestyle versus just going

02:57:47.610 --> 02:57:51.360
to the gym and
restricting calories,

02:57:51.360 --> 02:57:54.750
he's like, to maintain the
lifestyle of being 500 pounds

02:57:54.750 --> 02:57:58.480
was infinitely more
difficult than what I do now.

02:57:58.480 --> 02:58:00.760
And so again, great example.

02:58:00.760 --> 02:58:02.130
Short term-- hard.

02:58:02.130 --> 02:58:04.410
Going to the gym,
calorie restriction.

02:58:04.410 --> 02:58:07.080
Long term, life's easier.

02:58:07.080 --> 02:58:09.510
Just a really interesting
dichotomy I think about a lot.

02:58:09.510 --> 02:58:13.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And it can't
be restated often enough.

02:58:13.600 --> 02:58:15.710
Seed oils.

02:58:15.710 --> 02:58:17.460
People want to ask
about seed oils.

02:58:17.460 --> 02:58:17.960
And for--

02:58:17.960 --> 02:58:18.190
LAYNE NORTON: Cut.

02:58:18.190 --> 02:58:18.820
[CHUCKLES]

02:58:18.820 --> 02:58:20.362
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And for those of you

02:58:20.362 --> 02:58:22.850
that are listening who are
wondering why we're chuckling

02:58:22.850 --> 02:58:26.640
already, I should mentioned
that both in the Twitter sphere

02:58:26.640 --> 02:58:30.750
and Instagram and online, there
are these very polarized views

02:58:30.750 --> 02:58:33.090
that probably aren't worth
focusing on for too long.

02:58:33.090 --> 02:58:35.280
But there are a number
of folks out there

02:58:35.280 --> 02:58:39.210
who are arguing that seed
oils are the source of all--

02:58:39.210 --> 02:58:40.170
the obesity epidemic--

02:58:40.170 --> 02:58:41.100
LAYNE NORTON: Everything.

02:58:41.100 --> 02:58:42.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--inflammation, et cetera--

02:58:42.720 --> 02:58:43.200
LAYNE NORTON: Illuminati.

02:58:43.200 --> 02:58:44.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --everything.

02:58:44.680 --> 02:58:48.090
And then there are those that
would argue just the opposite,

02:58:48.090 --> 02:58:50.670
that meat is the source of
all problems, et cetera.

02:58:50.670 --> 02:58:54.240
And I think we've, thanks to
your nuance and expertise,

02:58:54.240 --> 02:58:57.180
we've hopefully
appropriately framed things

02:58:57.180 --> 02:58:58.920
that it's never that
black and white.

02:58:58.920 --> 02:59:00.265
It's simply not.

02:59:00.265 --> 02:59:01.140
LAYNE NORTON: Rarely.

02:59:01.140 --> 02:59:03.240
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Rarely.

02:59:03.240 --> 02:59:04.500
I love olive oil.

02:59:04.500 --> 02:59:06.090
I realize that
doesn't fit exactly

02:59:06.090 --> 02:59:08.080
into the seed oil category.

02:59:08.080 --> 02:59:09.300
I love olive oil.

02:59:09.300 --> 02:59:10.920
I use it in moderation.

02:59:10.920 --> 02:59:14.640
I do also consume some butter
in moderation, et cetera.

02:59:14.640 --> 02:59:17.497
But are there any
data on seed oils?

02:59:17.497 --> 02:59:19.080
And here, a good
example I think would

02:59:19.080 --> 02:59:22.500
be canola oil, which comes from
the rape seed, that literally

02:59:22.500 --> 02:59:26.460
was renamed canola oil
because rape seed oil is not

02:59:26.460 --> 02:59:27.220
good marketing.

02:59:27.220 --> 02:59:28.095
LAYNE NORTON: No, no.

02:59:28.095 --> 02:59:30.450
[CHUCKLES] No, exactly.

02:59:30.450 --> 02:59:35.700
So the first thing
I'll say is seed oils

02:59:35.700 --> 02:59:38.310
have negatively contributed
to our overall health

02:59:38.310 --> 02:59:45.180
because people in the last 20,
30 years, what they have tended

02:59:45.180 --> 02:59:47.970
to add into their diet that does
increase the overall calorie

02:59:47.970 --> 02:59:53.990
load is oil, mostly
from seed oils.

02:59:53.990 --> 02:59:59.540
But when we look at 1-to-1
replacement with other fats--

02:59:59.540 --> 03:00:02.570
if you look at the
epidemiology, yeah, you

03:00:02.570 --> 03:00:04.250
can find some
epidemiology showing

03:00:04.250 --> 03:00:06.890
people who consume more seed
oil have more negative health

03:00:06.890 --> 03:00:07.910
outcomes.

03:00:07.910 --> 03:00:12.170
Problem is, again, tied up with
a multitude of other behaviors.

03:00:12.170 --> 03:00:13.880
And then you can
find mechanisms.

03:00:13.880 --> 03:00:18.620
And the idea is, well,
they're polyunsaturated,

03:00:18.620 --> 03:00:21.230
which means in the
fatty acid chain,

03:00:21.230 --> 03:00:24.470
there's multiple double bonds,
which those double bonds can

03:00:24.470 --> 03:00:29.430
be oxidized when they're exposed
to heat and some other things.

03:00:29.430 --> 03:00:32.150
And so the idea is, well, when
you cook with these things,

03:00:32.150 --> 03:00:35.810
they make it oxidized.

03:00:35.810 --> 03:00:39.240
And that's going to cause
inflammation in your body.

03:00:39.240 --> 03:00:42.810
So that's a plausible mechanism.

03:00:42.810 --> 03:00:47.360
So as always, I defer to the
human randomized controlled

03:00:47.360 --> 03:00:48.690
trials.

03:00:48.690 --> 03:00:57.110
And so what you tend to
find is when you substitute

03:00:57.110 --> 03:01:00.420
saturated fats for
polyunsaturated fats,

03:01:00.420 --> 03:01:05.100
it's either neutral or positive
in terms of the effects on--

03:01:05.100 --> 03:01:07.140
inflammation is
basically neutral.

03:01:07.140 --> 03:01:09.990
There's some studies that
show a positive effect

03:01:09.990 --> 03:01:12.300
of doing polyunsaturated fats.

03:01:12.300 --> 03:01:15.600
But it probably depends on the
individual polyunsaturated fat.

03:01:15.600 --> 03:01:17.730
And that's the
other thing that's

03:01:17.730 --> 03:01:19.590
difficult because
you're categorizing

03:01:19.590 --> 03:01:20.940
everything in this one bucket.

03:01:20.940 --> 03:01:25.200
And there are some differences
between individual fatty acids.

03:01:25.200 --> 03:01:26.970
Even with saturated fat--

03:01:26.970 --> 03:01:29.190
for example,
stearic acid doesn't

03:01:29.190 --> 03:01:31.350
tend to raise LDL cholesterol.

03:01:31.350 --> 03:01:34.950
Whereas saturated fat as a whole
tends to raise LDL cholesterol.

03:01:34.950 --> 03:01:38.370
But there are some
saturated fats that don't.

03:01:38.370 --> 03:01:40.740
So again, we're putting
things in buckets.

03:01:40.740 --> 03:01:42.990
And it's a little more
nuanced than that.

03:01:42.990 --> 03:01:49.500
Then if you look at the
effects of polyunsaturated fats

03:01:49.500 --> 03:01:52.560
on markers of
cardiovascular disease,

03:01:52.560 --> 03:01:55.740
again, tends to either be a
neutral or positive effect

03:01:55.740 --> 03:02:00.240
when you substitute saturated
fat for polyunsaturated fat.

03:02:00.240 --> 03:02:02.220
Now, if you want to get
into monounsaturated

03:02:02.220 --> 03:02:06.870
versus polyunsaturated, there's
quite a bit of disagreement

03:02:06.870 --> 03:02:07.900
between the studies.

03:02:07.900 --> 03:02:10.800
What I would say, based on the
human randomized controlled

03:02:10.800 --> 03:02:14.400
trials, is that
you're probably better

03:02:14.400 --> 03:02:17.670
off consuming monounsaturated
and polyunsaturated

03:02:17.670 --> 03:02:19.560
in place of saturated fat.

03:02:19.560 --> 03:02:23.635
But again, if the idea is,
well, that means polyunsaturated

03:02:23.635 --> 03:02:26.010
are good for me, so I'm just
going to dump a bunch of oil

03:02:26.010 --> 03:02:28.093
on everything and now
you're upping your calories,

03:02:28.093 --> 03:02:30.090
well, that's a negative
now because you

03:02:30.090 --> 03:02:32.340
have to deal with the bigger
problem of overall energy

03:02:32.340 --> 03:02:33.630
toxicity.

03:02:33.630 --> 03:02:38.100
So I'm not somebody who likes to
demonize individual nutrients.

03:02:38.100 --> 03:02:40.500
I just haven't seen
really compelling evidence

03:02:40.500 --> 03:02:44.340
that seed oils are the root
cause of the problems that

03:02:44.340 --> 03:02:45.570
are being suggested.

03:02:45.570 --> 03:02:49.980
And I think this is a good
example of whenever there's

03:02:49.980 --> 03:02:52.230
something that pops up
in the fitness industry,

03:02:52.230 --> 03:02:53.790
there's always
the opposite thing

03:02:53.790 --> 03:02:58.380
that pops up and is the
reactionary, extreme reaction

03:02:58.380 --> 03:03:00.318
to whatever this
thing was over here.

03:03:00.318 --> 03:03:02.735
And I think that's what we're
seeing with some of the seed

03:03:02.735 --> 03:03:07.440
oil stuff, is it's mostly
people who are trying to espouse

03:03:07.440 --> 03:03:09.930
the virtues of saturated fat.

03:03:09.930 --> 03:03:14.160
And listen, I think it's fine
to consume some saturated fat.

03:03:14.160 --> 03:03:19.800
But again, I think
limiting it to 7% to 10%

03:03:19.800 --> 03:03:23.340
of your daily calorie intake
is probably wise, again,

03:03:23.340 --> 03:03:30.350
based on all the consensus
of the evidence I've seen.

03:03:30.350 --> 03:03:32.650
And so once again, we're
struggling with this.

03:03:32.650 --> 03:03:35.320
OK, we've got this epidemiology
and these mechanisms

03:03:35.320 --> 03:03:37.580
that sound good.

03:03:37.580 --> 03:03:39.080
But then, what
actually happens when

03:03:39.080 --> 03:03:41.550
we do some human randomized
controlled trials?

03:03:41.550 --> 03:03:44.390
And so far, I just
haven't seen the evidence

03:03:44.390 --> 03:03:49.430
to suggest that seed oils
are independently bad

03:03:49.430 --> 03:03:52.720
for you, independent of
the calories they contain.

03:03:52.720 --> 03:03:55.400
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You said
the words, "overall energy

03:03:55.400 --> 03:03:56.250
toxicity."

03:03:56.250 --> 03:03:57.710
And I just want to
highlight that I

03:03:57.710 --> 03:04:00.680
think that's a fabulous term.

03:04:00.680 --> 03:04:02.600
I don't think enough
people think about

03:04:02.600 --> 03:04:06.380
that because they are primed,
or we are all primed, to think,

03:04:06.380 --> 03:04:08.660
OK, seed oils might be bad.

03:04:08.660 --> 03:04:10.310
Or artificial
sweeteners might be bad.

03:04:10.310 --> 03:04:13.070
Or this particular
component of blood work

03:04:13.070 --> 03:04:15.110
might represent
something good or bad,

03:04:15.110 --> 03:04:18.110
without taking into
account overall energy

03:04:18.110 --> 03:04:23.540
toxicity, the toxicity of
overconsuming calories, energy.

03:04:23.540 --> 03:04:28.730
And thank you for pointing out
that most of the data point

03:04:28.730 --> 03:04:31.880
to the fact that saturated
fat should make up

03:04:31.880 --> 03:04:36.140
about no more than 7% to 10%
of total daily caloric intake.

03:04:36.140 --> 03:04:41.210
Is there a lower-end threshold
that can be problematic?

03:04:41.210 --> 03:04:44.030
For instance, I've noticed that
my blood profiles, especially

03:04:44.030 --> 03:04:47.330
in terms of hormones,
improve when I'm getting

03:04:47.330 --> 03:04:49.130
sufficient saturated fat.

03:04:49.130 --> 03:04:50.300
Maybe I'm a mutant.

03:04:50.300 --> 03:04:52.580
But years ago,
because I'm a product

03:04:52.580 --> 03:04:55.070
of growing up in the '90s,
I tried a low-fat diet.

03:04:55.070 --> 03:04:57.548
It certainly crushed
my androgen levels.

03:04:57.548 --> 03:04:59.090
I started adding
some butter back in.

03:04:59.090 --> 03:05:02.300
And I was right back
in the sweet zone,

03:05:02.300 --> 03:05:03.470
where I wanted to be.

03:05:03.470 --> 03:05:07.970
So 7% to 10% of total daily
caloric intake, I'm guessing,

03:05:07.970 --> 03:05:09.800
is probably about what I do now.

03:05:09.800 --> 03:05:10.850
I'll have to check.

03:05:10.850 --> 03:05:14.450
But is there a danger to going
too low in saturated fats?

03:05:14.450 --> 03:05:16.460
LAYNE NORTON: So
again, no solutions,

03:05:16.460 --> 03:05:17.720
only trade-offs, right?

03:05:17.720 --> 03:05:20.690
What maximizes out
testosterone might not

03:05:20.690 --> 03:05:24.225
be the best thing for
longevity, and vice versa.

03:05:24.225 --> 03:05:25.850
I'm not making that
claim specifically.

03:05:25.850 --> 03:05:27.260
But I think it's important
to understand this

03:05:27.260 --> 03:05:28.677
that I think we
all have this idea

03:05:28.677 --> 03:05:32.480
that there's this one
iconic diet out there that

03:05:32.480 --> 03:05:35.300
is going to be the best
diet for building muscle

03:05:35.300 --> 03:05:38.990
and burning fat and preventing
cancer and heart disease.

03:05:38.990 --> 03:05:42.050
And the reality is, there's
overall healthy dietary

03:05:42.050 --> 03:05:45.390
patterns that we see that
are good for those things.

03:05:45.390 --> 03:05:47.210
But when we get
down into the weeds,

03:05:47.210 --> 03:05:50.180
there's probably some push
and pull here, as well.

03:05:50.180 --> 03:05:56.280
So when it comes
to saturated fat,

03:05:56.280 --> 03:05:59.610
there is some evidence that
if you're too low on it,

03:05:59.610 --> 03:06:01.890
that yes, you can have a
reduction in testosterone.

03:06:01.890 --> 03:06:05.490
Now, is that reduction in
testosterone, let's say 15%,

03:06:05.490 --> 03:06:08.850
20%, whatever it may be, is that
sufficient to actually cause

03:06:08.850 --> 03:06:10.207
loss of lean mass?

03:06:10.207 --> 03:06:11.040
That, we don't know.

03:06:11.040 --> 03:06:13.080
That's never been shown.

03:06:13.080 --> 03:06:15.420
Interestingly, I
just remembered this.

03:06:15.420 --> 03:06:21.880
There was one study that was
comparing polyunsaturated fat

03:06:21.880 --> 03:06:25.090
versus saturated fat.

03:06:25.090 --> 03:06:27.733
And they equated total fat.

03:06:27.733 --> 03:06:29.400
And one of the really
interesting things

03:06:29.400 --> 03:06:33.150
was the group getting
the polyunsaturated fat

03:06:33.150 --> 03:06:35.340
had more lean mass at
the end of the study

03:06:35.340 --> 03:06:37.480
compared to the group
getting saturated fat.

03:06:37.480 --> 03:06:38.610
Now, it's only one study.

03:06:38.610 --> 03:06:39.943
I've never seen this replicated.

03:06:39.943 --> 03:06:44.505
So this is a
situation where I say,

03:06:44.505 --> 03:06:46.630
I would like to find out
what the mechanism of that

03:06:46.630 --> 03:06:50.330
is because this
could just be random.

03:06:50.330 --> 03:06:54.340
But if that gets shown over
and over, what I might say

03:06:54.340 --> 03:07:00.610
is, OK, well, if
polyunsaturateds are somehow

03:07:00.610 --> 03:07:03.038
increasing lean mass compared
to saturated fat, who cares

03:07:03.038 --> 03:07:04.330
what happens with testosterone?

03:07:04.330 --> 03:07:06.160
Unless that reduction
in testosterone

03:07:06.160 --> 03:07:10.990
is causing some kind of
impotence for your life, right?

03:07:10.990 --> 03:07:14.440
So all that to say,
I don't really know.

03:07:14.440 --> 03:07:17.830
And by the way, that's
something, for those watching

03:07:17.830 --> 03:07:20.160
and listening, real experts,
every once in a while,

03:07:20.160 --> 03:07:22.660
you should hear them say the
following words "I don't know."

03:07:22.660 --> 03:07:22.930
[CHUCKLES]

03:07:22.930 --> 03:07:23.972
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Exactly.

03:07:23.972 --> 03:07:26.110
My graduate advisor was
exceptional at that.

03:07:26.110 --> 03:07:27.745
And she was brilliant, right?

03:07:27.745 --> 03:07:31.030
LAYNE NORTON: And then in
terms of cholesterol synthesis,

03:07:31.030 --> 03:07:34.570
you really need a very, very
small amount of saturated fat

03:07:34.570 --> 03:07:36.190
for LDL cholesterol synthesis.

03:07:36.190 --> 03:07:40.870
Your liver can synthesize--
the amount of LDL cholesterol,

03:07:40.870 --> 03:07:42.640
or cholesterol that
your body requires

03:07:42.640 --> 03:07:46.670
is so small in terms of just
living and being healthy.

03:07:46.670 --> 03:07:49.820
So I don't think you
need to worry about that.

03:07:49.820 --> 03:07:52.247
And from a cardiovascular
disease standpoint,

03:07:52.247 --> 03:07:54.580
there is some evidence that
even taking people who have,

03:07:54.580 --> 03:07:57.850
quote, unquote, "low
LDL" of 80 or 90

03:07:57.850 --> 03:07:59.770
and taking them down
to like 30 or 40,

03:07:59.770 --> 03:08:04.090
that there is still a
benefit for the risk

03:08:04.090 --> 03:08:05.780
of cardiovascular disease.

03:08:05.780 --> 03:08:08.980
So again, you're weighing
these two buckets.

03:08:08.980 --> 03:08:12.430
So what I say, if you're doing
7% and 10% from saturated fat,

03:08:12.430 --> 03:08:14.653
you're probably fine.

03:08:14.653 --> 03:08:16.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I received
a lot of questions about

03:08:16.820 --> 03:08:20.090
whether or not there
are female-specific diet

03:08:20.090 --> 03:08:21.470
and exercise protocols.

03:08:21.470 --> 03:08:23.810
And I realize this
is a vast landscape.

03:08:23.810 --> 03:08:26.180
But some of those
questions related

03:08:26.180 --> 03:08:28.730
to menopause and premenopause.

03:08:28.730 --> 03:08:30.440
And some related to
the menstrual cycle.

03:08:30.440 --> 03:08:33.620
Most related to variations
across the menstrual cycle.

03:08:33.620 --> 03:08:42.110
In terms of, let's just say diet
maintenance or subcaloric diet,

03:08:42.110 --> 03:08:44.900
are there any things
that you've observed?

03:08:44.900 --> 03:08:48.200
We'll talk a little bit later
about this wonderful app

03:08:48.200 --> 03:08:50.300
that you've produced,
this Carbon app which

03:08:50.300 --> 03:08:52.940
helps people manage
their energy intake

03:08:52.940 --> 03:08:54.750
and a number of other things.

03:08:54.750 --> 03:08:56.660
And so there, you have
a sort of a database,

03:08:56.660 --> 03:08:58.370
or at least an experience base.

03:08:58.370 --> 03:09:00.650
And then I'm guessing there
are probably also studies

03:09:00.650 --> 03:09:03.230
exploring male-versus-female
differences in terms

03:09:03.230 --> 03:09:06.080
of adherence and what
sorts of diets work.

03:09:06.080 --> 03:09:09.110
Are there any general themes
that one can extract from that?

03:09:09.110 --> 03:09:11.610
LAYNE NORTON: This is going to
be a really unpopular segment

03:09:11.610 --> 03:09:12.197
for the women.

03:09:12.197 --> 03:09:13.780
Doesn't seem to make
a big difference.

03:09:13.780 --> 03:09:14.660
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, actually, they

03:09:14.660 --> 03:09:16.790
may be relieved to hear
that because it makes

03:09:16.790 --> 03:09:18.692
sorting through the
information space

03:09:18.692 --> 03:09:20.150
and certainly the
information we've

03:09:20.150 --> 03:09:22.130
covered in this podcast
up until now simpler.

03:09:22.130 --> 03:09:24.230
It means that everything
isn't different for them.

03:09:24.230 --> 03:09:27.150
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah,
so if you look

03:09:27.150 --> 03:09:30.600
at the male-versus-female
studies' relation to diet,

03:09:30.600 --> 03:09:32.700
they seem to respond
in a similar way.

03:09:32.700 --> 03:09:35.370
Like, similar
calorie deficit seems

03:09:35.370 --> 03:09:37.620
to produce similar results.

03:09:37.620 --> 03:09:41.580
If you do low carb, high
carb, regardless, it

03:09:41.580 --> 03:09:44.190
seems to boil down to
the same principles.

03:09:44.190 --> 03:09:47.108
Now, training wise, we
do know that females,

03:09:47.108 --> 03:09:49.650
the muscle fibers adapt a little
bit differently to training.

03:09:49.650 --> 03:09:53.010
But without getting
too far into the weeds,

03:09:53.010 --> 03:09:54.510
it doesn't really
change the way you

03:09:54.510 --> 03:09:58.410
should train because for the
most part, building muscle,

03:09:58.410 --> 03:10:01.870
there's a lot of different
ways to build muscle.

03:10:01.870 --> 03:10:07.390
So we know that light
loads up to maybe 30 reps,

03:10:07.390 --> 03:10:11.122
as long as it's taken close
to failure, have basically

03:10:11.122 --> 03:10:12.580
the same effect on
building muscle,

03:10:12.580 --> 03:10:17.230
at least in the short term,
as heavy loads for low reps.

03:10:17.230 --> 03:10:19.450
It's mostly about
taking the muscle close

03:10:19.450 --> 03:10:21.850
to fatigue or failure.

03:10:21.850 --> 03:10:24.430
You don't have to go to
failure, but getting close,

03:10:24.430 --> 03:10:26.330
within a few reps.

03:10:26.330 --> 03:10:29.180
If you're between
one rep and 30 reps,

03:10:29.180 --> 03:10:30.940
if you're getting
close to failure,

03:10:30.940 --> 03:10:33.440
seem to produce similar results.

03:10:33.440 --> 03:10:34.390
So again, great.

03:10:34.390 --> 03:10:38.665
You can pick whichever form of
discomfort you prefer, right?

03:10:41.210 --> 03:10:44.510
When it comes to
female-specific training,

03:10:44.510 --> 03:10:46.330
again, females
actually-- this is

03:10:46.330 --> 03:10:48.080
one thing that a lot
of people don't know.

03:10:48.080 --> 03:10:52.640
They actually put on a
similar amount of lean mass

03:10:52.640 --> 03:10:55.850
as a percentage of their
starting lean mass as men.

03:10:55.850 --> 03:10:57.830
In fact, there's no
statistically significant

03:10:57.830 --> 03:10:59.960
difference in the amount
of lean mass they put on.

03:10:59.960 --> 03:11:02.212
Now, the absolute amount
of lean mass that's added

03:11:02.212 --> 03:11:04.670
will be greater for men because
they started with a greater

03:11:04.670 --> 03:11:05.870
amount of lean mass.

03:11:05.870 --> 03:11:07.970
But the relative
increase in lean mass

03:11:07.970 --> 03:11:11.580
is pretty much the same
from similar training.

03:11:11.580 --> 03:11:16.260
Now, females, there's some
differences in fiber types,

03:11:16.260 --> 03:11:19.440
that females tend to be a little
bit less fatiguable than men.

03:11:19.440 --> 03:11:22.050
They can go a little bit
harder a little bit longer.

03:11:22.050 --> 03:11:23.940
And there's also some
evidence that they

03:11:23.940 --> 03:11:25.470
recover a little bit better.

03:11:25.470 --> 03:11:30.150
But that also could be
simply due to the fact

03:11:30.150 --> 03:11:33.870
that they're not able
to use as heavy of loads

03:11:33.870 --> 03:11:36.660
to induce hypertrophy.

03:11:36.660 --> 03:11:40.750
So I kind of have this theory
that while as a percentage

03:11:40.750 --> 03:11:43.140
of your one rep max,
you can program things,

03:11:43.140 --> 03:11:44.910
I think absolute load matters.

03:11:44.910 --> 03:11:47.840
When you look at the
most elite power lifters,

03:11:47.840 --> 03:11:50.340
the super heavy weights aren't
squatting three or four times

03:11:50.340 --> 03:11:55.417
a week because they're
squatting 800, 900 pounds.

03:11:55.417 --> 03:11:57.000
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
They need to recover.

03:11:57.000 --> 03:11:59.070
LAYNE NORTON: I think that
there's an overall recovery

03:11:59.070 --> 03:11:59.617
effect there.

03:11:59.617 --> 03:12:01.200
Again, I have no
data to back this up.

03:12:01.200 --> 03:12:03.197
This is just my observation.

03:12:03.197 --> 03:12:05.280
But when you get into the
lighter weight classes--

03:12:05.280 --> 03:12:07.050
and this goes for
men, too-- you do

03:12:07.050 --> 03:12:10.740
see quite a few people
who do many training

03:12:10.740 --> 03:12:13.710
sessions at high
RPEs and seem to be

03:12:13.710 --> 03:12:15.070
able to recover from that.

03:12:15.070 --> 03:12:17.580
So I do think the absolute
load makes a difference.

03:12:17.580 --> 03:12:21.660
Now, when it comes
to menstrual cycle,

03:12:21.660 --> 03:12:24.750
this is one of those things
where I kind of tell people,

03:12:24.750 --> 03:12:26.710
do what you prefer.

03:12:26.710 --> 03:12:28.740
So there are some
people who have said,

03:12:28.740 --> 03:12:30.630
you should schedule
your training

03:12:30.630 --> 03:12:32.160
around your menstrual
cycle, which

03:12:32.160 --> 03:12:36.330
is whenever you're going
through your menstrual cycle,

03:12:36.330 --> 03:12:37.650
reduce the intensity.

03:12:37.650 --> 03:12:41.040
Reduce the volume because you're
not going to feel as good.

03:12:41.040 --> 03:12:42.610
You're not going
to train as well.

03:12:42.610 --> 03:12:44.760
What I would say is
just autoregulate that.

03:12:44.760 --> 03:12:47.880
If you go in and you're on
your period but you feel good

03:12:47.880 --> 03:12:49.720
and you're doing
well that day, then I

03:12:49.720 --> 03:12:51.720
don't think you necessarily
need to back it off.

03:12:51.720 --> 03:12:55.290
And there was one study that
kind of supported that notion.

03:12:55.290 --> 03:12:57.330
But if you go in and
you feel terrible

03:12:57.330 --> 03:13:01.470
and you feel like you could
use a reduction in intensity

03:13:01.470 --> 03:13:04.390
and volume, then it's totally
fine to autoregulate that.

03:13:04.390 --> 03:13:06.960
And when I say
autoregulation, autoregulation

03:13:06.960 --> 03:13:10.950
means you are regulating the
individual training session

03:13:10.950 --> 03:13:13.090
based on your performance.

03:13:13.090 --> 03:13:18.870
So I autoregulate insofar
as, I'm a super nerd.

03:13:18.870 --> 03:13:20.970
So I have a velocity device.

03:13:20.970 --> 03:13:23.190
So I can actually
attach it to the bar

03:13:23.190 --> 03:13:24.930
and see how fast the load moves.

03:13:24.930 --> 03:13:28.140
And I know at various
different warm-up weights what

03:13:28.140 --> 03:13:29.670
velocities I should be hitting.

03:13:29.670 --> 03:13:32.610
So if I hit my last warm-up
and my velocity is about 10%

03:13:32.610 --> 03:13:35.300
higher than usual, I
can be pretty confident

03:13:35.300 --> 03:13:37.050
that that's going to
be a good day for me.

03:13:37.050 --> 03:13:39.850
If it's lower, than I can
back it off a little bit.

03:13:39.850 --> 03:13:46.920
In fact, at Worlds, when I
hit my last deadlift warm-up,

03:13:46.920 --> 03:13:48.972
it was 30% faster than I
usually hit in the gym.

03:13:48.972 --> 03:13:50.430
And I turned and
looked at my coach

03:13:50.430 --> 03:13:52.430
and I said, yeah, we're
going to get this today.

03:13:52.430 --> 03:13:53.640
[CHUCKLES]

03:13:53.640 --> 03:13:55.770
So there's various forms
of ways to autoregulate.

03:13:55.770 --> 03:13:59.410
But again, women, if you're on
your period but you feel good,

03:13:59.410 --> 03:14:01.660
I don't think there's any
reason you need to back off.

03:14:01.660 --> 03:14:04.202
But if you're not feeling good,
then it's totally appropriate

03:14:04.202 --> 03:14:05.295
to back off.

03:14:05.295 --> 03:14:07.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Raw
versus cooked foods.

03:14:07.410 --> 03:14:10.020
People wanted to
know whether or not,

03:14:10.020 --> 03:14:13.318
for instance, eating
a raw apple versus--

03:14:13.318 --> 03:14:13.860
I don't know.

03:14:13.860 --> 03:14:15.600
Does anyone cook apples?

03:14:15.600 --> 03:14:16.527
People used to bake--

03:14:16.527 --> 03:14:18.360
baked apples was a
dessert when I was a kid.

03:14:18.360 --> 03:14:19.830
It was kind of the
letdown dessert.

03:14:19.830 --> 03:14:20.550
Sorry, Mom.

03:14:20.550 --> 03:14:23.250
That was not awesome unless it
had a scoop of ice cream in it.

03:14:23.250 --> 03:14:23.580
LAYNE NORTON: Or apple pie.

03:14:23.580 --> 03:14:25.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And even
then, maybe not awesome.

03:14:25.740 --> 03:14:28.740
But anyway, raw versus cooked.

03:14:28.740 --> 03:14:31.320
Obviously, if you
burn a piece of meat

03:14:31.320 --> 03:14:33.780
to the point where it's pure
charcoal, that's too much.

03:14:33.780 --> 03:14:39.030
And there is a small movement
surrounding eating raw meats.

03:14:39.030 --> 03:14:41.100
That's not something
I particularly enjoy.

03:14:41.100 --> 03:14:44.208
Frankly, sushi is the only
raw food I personally ingest.

03:14:44.208 --> 03:14:45.000
LAYNE NORTON: Same.

03:14:45.000 --> 03:14:47.333
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And I am
very careful about the source,

03:14:47.333 --> 03:14:48.540
frankly--

03:14:48.540 --> 03:14:51.150
reputable places.

03:14:51.150 --> 03:14:53.130
Is there anything
real about this

03:14:53.130 --> 03:14:55.050
in terms of being
able to extract

03:14:55.050 --> 03:14:57.810
the amino acids, vitamins,
and minerals from the food

03:14:57.810 --> 03:14:58.710
raw versus cooked?

03:14:58.710 --> 03:15:01.380
LAYNE NORTON: It just
looks cool for Instagram.

03:15:01.380 --> 03:15:05.280
So when you cook foods, they
actually tend to become--

03:15:05.280 --> 03:15:06.870
in terms of
protein-containing foods,

03:15:06.870 --> 03:15:09.810
they tend to become more
digestible, not less.

03:15:09.810 --> 03:15:10.950
Eggs are this way.

03:15:10.950 --> 03:15:12.630
Meats are this way.

03:15:12.630 --> 03:15:17.220
People say, well, when you
heat protein, you denature it.

03:15:17.220 --> 03:15:19.380
And I think they hear
that word "denature,"

03:15:19.380 --> 03:15:20.640
and they think destroy.

03:15:20.640 --> 03:15:22.260
And that is not
what denature means.

03:15:22.260 --> 03:15:25.950
So proteins fold up into
3D dimensional structures,

03:15:25.950 --> 03:15:28.920
you know this of course, based
on their amino acid sequence.

03:15:28.920 --> 03:15:31.920
And there are specific
energies of those amino acids.

03:15:31.920 --> 03:15:35.280
When you heat
protein or add acid,

03:15:35.280 --> 03:15:41.560
it starts to unfold
that protein structure.

03:15:41.560 --> 03:15:43.750
That happens during
digestion anyway.

03:15:43.750 --> 03:15:45.285
So I always chuckle when--

03:15:45.285 --> 03:15:46.660
I've seen some
companies come out

03:15:46.660 --> 03:15:48.890
with "way that
you can cook with"

03:15:48.890 --> 03:15:51.100
that's not going to
destroy the amino acids.

03:15:51.100 --> 03:15:54.040
And I'm like, so you
mean regular way, right?

03:15:54.040 --> 03:15:58.540
So yeah, typically,
cooking actually

03:15:58.540 --> 03:16:01.360
makes amino acids more
bioavailable, not less.

03:16:01.360 --> 03:16:05.860
Now, I would stay away
from charring your meat

03:16:05.860 --> 03:16:09.700
because there is some
evidence that charring creates

03:16:09.700 --> 03:16:13.720
polyaromatic hydrocarbons,
which at least in animals,

03:16:13.720 --> 03:16:16.450
when they give those, they
appear to be carcinogenic.

03:16:16.450 --> 03:16:18.670
So if you do char
your meat by accident,

03:16:18.670 --> 03:16:20.848
I would just cut off
the charred portions.

03:16:20.848 --> 03:16:22.015
And then you should be fine.

03:16:22.015 --> 03:16:23.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The
char is delicious.

03:16:23.770 --> 03:16:24.760
LAYNE NORTON: [LAUGHS]

03:16:24.760 --> 03:16:26.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Not if
it's charred too much.

03:16:26.350 --> 03:16:28.642
But there is something about
a charred crust on a meat.

03:16:28.642 --> 03:16:30.850
My dad's Argentine and likes
a good charred barbecue.

03:16:30.850 --> 03:16:32.059
LAYNE NORTON: Oh, yeah, yeah.

03:16:32.059 --> 03:16:33.460
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What about--

03:16:33.460 --> 03:16:36.243
people referred to them in their
questions as "carb blockers."

03:16:36.243 --> 03:16:37.660
But I think what
they're referring

03:16:37.660 --> 03:16:41.230
to are things like berberine and
some of the glucose scavengers.

03:16:41.230 --> 03:16:43.870
And one glucose scavenger I'd
love for you to comment on

03:16:43.870 --> 03:16:47.050
is this assertion that taking
a brisk walk after a meal,

03:16:47.050 --> 03:16:48.950
or maybe even a slow
walk after a meal,

03:16:48.950 --> 03:16:53.710
some movement can help downshift
the amount of circulating

03:16:53.710 --> 03:16:55.010
glucose in some way.

03:16:55.010 --> 03:16:57.220
I've heard that.

03:16:57.220 --> 03:17:00.250
Not a lot of people, but some
are starting to pay attention

03:17:00.250 --> 03:17:02.170
to this idea of taking
things like berberine

03:17:02.170 --> 03:17:06.190
or even metformin
can scavenge glucose.

03:17:06.190 --> 03:17:08.158
I personally can't
take berberine.

03:17:08.158 --> 03:17:09.700
If I take it, I get
massive headaches

03:17:09.700 --> 03:17:12.470
unless I've ingested tons
of sugar and carbohydrates.

03:17:12.470 --> 03:17:14.020
So I just don't
mess around with it.

03:17:14.020 --> 03:17:16.210
But I know there are a
number of people out there

03:17:16.210 --> 03:17:19.480
that want to know whether or
not these glucose scavengers can

03:17:19.480 --> 03:17:20.280
be useful.

03:17:20.280 --> 03:17:22.780
LAYNE NORTON: I think that is
really majoring in the minors,

03:17:22.780 --> 03:17:23.950
if I'm being honest.

03:17:23.950 --> 03:17:25.570
As far as the carb
blockers, there's

03:17:25.570 --> 03:17:28.780
some white kidney bean extract
and those sorts of things.

03:17:28.780 --> 03:17:33.730
They do block the digestion
of carbohydrates, some.

03:17:33.730 --> 03:17:39.250
So when I say "block,"
those watching or listening,

03:17:39.250 --> 03:17:42.470
metabolism is typically
not on and off switches.

03:17:42.470 --> 03:17:45.190
So when we say things like
"block" or "attenuate"

03:17:45.190 --> 03:17:48.633
or "inhibit,"
typically we're not

03:17:48.633 --> 03:17:51.050
talking about just a switch
on the wall that you press it,

03:17:51.050 --> 03:17:52.130
and everything turns off.

03:17:52.130 --> 03:17:54.530
We're talking about
a dimmer switch, OK?

03:17:54.530 --> 03:17:57.590
So it just changes the emphasis.

03:17:57.590 --> 03:18:00.410
But these carb
blockers can reduce

03:18:00.410 --> 03:18:01.790
the absorption of carbohydrate.

03:18:01.790 --> 03:18:06.502
Now, they don't seem to cause
weight loss when you just

03:18:06.502 --> 03:18:07.460
do it in a normal diet.

03:18:07.460 --> 03:18:08.420
Now, why is that?

03:18:08.420 --> 03:18:12.650
Well, all it does is once
those carbohydrates get

03:18:12.650 --> 03:18:16.370
to the large intestine and your
bacteria get a hold of them,

03:18:16.370 --> 03:18:19.168
they start fermenting them to
volatile fatty acids, which

03:18:19.168 --> 03:18:20.460
get reabsorbed into your liver.

03:18:20.460 --> 03:18:22.040
So you don't get the
increase in blood glucose.

03:18:22.040 --> 03:18:24.470
But you still get almost
all the calories from it.

03:18:24.470 --> 03:18:25.760
It's just in a different form.

03:18:25.760 --> 03:18:27.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I see.

03:18:27.140 --> 03:18:30.200
LAYNE NORTON: So carb blockers,
if they actually worked really

03:18:30.200 --> 03:18:33.680
well, I mean, if you block
something from being absorbed,

03:18:33.680 --> 03:18:37.220
your GI typically does not just
let undigested material sit

03:18:37.220 --> 03:18:37.850
in there.

03:18:37.850 --> 03:18:39.230
You get diarrhea.

03:18:39.230 --> 03:18:41.270
I mean, that would
be the outcome.

03:18:41.270 --> 03:18:44.420
It's also how I debunked the
whole 30 grams of protein

03:18:44.420 --> 03:18:46.820
at a meal, you can't
absorb any more than that.

03:18:46.820 --> 03:18:49.850
I'm like, if that was the
case, when you ate a steak,

03:18:49.850 --> 03:18:52.220
you would just start
having diarrhea

03:18:52.220 --> 03:18:54.710
every time you went over that
30-gram threshold, right?

03:18:54.710 --> 03:18:55.940
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
remember during college--

03:18:55.940 --> 03:18:57.290
so this would be early '90s--

03:18:57.290 --> 03:18:58.570
there was the Olestra craze.

03:18:58.570 --> 03:18:59.150
LAYNE NORTON: Oh, yeah.

03:18:59.150 --> 03:19:00.692
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
This idea of putting

03:19:00.692 --> 03:19:05.030
in a nondigestible thing
into things like potato chips

03:19:05.030 --> 03:19:09.320
so that it would clear
through the GI tract

03:19:09.320 --> 03:19:12.440
faster, not absorb
as many calories.

03:19:12.440 --> 03:19:14.533
This went nowhere, obviously.

03:19:14.533 --> 03:19:15.950
You don't hear
about this anymore.

03:19:15.950 --> 03:19:17.658
But it does raise an
interesting question

03:19:17.658 --> 03:19:22.280
related to energy balance,
which is gastric emptying time.

03:19:22.280 --> 03:19:25.940
And obviously, in the
landscape of eating disorders,

03:19:25.940 --> 03:19:29.120
in particular anorexia.

03:19:29.120 --> 03:19:31.730
Use and abuse of
laxatives is a way

03:19:31.730 --> 03:19:34.070
in which people will,
in an unhealthy way,

03:19:34.070 --> 03:19:35.600
try and control their weight.

03:19:35.600 --> 03:19:37.865
And there's a lot of
problems with that approach.

03:19:37.865 --> 03:19:38.823
LAYNE NORTON: Oh, yeah.

03:19:38.823 --> 03:19:41.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But what
about gastric emptying time?

03:19:41.120 --> 03:19:46.640
Is this one way that people
could control their energy

03:19:46.640 --> 03:19:48.200
balance in a healthy way?

03:19:48.200 --> 03:19:51.770
And where does fiber
come into play?

03:19:51.770 --> 03:19:54.020
LAYNE NORTON: Fiber tends
to improve GI transit

03:19:54.020 --> 03:19:55.160
time because it adds bulk.

03:19:55.160 --> 03:20:01.490
So your GI system
is basically a tube.

03:20:01.490 --> 03:20:04.700
And it has peristalsis, which
is wavelike contractions that

03:20:04.700 --> 03:20:06.315
moves the food down
through the tube.

03:20:06.315 --> 03:20:08.690
Well, if you have more bulk
to the food, like with fiber,

03:20:08.690 --> 03:20:10.523
you can move it through
a little bit better.

03:20:10.523 --> 03:20:14.900
Now, in the gastric, the
stomach specifically, fiber

03:20:14.900 --> 03:20:19.280
tends to delay gastric
emptying and slow it a bit,

03:20:19.280 --> 03:20:22.790
probably because it
congeals a little bit.

03:20:22.790 --> 03:20:27.550
Now, this gets into the
glycemic index argument, right?

03:20:27.550 --> 03:20:30.850
If you do low GI
foods, you'll have

03:20:30.850 --> 03:20:33.400
a slower release of glucose.

03:20:33.400 --> 03:20:35.950
It's a slower gastric
emptying time.

03:20:35.950 --> 03:20:37.990
Does that affect energy balance?

03:20:37.990 --> 03:20:39.490
And so there are
quite a few studies

03:20:39.490 --> 03:20:42.998
looking at low GI
versus high GI foods.

03:20:42.998 --> 03:20:45.040
In the studies where they
don't control calories,

03:20:45.040 --> 03:20:47.620
low GI tends to
outperform high GI.

03:20:47.620 --> 03:20:50.660
But when they control calories,
there's no difference.

03:20:50.660 --> 03:20:53.140
And so what I
think that suggests

03:20:53.140 --> 03:20:55.660
is low GI foods,
just by their nature,

03:20:55.660 --> 03:20:57.250
tend to be higher in fiber.

03:20:57.250 --> 03:20:59.950
And so I think it just kind of
comes back to the fiber issue.

03:20:59.950 --> 03:21:01.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Got it.

03:21:01.130 --> 03:21:03.820
I'd like to ask you about
supplements for a moment.

03:21:03.820 --> 03:21:05.260
It's an enormous landscape.

03:21:05.260 --> 03:21:09.130
But I believe there are a few
things that you believe in,

03:21:09.130 --> 03:21:10.540
meaning they exist.

03:21:10.540 --> 03:21:13.300
And there are decent data
to support their use.

03:21:13.300 --> 03:21:16.780
Maybe even some anecdotal data
based on your own experience,

03:21:16.780 --> 03:21:19.570
as long as we highlight it as
such, it could be interesting.

03:21:19.570 --> 03:21:22.060
I've heard you talk about
two in particular, one

03:21:22.060 --> 03:21:25.030
that I'm very familiar with,
which is creatine monohydrate.

03:21:25.030 --> 03:21:27.460
If you could share
your thoughts on that,

03:21:27.460 --> 03:21:30.310
not just for muscle building
but maybe any other purposes

03:21:30.310 --> 03:21:31.210
for it.

03:21:31.210 --> 03:21:33.370
And then the other one
is one that frankly I'm

03:21:33.370 --> 03:21:34.940
learning more about
all the time now,

03:21:34.940 --> 03:21:38.488
thanks to your prompt,
which is Rhodiola rosea--

03:21:38.488 --> 03:21:40.030
I think I pronounced
that correctly--

03:21:40.030 --> 03:21:44.140
and why that might
be interesting

03:21:44.140 --> 03:21:45.280
or of use to people.

03:21:45.280 --> 03:21:49.240
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah, so
touching on creatine,

03:21:49.240 --> 03:21:53.750
it is the most tested,
safe, and effective support

03:21:53.750 --> 03:21:54.670
supplement we have.

03:21:54.670 --> 03:21:58.210
I mean, there are thousands of
studies on creatine monohydrate

03:21:58.210 --> 03:21:58.900
now.

03:21:58.900 --> 03:22:02.350
And I would say
very clearly, too,

03:22:02.350 --> 03:22:04.180
if you're using any
other form of creatine,

03:22:04.180 --> 03:22:06.160
I think you're
wasting your money.

03:22:06.160 --> 03:22:10.480
Creatine hydrochloride
has some hype around it.

03:22:10.480 --> 03:22:12.550
Apparently, it's a
little more soluble.

03:22:12.550 --> 03:22:14.658
The claim is that you need less.

03:22:14.658 --> 03:22:16.450
But there's only a
couple of studies on it.

03:22:16.450 --> 03:22:17.802
And it's more expensive.

03:22:17.802 --> 03:22:19.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
creatine monohydrate

03:22:19.510 --> 03:22:20.873
is not particularly expensive.

03:22:20.873 --> 03:22:22.540
I realize people have
different budgets.

03:22:22.540 --> 03:22:25.405
But it's not a budget breaker.

03:22:25.405 --> 03:22:28.030
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah, it's gotten
more expensive because of COVID

03:22:28.030 --> 03:22:30.048
and supply chain issues.

03:22:30.048 --> 03:22:31.840
There's forms of creatine
that appear to be

03:22:31.840 --> 03:22:34.090
as good, like hydrochloride.

03:22:34.090 --> 03:22:36.050
But it's more expensive.

03:22:36.050 --> 03:22:38.200
And then things like
creatine ethyl ester

03:22:38.200 --> 03:22:41.170
has been shown to be worse
than creatine monohydrate.

03:22:41.170 --> 03:22:44.560
Buffered creatine
is as good or worse.

03:22:44.560 --> 03:22:47.030
And it's much more expensive.

03:22:47.030 --> 03:22:49.690
So I tell people, just
take creatine monohydrate.

03:22:49.690 --> 03:22:50.930
It is tried and true.

03:22:50.930 --> 03:22:53.860
It's been shown to saturate
the muscle cells 100%

03:22:53.860 --> 03:22:55.060
with phosphocreatine.

03:22:55.060 --> 03:22:56.530
And that's what you want.

03:22:56.530 --> 03:22:59.750
So creatine works through a
few different methodologies.

03:22:59.750 --> 03:23:02.020
One, through increasing
phosphocreatine content,

03:23:02.020 --> 03:23:04.730
which helps improve
exercise performance.

03:23:04.730 --> 03:23:09.370
It also appears to
improve recovery.

03:23:09.370 --> 03:23:13.600
And it increases
lean mass, a lot

03:23:13.600 --> 03:23:17.650
of which is through bringing
water into the muscle cells.

03:23:17.650 --> 03:23:20.392
But I mean, muscle
cells are mostly water.

03:23:20.392 --> 03:23:21.850
So when people say,
well, it's just

03:23:21.850 --> 03:23:26.080
water, that's what
muscle cells mostly are.

03:23:26.080 --> 03:23:30.460
And it also increases strength
and some other metrics.

03:23:30.460 --> 03:23:33.400
Now, it also has
been shown in studies

03:23:33.400 --> 03:23:35.320
that people tend to
get a decrease in body

03:23:35.320 --> 03:23:35.945
fat percentage.

03:23:35.945 --> 03:23:37.778
Now, that's probably
because they're getting

03:23:37.778 --> 03:23:38.860
an increase in lean mass.

03:23:38.860 --> 03:23:40.600
And so the relative
is a decrease

03:23:40.600 --> 03:23:41.778
in body fat percentage.

03:23:41.778 --> 03:23:44.320
But there are a few studies that
show a decrease in fat mass,

03:23:44.320 --> 03:23:44.820
as well.

03:23:44.820 --> 03:23:46.653
I don't think that
creatine is a fat burner.

03:23:46.653 --> 03:23:49.000
I think that people are
able to train harder, build

03:23:49.000 --> 03:23:49.850
more lean tissue.

03:23:49.850 --> 03:23:52.732
And so that's probably
having an effect on fat mass.

03:23:52.732 --> 03:23:54.440
Then they've actually
shown more recently

03:23:54.440 --> 03:23:56.810
some cognitive benefits
to creatine, which I find

03:23:56.810 --> 03:23:58.430
really interesting, as well.

03:23:58.430 --> 03:24:00.872
But the only knock on creatine
that anybody's been able

03:24:00.872 --> 03:24:03.080
to come up with-- because
they've debunked the kidney

03:24:03.080 --> 03:24:05.150
stuff; they've debunked
the liver study;

03:24:05.150 --> 03:24:08.990
there's no evidence that it
harms healthy kidney or liver--

03:24:08.990 --> 03:24:10.600
is hair loss.

03:24:10.600 --> 03:24:11.660
So what about hair loss?

03:24:11.660 --> 03:24:14.000
Because there was
one study in 2009

03:24:14.000 --> 03:24:18.050
that showed that
creatine increased DHT.

03:24:18.050 --> 03:24:20.030
But they didn't
really show an effect

03:24:20.030 --> 03:24:22.050
on any other sex hormone.

03:24:22.050 --> 03:24:23.420
So it's kind of strange.

03:24:23.420 --> 03:24:25.670
Like, you would think if
there was an increase in DHT,

03:24:25.670 --> 03:24:30.180
there would be something
else that changes, as well.

03:24:30.180 --> 03:24:31.550
And it's only one study.

03:24:31.550 --> 03:24:33.950
And again, didn't directly
measure hair loss.

03:24:33.950 --> 03:24:36.980
It measured DHT, which
we know is involved

03:24:36.980 --> 03:24:40.350
in the loss of the follicle.

03:24:40.350 --> 03:24:42.890
So what I would say is
that I am not convinced.

03:24:42.890 --> 03:24:44.000
It's only one study.

03:24:44.000 --> 03:24:45.755
Never been replicated
to my knowledge.

03:24:45.755 --> 03:24:47.630
And it was looking at
a mechanism rather than

03:24:47.630 --> 03:24:49.640
an outcome.

03:24:49.640 --> 03:24:51.690
So if you're somebody
who's prone to hair loss

03:24:51.690 --> 03:24:54.560
and you want to avoid creatine
because of that, I understand.

03:24:54.560 --> 03:24:57.230
But for most people, I
don't think it's something

03:24:57.230 --> 03:24:57.950
to worry about.

03:24:57.950 --> 03:25:00.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Do you
emphasize the classic loading

03:25:00.800 --> 03:25:02.960
of creatine, taking it
a bunch of times per day

03:25:02.960 --> 03:25:04.040
and then backing off?

03:25:04.040 --> 03:25:06.020
Or just taking it
consistently at the--

03:25:06.020 --> 03:25:10.610
I think 5 grams per day is the
typical dose that people take.

03:25:10.610 --> 03:25:14.000
LAYNE NORTON: So again, no
solutions, only trade-offs.

03:25:14.000 --> 03:25:14.810
You can load it.

03:25:14.810 --> 03:25:19.520
And you will saturate your
phosphocreatine stores faster,

03:25:19.520 --> 03:25:21.200
usually within a week.

03:25:21.200 --> 03:25:22.760
If you just take
5 grams per day,

03:25:22.760 --> 03:25:25.460
it'll take two,
three, four weeks.

03:25:25.460 --> 03:25:27.620
But you will get
to the same place.

03:25:27.620 --> 03:25:29.720
And you're probably
going to have

03:25:29.720 --> 03:25:33.830
a much lower risk of GI issues.

03:25:33.830 --> 03:25:36.320
Creatine can be a gut irritant.

03:25:36.320 --> 03:25:38.810
If it is for some folks, I
would recommend splitting it

03:25:38.810 --> 03:25:42.260
into multiple doses, so
maybe multiple 1- or 2-gram

03:25:42.260 --> 03:25:44.472
doses per day.

03:25:44.472 --> 03:25:46.430
And definitely don't load
it if you're somebody

03:25:46.430 --> 03:25:48.950
who has GI issues from it.

03:25:48.950 --> 03:25:52.840
As far as Rhodiola
rosea, the research

03:25:52.840 --> 03:25:53.840
is still in its infancy.

03:25:53.840 --> 03:25:56.000
I was just reading a
new systematic review

03:25:56.000 --> 03:25:58.790
that concluded that we need
more high-quality research.

03:25:58.790 --> 03:26:01.340
But the research
that is out there

03:26:01.340 --> 03:26:03.890
seems to suggest
that not only does it

03:26:03.890 --> 03:26:05.750
reduce physical fatigue.

03:26:05.750 --> 03:26:09.140
But also reduces the
perception of fatigue

03:26:09.140 --> 03:26:13.820
and may also enhance memory
and cognition, as well.

03:26:13.820 --> 03:26:16.220
And it's referred
to as an adaptogen.

03:26:16.220 --> 03:26:18.620
So I really like it.

03:26:18.620 --> 03:26:21.290
My anecdotal
experience is when I

03:26:21.290 --> 03:26:25.280
combine that with caffeine,
it tends to smooth out

03:26:25.280 --> 03:26:26.660
the effects of caffeine.

03:26:26.660 --> 03:26:28.910
It's a more pleasant experience.

03:26:28.910 --> 03:26:32.300
And there's also some evidence
that if you're coming off

03:26:32.300 --> 03:26:36.890
caffeine, that it can
reduce the negative side

03:26:36.890 --> 03:26:39.080
effects to caffeine
withdrawal, which, by the way,

03:26:39.080 --> 03:26:41.210
I didn't really believe
in that until I actually

03:26:41.210 --> 03:26:43.220
did a cold turkey--

03:26:43.220 --> 03:26:45.980
so before a meet, I will cut
out caffeine for seven days

03:26:45.980 --> 03:26:48.170
because you can basically
reset your caffeine

03:26:48.170 --> 03:26:49.790
tolerance in seven days.

03:26:49.790 --> 03:26:52.550
And two days in, I
mean, I'm groggy.

03:26:52.550 --> 03:26:54.080
I've got the headaches.

03:26:54.080 --> 03:26:56.870
Usually, I'll get body aches
that come up because caffeine

03:26:56.870 --> 03:27:00.020
is actually a mild analgesic.

03:27:00.020 --> 03:27:02.150
And yeah, so it was
very interesting to see-

03:27:02.150 --> 03:27:04.160
but I slept like a baby,
I'll tell you that.

03:27:04.160 --> 03:27:04.400
I slept like a baby.

03:27:04.400 --> 03:27:05.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
then you took caffeine

03:27:05.210 --> 03:27:06.487
prior to your event--

03:27:06.487 --> 03:27:07.570
LAYNE NORTON: To the meet.

03:27:07.570 --> 03:27:09.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So you really
want the maximum punch from it.

03:27:09.770 --> 03:27:09.950
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

03:27:09.950 --> 03:27:10.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's why you do that.

03:27:10.430 --> 03:27:11.222
LAYNE NORTON: Yeah.

03:27:11.222 --> 03:27:14.180
And like I said,
Rhodiola tends to--

03:27:14.180 --> 03:27:16.530
it doesn't eliminate
those negative effects.

03:27:16.530 --> 03:27:18.540
But it tends to dampen
them a little bit.

03:27:18.540 --> 03:27:19.910
So I really like it.

03:27:19.910 --> 03:27:22.190
Again, would like to
see more research on it.

03:27:22.190 --> 03:27:24.500
But there's a lot
more stuff coming out.

03:27:24.500 --> 03:27:28.130
Ashwagandha is another thing
that looks pretty promising.

03:27:28.130 --> 03:27:30.350
Seems to increase
testosterone modestly.

03:27:30.350 --> 03:27:32.155
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Interesting.

03:27:32.155 --> 03:27:34.280
LAYNE NORTON: They've shown
increases in lean mass.

03:27:34.280 --> 03:27:37.430
I don't think the
increase in testosterone

03:27:37.430 --> 03:27:38.900
explains the increase
in lean mass.

03:27:38.900 --> 03:27:40.400
It's just not a big
enough increase.

03:27:40.400 --> 03:27:42.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Could it
be the decrease in cortisol?

03:27:42.350 --> 03:27:43.250
People have talked about--

03:27:43.250 --> 03:27:44.417
LAYNE NORTON: It's possible.

03:27:44.417 --> 03:27:46.790
It does decrease
stress hormones.

03:27:46.790 --> 03:27:50.348
It also has been shown
to help with sleep.

03:27:50.348 --> 03:27:52.390
But I would like to see
more research looking at,

03:27:52.390 --> 03:27:55.690
mechanistically, how it's
increasing lean mass before I

03:27:55.690 --> 03:27:58.435
say conclusively that
this is the next creatine.

03:27:58.435 --> 03:28:00.310
There's more research
that needs to come out.

03:28:00.310 --> 03:28:04.210
And then there's some other
things that have an effect.

03:28:04.210 --> 03:28:07.420
Citrulline malate, there
was a new meta analysis that

03:28:07.420 --> 03:28:09.340
showed that
citrulline malate can

03:28:09.340 --> 03:28:13.150
reduce fatigue and increase,
I think, time to fatigue.

03:28:13.150 --> 03:28:16.060
And it may actually have
some small recovery benefits,

03:28:16.060 --> 03:28:18.040
as well.

03:28:18.040 --> 03:28:20.142
Different forms of
carnitine can actually

03:28:20.142 --> 03:28:21.100
have recovery benefits.

03:28:21.100 --> 03:28:24.220
And actually-- interesting-- I
think it's carnitine tartrate

03:28:24.220 --> 03:28:25.798
actually has been shown--

03:28:25.798 --> 03:28:27.340
Volek published a
study that actually

03:28:27.340 --> 03:28:29.423
showed that it increased
androgen-receptor density

03:28:29.423 --> 03:28:30.100
in muscle cells.

03:28:30.100 --> 03:28:31.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's interesting.

03:28:31.600 --> 03:28:34.115
L-carnitine and its
other forms are pretty--

03:28:34.115 --> 03:28:35.740
I think there's good
evidence that they

03:28:35.740 --> 03:28:37.723
can improve sperm and
egg health for people

03:28:37.723 --> 03:28:38.890
who are looking to conceive.

03:28:38.890 --> 03:28:39.370
LAYNE NORTON: Oh, interesting.

03:28:39.370 --> 03:28:41.537
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, there
are a surprising number

03:28:41.537 --> 03:28:45.400
of studies on this in humans.

03:28:45.400 --> 03:28:47.350
But yeah,
androgen-receptor density--

03:28:47.350 --> 03:28:49.437
and that's from
oral L-carnitine.

03:28:49.437 --> 03:28:51.520
People are taking capsules,
not injecting directly

03:28:51.520 --> 03:28:52.570
into the muscle, yeah.

03:28:52.570 --> 03:28:54.653
LAYNE NORTON: And then
you've got things obviously

03:28:54.653 --> 03:28:57.460
like the other most effective
supplement out there

03:28:57.460 --> 03:28:58.657
is probably caffeine.

03:28:58.657 --> 03:29:00.490
I mean, if you look at
the research studies,

03:29:00.490 --> 03:29:02.530
caffeine produces
very consistently

03:29:02.530 --> 03:29:04.493
improvements in performance.

03:29:04.493 --> 03:29:05.410
So that's another one.

03:29:05.410 --> 03:29:06.910
Some people don't like
the effect of caffeine.

03:29:06.910 --> 03:29:07.390
That's OK.

03:29:07.390 --> 03:29:07.780
But--

03:29:07.780 --> 03:29:09.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I wouldn't know
because I've never come off it.

03:29:09.700 --> 03:29:10.200
[CHUCKLES]

03:29:10.200 --> 03:29:11.740
LAYNE NORTON: Exactly, exactly.

03:29:11.740 --> 03:29:13.390
Well, interestingly,
they do show

03:29:13.390 --> 03:29:16.480
that the effect appears
to be consistent,

03:29:16.480 --> 03:29:19.930
that even if you're a
habitual caffeine user,

03:29:19.930 --> 03:29:22.450
you do still get a benefit
every time you take it.

03:29:22.450 --> 03:29:26.410
But like you said,
you're just used to it.

03:29:26.410 --> 03:29:28.090
So there's those things.

03:29:28.090 --> 03:29:30.550
Then you've got things
like beta alanine,

03:29:30.550 --> 03:29:35.130
which it's in our preworkout.

03:29:35.130 --> 03:29:40.440
Probably not super helpful
for most people for resistance

03:29:40.440 --> 03:29:41.110
training.

03:29:41.110 --> 03:29:45.720
It does seem to have some
benefits for high intensity.

03:29:45.720 --> 03:29:48.960
If you get out more than
45 seconds or 60 seconds

03:29:48.960 --> 03:29:51.630
of really hard
training, it does appear

03:29:51.630 --> 03:29:55.360
to help with delaying
fatigue for that.

03:29:55.360 --> 03:29:58.230
And then you've got
things like betaine,

03:29:58.230 --> 03:30:00.840
or also called
trimethylglycine, which

03:30:00.840 --> 03:30:03.840
there's some evidence it
can improve lean mass.

03:30:03.840 --> 03:30:09.130
There's some evidence that
it can improve power output.

03:30:09.130 --> 03:30:10.770
So there's a few
things out there.

03:30:10.770 --> 03:30:13.170
But most of the stuff
is not very good.

03:30:13.170 --> 03:30:19.800
So I think that those kinds
of supplements, very useful.

03:30:19.800 --> 03:30:22.320
But again, I would never tell
people they need supplements.

03:30:22.320 --> 03:30:24.780
Again, even something
like creatine

03:30:24.780 --> 03:30:27.780
is going to be a very
small effect compared

03:30:27.780 --> 03:30:32.100
to proper nutrition,
recovery, and hard training.

03:30:32.100 --> 03:30:34.740
One of the things--

03:30:34.740 --> 03:30:36.570
I was talking with Ben
Bruno the other day.

03:30:36.570 --> 03:30:39.000
And I said, you
know, some people

03:30:39.000 --> 03:30:41.760
will ask me like, how does
this person make progress?

03:30:41.760 --> 03:30:44.550
Because their programming
is not evidence-based?

03:30:44.550 --> 03:30:47.940
Or this guy, his
exercises are dumb.

03:30:47.940 --> 03:30:50.520
And I'll say, yeah, but
they trained really hard

03:30:50.520 --> 03:30:52.350
for 20 years.

03:30:52.350 --> 03:30:55.740
One commonality you see between
really successful athletes

03:30:55.740 --> 03:30:59.320
or bodybuilders is
they train really hard.

03:30:59.320 --> 03:31:01.110
And one of the things
I have observed

03:31:01.110 --> 03:31:04.080
is the more into the
weeds people tend to get--

03:31:04.080 --> 03:31:06.900
and again, this is just my
own anecdote and observation--

03:31:06.900 --> 03:31:09.630
the more in the weeds they
tend to get, the less hard

03:31:09.630 --> 03:31:10.950
I see them train.

03:31:10.950 --> 03:31:13.140
And so one of the
things I really

03:31:13.140 --> 03:31:16.620
like that Mike Israetel
said, who's got a PhD

03:31:16.620 --> 03:31:19.620
and is a bodybuilder
himself, he said,

03:31:19.620 --> 03:31:23.190
you can't outscience
hard training,

03:31:23.190 --> 03:31:24.773
that if you're looking
to build muscle

03:31:24.773 --> 03:31:26.940
and you're looking to improve
your body composition,

03:31:26.940 --> 03:31:29.085
the main thing is just
doing the work over time.

03:31:29.085 --> 03:31:31.043
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Consistency
and the hard work.

03:31:31.043 --> 03:31:33.780
And I would add to that--
and this is true of academic

03:31:33.780 --> 03:31:37.418
endeavors, too, of course;
I hope you'll agree--

03:31:37.418 --> 03:31:38.460
LAYNE NORTON: Absolutely.

03:31:38.460 --> 03:31:41.633
ANDREW HUBERMAN: --which is
that, yeah, the other thing is,

03:31:41.633 --> 03:31:43.050
given the mental
side-- earlier we

03:31:43.050 --> 03:31:45.712
were talking about how
satiety signals in the brain

03:31:45.712 --> 03:31:47.670
and what you think about
foods can be relevant.

03:31:47.670 --> 03:31:51.840
Learning to really enjoy
training hard in addition

03:31:51.840 --> 03:31:54.070
to learning to really
enjoy eating well,

03:31:54.070 --> 03:31:58.200
not just for the effects that
it has on body composition--

03:31:58.200 --> 03:31:59.670
those, too, of course.

03:31:59.670 --> 03:32:03.000
But just learning to really
enjoy the process of training

03:32:03.000 --> 03:32:07.170
hard and a really hard
workout or a really hard paper

03:32:07.170 --> 03:32:09.270
that you have to sort
through or really digging

03:32:09.270 --> 03:32:11.070
through a book
that's challenging,

03:32:11.070 --> 03:32:14.700
learning to really enjoy that, I
think, if there is a power tool

03:32:14.700 --> 03:32:16.530
out there, it's the
psychological end.

03:32:16.530 --> 03:32:18.155
LAYNE NORTON: And I
think a lot of that

03:32:18.155 --> 03:32:22.380
is getting the confidence
of doing something hard,

03:32:22.380 --> 03:32:25.830
that there's a
payoff at the end.

03:32:25.830 --> 03:32:28.560
I get asked a lot in my Q&amp;As,
how do I get more confident?

03:32:28.560 --> 03:32:30.000
How do I become more confident?

03:32:30.000 --> 03:32:32.700
And I'll tell people,
you have to do.

03:32:32.700 --> 03:32:33.630
There's no hack.

03:32:33.630 --> 03:32:34.260
You can't read about it.

03:32:34.260 --> 03:32:35.552
You've got to get in the arena.

03:32:35.552 --> 03:32:39.450
And I don't mean like compete
in sports necessarily, but doing

03:32:39.450 --> 03:32:43.065
a PhD or doing something, just
something hard, where you're

03:32:43.065 --> 03:32:44.190
putting yourself out there.

03:32:44.190 --> 03:32:45.648
And you're saying,
this is my goal.

03:32:45.648 --> 03:32:47.350
And I'm going to go for it.

03:32:47.350 --> 03:32:51.790
You just learn so much by
doing that about yourself.

03:32:51.790 --> 03:32:56.600
And so just what you said, I
will reframe things in my mind

03:32:56.600 --> 03:32:58.850
when bad things happen from--

03:32:58.850 --> 03:33:01.250
it's not to say I never get
stressed out, because I do.

03:33:01.250 --> 03:33:03.720
And it's not to say that I
never get down, because I do,

03:33:03.720 --> 03:33:04.830
because I'm a human.

03:33:04.830 --> 03:33:06.440
But when something bad happens--

03:33:06.440 --> 03:33:08.398
I actually posted about
this in my story today.

03:33:08.398 --> 03:33:12.200
When something bad happens,
very rarely anymore do I go,

03:33:12.200 --> 03:33:12.930
woe is me.

03:33:12.930 --> 03:33:14.013
Why did this happen to me?

03:33:14.013 --> 03:33:16.340
Because you're in the universe.

03:33:16.340 --> 03:33:19.420
Random bad things
are going to happen.

03:33:19.420 --> 03:33:22.410
So instead, I say--

03:33:22.410 --> 03:33:23.850
if I'm not dead--

03:33:23.850 --> 03:33:27.480
instead, I say, well, what
an exciting opportunity

03:33:27.480 --> 03:33:29.410
to overcome an obstacle.

03:33:29.410 --> 03:33:33.330
And I bet, because in the
experience of my life,

03:33:33.330 --> 03:33:36.330
the biggest lessons and
the best things in my life

03:33:36.330 --> 03:33:38.640
have actually come out
of the most challenging,

03:33:38.640 --> 03:33:40.900
worst things that have happened.

03:33:40.900 --> 03:33:44.070
And so again, I
would never have been

03:33:44.070 --> 03:33:47.133
able to do these sorts of
things if I hadn't taken up

03:33:47.133 --> 03:33:49.050
weightlifting, because
weightlifting taught me

03:33:49.050 --> 03:33:53.280
so much about perseverance,
delayed gratification,

03:33:53.280 --> 03:33:54.990
overcoming obstacles.

03:33:54.990 --> 03:33:58.122
And that's why I love
it even to this day.

03:33:58.122 --> 03:33:59.580
And I'll still get
butterflies when

03:33:59.580 --> 03:34:01.620
I go in for a squat
session, even though I've

03:34:01.620 --> 03:34:02.790
been doing it for 23 years.

03:34:02.790 --> 03:34:03.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's wonderful.

03:34:03.990 --> 03:34:06.030
Well, it's clear that
you embrace hard things.

03:34:06.030 --> 03:34:08.190
And for people listening
to this, obviously

03:34:08.190 --> 03:34:09.732
it doesn't have to
be weightlifting--

03:34:09.732 --> 03:34:11.820
picking hard things,
learning an instrument,

03:34:11.820 --> 03:34:13.710
learning a language.

03:34:13.710 --> 03:34:16.703
Challenge is an
absolute builder.

03:34:16.703 --> 03:34:18.120
LAYNE NORTON: And
they've actually

03:34:18.120 --> 03:34:21.210
shown those sorts of things,
when you challenge yourself

03:34:21.210 --> 03:34:24.367
and also mentally, I think there
was a new study that came out

03:34:24.367 --> 03:34:26.700
basically showing a reduction
in the risk of Alzheimer's

03:34:26.700 --> 03:34:29.140
and other age-related
cognitive decline.

03:34:29.140 --> 03:34:32.070
I mean, basically, use
it or lose it, right?

03:34:32.070 --> 03:34:35.640
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, the desire

03:34:35.640 --> 03:34:40.770
and the will to persevere no
doubt translates to this thing

03:34:40.770 --> 03:34:42.870
that we call the
will to live, right?

03:34:42.870 --> 03:34:44.730
It's related to
the will to live.

03:34:44.730 --> 03:34:49.410
Well, I think that what
you just said beautifully

03:34:49.410 --> 03:34:52.500
embodies what most people are
aspiring to, which is to--

03:34:52.500 --> 03:34:55.720
I think most people actually
want to do hard things.

03:34:55.720 --> 03:34:58.320
They don't just want
to have the results.

03:34:58.320 --> 03:35:00.270
I think that most
people deep down

03:35:00.270 --> 03:35:02.940
have some understanding that
their reward system works

03:35:02.940 --> 03:35:03.670
that way.

03:35:03.670 --> 03:35:05.520
I must say, this
conversation for me

03:35:05.520 --> 03:35:06.870
has been tremendously rewarding.

03:35:06.870 --> 03:35:08.495
First of all, it
allowed me to meet you

03:35:08.495 --> 03:35:11.460
in person for the first time,
which I've really enjoyed.

03:35:11.460 --> 03:35:14.010
Certainly, this won't
be our last interaction

03:35:14.010 --> 03:35:16.620
on this podcast and elsewhere.

03:35:16.620 --> 03:35:20.460
Also, the amount of knowledge
that you contain inside you

03:35:20.460 --> 03:35:21.850
is astonishing.

03:35:21.850 --> 03:35:22.350
And--

03:35:22.350 --> 03:35:23.940
LAYNE NORTON: There's a lot of
stuff rattling around up there.

03:35:23.940 --> 03:35:24.195
[CHUCKLES]

03:35:24.195 --> 03:35:25.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And we all benefit

03:35:25.410 --> 03:35:27.840
because your ability to pull
from the mechanistic side,

03:35:27.840 --> 03:35:30.960
again I think not
limited to but related

03:35:30.960 --> 03:35:33.090
to your background in
biochemistry, all the way

03:35:33.090 --> 03:35:36.868
through to the impact in
humans, animal studies,

03:35:36.868 --> 03:35:38.910
being able to understand
where those sit relative

03:35:38.910 --> 03:35:39.600
to one another.

03:35:39.600 --> 03:35:42.090
And then you're
obviously a practitioner.

03:35:42.090 --> 03:35:43.530
You practice what you preach.

03:35:43.530 --> 03:35:47.940
And what you talk about
pertains to men, to women,

03:35:47.940 --> 03:35:51.870
younger people, older
people, people who are vegan,

03:35:51.870 --> 03:35:53.130
Keto, carnivore.

03:35:53.130 --> 03:35:55.890
You really are able to net
a tremendous number of ideas

03:35:55.890 --> 03:35:58.750
while staying really
nuanced and data driven.

03:35:58.750 --> 03:36:02.370
And so I just want to say
for myself and on behalf

03:36:02.370 --> 03:36:04.050
of the listeners,
really appreciate

03:36:04.050 --> 03:36:06.840
you coming in here today and
sharing with us your knowledge.

03:36:06.840 --> 03:36:10.840
We will absolutely point people
in the direction of where

03:36:10.840 --> 03:36:12.090
they can learn more about you.

03:36:12.090 --> 03:36:15.330
And one of the places
that I definitely

03:36:15.330 --> 03:36:20.160
want to mention before we part,
however, is this Carbon app.

03:36:20.160 --> 03:36:22.590
And I should just mention
this isn't a paid promotion

03:36:22.590 --> 03:36:23.632
or anything of that sort.

03:36:23.632 --> 03:36:25.410
Actually, one of our
podcast team members

03:36:25.410 --> 03:36:27.450
has been using Carbon
for a long time.

03:36:27.450 --> 03:36:29.610
This is an app that
you devised which

03:36:29.610 --> 03:36:32.430
allows people to navigate the
exercise, nutrition, and energy

03:36:32.430 --> 03:36:35.820
balance space for weight loss,
muscle gain, fat loss, weight

03:36:35.820 --> 03:36:36.720
maintenance.

03:36:36.720 --> 03:36:38.910
I would just like to
briefly ask you about that

03:36:38.910 --> 03:36:41.510
before we conclude.

03:36:41.510 --> 03:36:43.423
Without necessarily
telling us everything

03:36:43.423 --> 03:36:44.840
that's in the
Carbon app, I'd love

03:36:44.840 --> 03:36:46.590
to know, what are the
major things that it

03:36:46.590 --> 03:36:47.720
does and is good for?

03:36:47.720 --> 03:36:50.330
And then, what were
some of the key things

03:36:50.330 --> 03:36:52.400
that you wanted to
make sure were in there

03:36:52.400 --> 03:36:53.650
when you built it?

03:36:53.650 --> 03:36:55.670
What's the logical
backbone behind it?

03:36:55.670 --> 03:36:57.560
Because I think there
are a lot of food

03:36:57.560 --> 03:37:01.070
counting, calorie counting,
exercise apps out there.

03:37:01.070 --> 03:37:02.540
Everyone I've
talked to that uses

03:37:02.540 --> 03:37:05.630
Carbon, including
our mutual friend,

03:37:05.630 --> 03:37:08.060
Saagar Enjeti, this member
of my podcast, et cetera,

03:37:08.060 --> 03:37:09.230
raves about it.

03:37:09.230 --> 03:37:10.790
So what is Carbon?

03:37:10.790 --> 03:37:12.980
And what does it do?

03:37:12.980 --> 03:37:15.260
And what was your
mindset in building it?

03:37:15.260 --> 03:37:17.870
What did you really
want to see there

03:37:17.870 --> 03:37:19.463
that you didn't see elsewhere?

03:37:19.463 --> 03:37:21.380
LAYNE NORTON: So those
listening may not know,

03:37:21.380 --> 03:37:24.620
but I started online
coaching people for nutrition

03:37:24.620 --> 03:37:26.270
back in 2005.

03:37:26.270 --> 03:37:29.780
And that was the vast
majority of my business

03:37:29.780 --> 03:37:32.450
all the way up until 2017.

03:37:32.450 --> 03:37:34.580
And I had a lot of
success with that,

03:37:34.580 --> 03:37:38.270
whether it be just average
folks looking to lose weight

03:37:38.270 --> 03:37:41.480
or build muscle and
right up to elite level

03:37:41.480 --> 03:37:43.970
competitors in physique sport.

03:37:43.970 --> 03:37:48.382
So I had this idea, like--

03:37:48.382 --> 03:37:49.840
I don't want to
say I had the idea.

03:37:49.840 --> 03:37:52.140
A few people had the idea.

03:37:52.140 --> 03:37:56.370
What if we could take
what I do in coaching

03:37:56.370 --> 03:37:59.460
and try to automate as
much of that as possible?

03:37:59.460 --> 03:38:02.400
Because by the time I was
becoming a really popular

03:38:02.400 --> 03:38:04.170
coach, I mean, I was expensive.

03:38:04.170 --> 03:38:05.933
You were looking
at me charging--

03:38:05.933 --> 03:38:08.100
I got to the point where I
was charging about $1,000

03:38:08.100 --> 03:38:09.300
a month for coaching.

03:38:09.300 --> 03:38:12.450
And most people
cannot afford that.

03:38:12.450 --> 03:38:16.110
And I would like to not
just coach rich people.

03:38:16.110 --> 03:38:17.590
[CHUCKLES] You know what I mean?

03:38:17.590 --> 03:38:20.410
I would like to be able
to help other people.

03:38:20.410 --> 03:38:23.043
So the idea was to
create an app that

03:38:23.043 --> 03:38:24.210
could do some of this stuff.

03:38:24.210 --> 03:38:27.460
Now, there's always a place
for human interaction.

03:38:27.460 --> 03:38:29.550
But for people who
can't afford that,

03:38:29.550 --> 03:38:32.730
our app is basically
$10 a month.

03:38:32.730 --> 03:38:36.060
And basically, what we wanted
to do was set up an app where--

03:38:38.640 --> 03:38:41.400
think about if you went
to a nutrition coach.

03:38:41.400 --> 03:38:42.790
What would they do?

03:38:42.790 --> 03:38:45.900
They would probably ask you
some questions about your goals,

03:38:45.900 --> 03:38:51.263
take some anthropometrics,
maybe dietary preference.

03:38:51.263 --> 03:38:53.430
And they'd use that information
to kind of formulate

03:38:53.430 --> 03:38:55.170
a baseline plan.

03:38:55.170 --> 03:38:57.180
That's what Carbon does.

03:38:57.180 --> 03:38:59.610
So I think there's eight
questions in the signup

03:38:59.610 --> 03:39:05.590
flow about your activity,
your exercise, your lifestyle,

03:39:05.590 --> 03:39:08.110
your body weight, your
body fat percentage.

03:39:08.110 --> 03:39:10.270
And if you don't know it,
we help you calculate it.

03:39:10.270 --> 03:39:10.990
It's not perfect.

03:39:10.990 --> 03:39:13.060
But it's better than nothing.

03:39:13.060 --> 03:39:14.790
And then your
dietary preferences.

03:39:14.790 --> 03:39:17.880
And we use that to come
up with your baseline.

03:39:17.880 --> 03:39:20.410
And your baseline will be
your calories, your protein,

03:39:20.410 --> 03:39:21.990
your carbohydrates, and fats.

03:39:21.990 --> 03:39:24.480
And what's different
about our app--

03:39:24.480 --> 03:39:27.150
because apps like
MyFitnessPal or whatever

03:39:27.150 --> 03:39:28.800
will do that, as well.

03:39:28.800 --> 03:39:31.530
What's different
about ours is we

03:39:31.530 --> 03:39:34.020
encourage people to log their
weight daily for the reasons

03:39:34.020 --> 03:39:36.028
that we talked about earlier.

03:39:36.028 --> 03:39:38.070
And then you can also
track your food in the app.

03:39:38.070 --> 03:39:41.580
And honestly, I think our food
tracker is actually way easier

03:39:41.580 --> 03:39:43.920
to use than most
of them out there.

03:39:43.920 --> 03:39:46.050
What we typically get
great rave reviews

03:39:46.050 --> 03:39:49.020
about is how user-friendly
our interface is,

03:39:49.020 --> 03:39:51.830
that it makes intuitive sense.

03:39:51.830 --> 03:39:55.660
And so you track your food,
try to hit these macros

03:39:55.660 --> 03:39:57.390
that you're prescribed.

03:39:57.390 --> 03:40:00.990
And each week, you
will be prompted

03:40:00.990 --> 03:40:03.300
to check in with a coach
on your check-in day.

03:40:03.300 --> 03:40:05.790
And then you put in
some information.

03:40:05.790 --> 03:40:08.220
And then based on how
you're progressing,

03:40:08.220 --> 03:40:12.627
the app will adjust
or not adjust based

03:40:12.627 --> 03:40:13.710
on how you're progressing.

03:40:13.710 --> 03:40:16.920
So for example, if you're
hitting a weight-loss plateau,

03:40:16.920 --> 03:40:18.240
it will sense that.

03:40:18.240 --> 03:40:20.280
And it will reduce
your calories.

03:40:20.280 --> 03:40:22.290
Or if you're trying
to gain weight

03:40:22.290 --> 03:40:25.470
and you've hit a plateau,
it'll increase your calories.

03:40:25.470 --> 03:40:27.870
And there's a lot of
back-end algorithm

03:40:27.870 --> 03:40:29.232
stuff that takes care of this.

03:40:29.232 --> 03:40:30.690
But the fundamental
crux of the app

03:40:30.690 --> 03:40:33.998
is we try to determine
your total daily energy

03:40:33.998 --> 03:40:35.790
expenditure because
that's going to tell us

03:40:35.790 --> 03:40:37.290
the first big thing
we need to know,

03:40:37.290 --> 03:40:40.050
which is, how many calories
do you need to be eating

03:40:40.050 --> 03:40:41.760
for your goal, right?

03:40:41.760 --> 03:40:48.100
So on the front
end, we basically

03:40:48.100 --> 03:40:50.192
do our best guess based
on your anthropometrics.

03:40:50.192 --> 03:40:51.400
It's not going to be perfect.

03:40:51.400 --> 03:40:53.210
But it'll get us
in the ballpark.

03:40:53.210 --> 03:40:55.712
And if you do know, like
some people already know,

03:40:55.712 --> 03:40:57.670
well, I know what I
maintain my body weight on,

03:40:57.670 --> 03:40:59.890
there's actually a spot where
you can manually enter that

03:40:59.890 --> 03:41:00.910
during the signup flow.

03:41:00.910 --> 03:41:04.780
So that's helpful for people
who are super nerds like me.

03:41:04.780 --> 03:41:09.952
But then people will ask, well,
do you take Apple Watch data?

03:41:09.952 --> 03:41:10.660
Do you take this?

03:41:10.660 --> 03:41:11.368
Do you take that?

03:41:11.368 --> 03:41:13.090
And no, for the reasons
we talked about,

03:41:13.090 --> 03:41:14.920
that it overestimates
energy expenditure.

03:41:14.920 --> 03:41:19.000
What our app does is
it's an algebra equation.

03:41:19.000 --> 03:41:21.850
If you-- because
your body weight,

03:41:21.850 --> 03:41:25.120
your maintenance calories
is your total daily energy

03:41:25.120 --> 03:41:25.660
expenditure.

03:41:25.660 --> 03:41:28.480
Your average calories that
you eat to maintain your body

03:41:28.480 --> 03:41:32.410
weight will be the same as your
total daily energy expenditure.

03:41:32.410 --> 03:41:37.110
So if we know how body
weight is changing

03:41:37.110 --> 03:41:39.820
and we know how many calories
the person is consuming,

03:41:39.820 --> 03:41:42.870
we can actually solve for
what energy expenditure is.

03:41:42.870 --> 03:41:45.090
And you can see in
the app that we'll--

03:41:45.090 --> 03:41:48.210
there's a maintenance calorie
tracker, or energy expenditure

03:41:48.210 --> 03:41:48.900
tracker.

03:41:48.900 --> 03:41:51.970
And typically, after
about three to four weeks,

03:41:51.970 --> 03:41:54.300
even if the app
was off at first,

03:41:54.300 --> 03:41:58.320
it will have you pretty darn
close because, let's say

03:41:58.320 --> 03:42:00.420
somebody comes on.

03:42:00.420 --> 03:42:03.780
And their goal is to
lose a pound and a half

03:42:03.780 --> 03:42:05.260
a week or something like that.

03:42:05.260 --> 03:42:08.430
And the first week,
they lose 3 pounds.

03:42:08.430 --> 03:42:10.415
Now, the app actually
accounts for the fact

03:42:10.415 --> 03:42:12.540
that you can lose more
water weight the first week.

03:42:12.540 --> 03:42:14.490
So they probably wouldn't
get an adjustment.

03:42:14.490 --> 03:42:17.130
But let's say the next
week, they lose 3 pounds.

03:42:17.130 --> 03:42:21.420
The app will sense that and
adjust their calories up

03:42:21.420 --> 03:42:24.750
because it will be estimating
that their energy expenditures

03:42:24.750 --> 03:42:27.570
are actually higher
than what it previously

03:42:27.570 --> 03:42:29.892
estimated based on the amount
of weight they're losing.

03:42:29.892 --> 03:42:31.350
And the same thing
goes in reverse.

03:42:31.350 --> 03:42:33.100
If they're not losing
the amount of weight

03:42:33.100 --> 03:42:35.700
that they're supposed
to, it will lower them

03:42:35.700 --> 03:42:38.280
based on the fact that it may
have overestimated their energy

03:42:38.280 --> 03:42:39.120
expenditure.

03:42:39.120 --> 03:42:43.710
But that's the first crux of
it, is tracking that energy

03:42:43.710 --> 03:42:44.640
expenditure.

03:42:44.640 --> 03:42:48.280
And then the next
thing is protein.

03:42:48.280 --> 03:42:52.110
So when the back-end
algorithm stuff is happening,

03:42:52.110 --> 03:42:54.300
calories are set first
based on your energy

03:42:54.300 --> 03:42:55.560
expenditure and your goal.

03:42:55.560 --> 03:42:59.550
So for example, if you're
on an aggressive diet,

03:42:59.550 --> 03:43:02.280
your calories are going to
be lower even if your energy

03:43:02.280 --> 03:43:04.405
expenditure might be a
little bit high, just

03:43:04.405 --> 03:43:06.780
because if you're trying to
lose 2 pounds a week, I mean,

03:43:06.780 --> 03:43:09.700
you're going to be in a pretty
aggressive calorie deficit.

03:43:09.700 --> 03:43:11.440
So it's going to set
the calories first.

03:43:11.440 --> 03:43:15.350
Then, it will set protein
based on your lean body mass.

03:43:15.350 --> 03:43:17.680
Then, the calories
that are left over

03:43:17.680 --> 03:43:19.690
will be allotted to
carbohydrate and fat

03:43:19.690 --> 03:43:21.340
depending on your
dietary preference.

03:43:21.340 --> 03:43:23.340
And we have a few different
dietary preferences.

03:43:23.340 --> 03:43:29.260
There's balanced, which is about
50/50 to 60/40 carbohydrate

03:43:29.260 --> 03:43:31.330
to fat of the
remaining calories.

03:43:31.330 --> 03:43:34.630
Then you have low fat,
which is obviously a higher

03:43:34.630 --> 03:43:35.890
ratio of carbohydrates.

03:43:35.890 --> 03:43:37.960
You have low carb.

03:43:37.960 --> 03:43:40.750
You have a ketogenic diet,
which is very, very low carb.

03:43:40.750 --> 03:43:43.360
And then there's also
a plant-based option.

03:43:43.360 --> 03:43:46.120
And within each
of those options,

03:43:46.120 --> 03:43:48.970
still, you can go in and
actually shimmy the macros

03:43:48.970 --> 03:43:50.930
a little bit within
a certain range

03:43:50.930 --> 03:43:54.292
so that you can kind of dial
in what your specific dietary

03:43:54.292 --> 03:43:55.750
preference is
because, again, if we

03:43:55.750 --> 03:43:59.080
go back to what is going
to produce the best

03:43:59.080 --> 03:44:02.300
long-term results, it's whatever
the person can adhere to.

03:44:02.300 --> 03:44:06.640
So we really try to start
with the concept of adherence

03:44:06.640 --> 03:44:09.700
by allowing people to have the
dietary preference that they

03:44:09.700 --> 03:44:10.690
want.

03:44:10.690 --> 03:44:13.175
And there's some other apps
out there that are good apps.

03:44:13.175 --> 03:44:14.800
For example, we get
asked a lot, what's

03:44:14.800 --> 03:44:16.660
the difference between our
app and the Renaissance

03:44:16.660 --> 03:44:17.620
Periodization app?

03:44:17.620 --> 03:44:19.420
And they have a great app.

03:44:19.420 --> 03:44:20.925
But theirs is kind
of more rigid.

03:44:20.925 --> 03:44:23.050
And it'll say, you're going
to eat this many meals.

03:44:23.050 --> 03:44:25.420
And you're going to have
these foods at these times.

03:44:25.420 --> 03:44:26.750
So we're kind of the opposite.

03:44:26.750 --> 03:44:28.630
We want to give you
maximum flexibility.

03:44:28.630 --> 03:44:31.390
Now, for some people, they
would prefer the rigid structure

03:44:31.390 --> 03:44:32.680
at first.

03:44:32.680 --> 03:44:34.910
But we find that
for most people,

03:44:34.910 --> 03:44:37.990
giving them more flexibility
typically improves

03:44:37.990 --> 03:44:40.070
adherence over the long run.

03:44:40.070 --> 03:44:41.950
So that's kind of
how the app works.

03:44:41.950 --> 03:44:44.247
And again, there's
multiple different goals.

03:44:44.247 --> 03:44:45.580
It's not just a weight-loss app.

03:44:45.580 --> 03:44:46.910
There's a maintenance.

03:44:46.910 --> 03:44:49.190
There's muscle-building.

03:44:49.190 --> 03:44:51.340
So you've got all kinds
of different goals that

03:44:51.340 --> 03:44:54.740
can be accommodated, different
rates of each of those goals.

03:44:54.740 --> 03:44:58.790
And I mean, I've used the
app for over three years

03:44:58.790 --> 03:45:00.710
now to do my body weight.

03:45:00.710 --> 03:45:03.740
And I mean, when I say
that it's dialed me

03:45:03.740 --> 03:45:08.300
in-- because I'm very regimented
with logging and logging

03:45:08.300 --> 03:45:09.230
my weight.

03:45:09.230 --> 03:45:12.770
So what I targeted to
weigh in at Worlds,

03:45:12.770 --> 03:45:16.183
I got down to the 0.1 kilogram.

03:45:16.183 --> 03:45:17.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's fantastic.

03:45:17.600 --> 03:45:19.308
LAYNE NORTON: So it
was pretty cool to be

03:45:19.308 --> 03:45:24.590
able to use a tool that I helped
develop to actually coach me.

03:45:24.590 --> 03:45:26.660
So it's a great tool.

03:45:26.660 --> 03:45:28.640
We did some statistics.

03:45:28.640 --> 03:45:30.560
We polled 2,500 members.

03:45:30.560 --> 03:45:32.990
And one of the
questions we asked is,

03:45:32.990 --> 03:45:34.580
would you recommend
this to a friend?

03:45:34.580 --> 03:45:36.710
And 91% said yes.

03:45:36.710 --> 03:45:40.100
So I think our average
retention is, like,

03:45:40.100 --> 03:45:42.500
seven months, which for an
app that costs $10 a month

03:45:42.500 --> 03:45:43.167
is really great.

03:45:43.167 --> 03:45:44.417
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's great.

03:45:44.417 --> 03:45:47.010
Yeah, as I mentioned, a number
of people I know use it.

03:45:47.010 --> 03:45:49.430
This is not a paid promotion.

03:45:49.430 --> 03:45:52.130
But I think people need
guidance and tools.

03:45:52.130 --> 03:45:55.340
And what we know about the
human brain is that winging it

03:45:55.340 --> 03:45:59.642
can work, but that the brain
will cheat itself often.

03:45:59.642 --> 03:46:01.100
There's a Feynman
quote about this.

03:46:01.100 --> 03:46:02.017
And I'll get it wrong.

03:46:02.017 --> 03:46:04.820
It's always bad to try
and quote Feynman anyway

03:46:04.820 --> 03:46:06.240
because he said
it so much better.

03:46:06.240 --> 03:46:08.140
But that we are the easiest--

03:46:08.140 --> 03:46:09.723
it's easy to fool
ourselves basically,

03:46:09.723 --> 03:46:11.807
is what he was saying--
easiest to fool ourselves.

03:46:11.807 --> 03:46:13.160
LAYNE NORTON: Absolutely.

03:46:13.160 --> 03:46:14.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sounds great.

03:46:14.410 --> 03:46:17.480
